[{"qid":1,"stem":"What is the cella of a Greek temple called?","choices":["Pronaos","Opisthodomos","Peristasis","Peristalsis","Naos"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The naos, or cella, is the main enclosed chamber of the temple. The pronaos is the porch in front of it.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":2,"stem":"What is the wedge-shaped stone at the apex of an arch called?","choices":["Pier","Impost","Metope","Acroterion","Keystone"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The keystone is the central stone at the crown of an arch; the impost is where the arch springs from its support.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":3,"stem":"What is the sculpture shown, and who made it?","choices":["Hebe by Antonio Canova","Naiad by Antonio Canova","Truth Unveiled by Time by Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Pauline Borghese by Antonio Canova","Medici Venus"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Canova portrayed Pauline Borghese reclining as Venus Victorious.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":4,"stem":"Which architectural style does the illustration represent?","choices":["Romanesque","Neoclassical","Byzantine","Baroque","Gothic"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The pointed arch and clustered supports are characteristic Gothic features.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":5,"stem":"What is a plan called when its longitudinal arm is longer than its transverse arm?","choices":["Greek cross","Tau cross","St Andrew's cross","Latin cross","Cross of Lorraine"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A Latin cross has a longer longitudinal arm. A Greek cross has arms of equal length.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":6,"stem":"Which architect is associated with the Tempio Malatestiano?","choices":["Francesco di Giorgio Martini","Giuliano da Sangallo","Leon Battista Alberti","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Giuseppe Piermarini"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Alberti designed the Renaissance exterior of the Tempio Malatestiano in Rimini.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":7,"stem":"The Christian basilica derives from earlier models that were:","choices":["Greek","Jewish","Roman","Hellenistic","Etruscan"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The church basilica adopted the longitudinal hall form of the Roman civic basilica.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":8,"stem":"Who founded the Bauhaus?","choices":["Le Corbusier","Walter Gropius","Marcel Breuer","Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Eugène Viollet-le-Duc"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Walter Gropius founded the Bauhaus in Weimar in 1919. Mies van der Rohe later directed it.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":9,"stem":"Who designed the building housing the Guggenheim Museum in New York?","choices":["Le Corbusier","Frank Lloyd Wright","Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Gerrit Rietveld","Renzo Piano"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Frank Lloyd Wright designed its distinctive spiral-ramp building.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":10,"stem":"The plan below is that of a church in which style?","choices":["Baroque","Gothic","Early Christian","Romanesque","Renaissance"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The repeated rib-vaulted bays and the developed east end identify this Gothic church plan.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":11,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order of these architects?\na) Borromini\nb) Vanvitelli\nc) Bramante\nd) Brunelleschi\ne) Palladio","choices":["c; d; e; a; b","d; e; a; b; c","d; c; a; e; b","d; c; e; a; b","a; b; c; d; e"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The sequence is Brunelleschi, Bramante, Palladio, Borromini, then Vanvitelli, from the early Renaissance to the eighteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":12,"stem":"Who wrote the novel Doctor Zhivago?","choices":["Gogol","Pasternak","Dostoevsky","Tolstoy","Chekhov"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Boris Pasternak wrote Doctor Zhivago; the other options are different Russian writers.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":13,"stem":"Match each film to its historical setting.\n1. The Patriot\n2. Braveheart\n3. Rome, Open City\n4. Good Bye, Lenin!\n5. Apocalypse Now\na. Resistance to Nazism and Fascism\nb. Vietnam War\nc. American War of Independence\nd. Fall of the Berlin Wall\ne. Struggle for Scottish independence","choices":["5–b; 2–e; 4–a; 3–d; 1–c","3–a; 5–c; 1–e; 2–b; 4–d","3–a; 5–b; 2–e; 4–d; 1–c","1–a; 4–b; 3–d; 5–c; 2–e","2–e; 1–b; 4–a; 3–d; 5–c"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Braveheart concerns Scotland; The Patriot the American Revolution; Rome, Open City the Resistance; Good Bye, Lenin! reunification-era Germany; Apocalypse Now Vietnam.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":14,"stem":"Marmolada belongs to which part of the Alps?","choices":["Carnic Alps","Dolomites","Cottian Alps","Julian Alps","Lepontine Alps"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Marmolada is a mountain massif in the Dolomites.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":15,"stem":"Complete the sentence with the appropriate noun: “A hiatal hernia is a … of part of the stomach from its normal abdominal position through the diaphragm.”","choices":["Protrusion","Intrusion","Eversion","Invasion","Abduction"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"“Protrusion” describes tissue projecting through an opening, which is the meaning required here.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":16,"stem":"Put these composers in chronological order of birth.\n1. Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart\n2. Johann Sebastian Bach\n3. Claudio Monteverdi\n4. Ottorino Respighi\n5. Giuseppe Verdi","choices":["2; 1; 3; 5; 4","3; 2; 1; 5; 4","3; 1; 2; 4; 5","1; 3; 2; 5; 4","2; 3; 1; 4; 5"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Monteverdi was born first, followed by Bach, Mozart, Verdi and Respighi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":17,"stem":"The famous villa known as La Rotonda is a work by:","choices":["Filippo Brunelleschi","Leon Battista Alberti","Donato Bramante","Giuliano da Sangallo","Andrea Palladio"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Palladio designed La Rotonda with a central domed hall and four temple-front porticoes.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":18,"stem":"Which style does the building in the photograph belong to?","choices":["Romanesque","Gothic","Neoclassical","Renaissance","Baroque"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The supplied key identifies it as Romanesque. Rounded arcading and heavy masonry are more useful clues than the rose window alone.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":19,"stem":"Who designed the important museum shown?","choices":["Frank O. Gehry","Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Philip Johnson","Frank Lloyd Wright","Alvar Aalto"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The spiral building is the Guggenheim Museum in New York, designed by Frank Lloyd Wright.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":20,"stem":"Which list places these art movements in the correct chronological order?","choices":["Neoclassicism; Impressionism; Expressionism; Pop Art","Impressionism; Pop Art; Expressionism; Neoclassicism","Impressionism; Expressionism; Neoclassicism; Pop Art","Pop Art; Expressionism; Impressionism; Neoclassicism","Neoclassicism; Impressionism; Pop Art; Expressionism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Neoclassicism precedes nineteenth-century Impressionism, then twentieth-century Expressionism and Pop Art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":21,"stem":"Which event was followed by the “Aventine Secession”?","choices":["The exceptional Fascist laws","The murder of Matteotti","The March on Rome","The imprisonment of Antonio Gramsci","The Biennio Rosso"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Opposition deputies withdrew from parliament after Giacomo Matteotti's murder in 1924.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":22,"stem":"What is the correct chronological order of these events?\na) Promulgation of the Italian Constitution\nb) Establishment of the Italian Republic\nc) Designation of Rome as capital\nd) March on Rome\ne) End of the Second World War","choices":["c; d; e; b; a","a; b; c; e; d","c; d; e; a; b","c; e; d; b; a","d; c; e; b; a"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Rome became capital before the 1922 March on Rome; the war ended in 1945, the Republic began in 1946, and the Constitution was promulgated in 1947.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":23,"stem":"What is the correct chronological order of these events?\na) Storming of the Bastille\nb) Napoleon Bonaparte's Russian campaign\nc) United States Declaration of Independence\nd) Congress of Vienna\ne) End of Napoleon III's empire","choices":["a; c; d; b; e","c; a; d; b; e","a; e; b; d; c","c; a; b; d; e","c; a; b; e; d"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The order is 1776, 1789, 1812, 1814–1815 and 1870.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":24,"stem":"In which year did the socialist government known as the Paris Commune exist?","choices":["1861","1871","1848","1815","1890"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Paris Commune governed Paris briefly in 1871.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":25,"stem":"Who was the first President of the Italian Republic?","choices":["Luigi Einaudi","Giovanni Leone","Sandro Pertini","Antonio Gramsci","Enrico De Nicola"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Enrico De Nicola was the Republic's first head of state; Einaudi succeeded him.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":26,"stem":"The term Blitzkrieg (“lightning war”) is mainly used for the rapid breakthrough tactics employed by:","choices":["Frederick II of Prussia","Josef Radetzky von Radetz","Adolf Hitler","Otto von Bismarck","Klemens von Metternich"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The term is associated with Nazi Germany's rapid, coordinated military offensives.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":27,"stem":"On which date was the State of Israel proclaimed?","choices":["5 June 1967","14 May 1948","20 January 1933","27 January 1945","6 October 1973"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Israel's declaration of independence was proclaimed on 14 May 1948.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":28,"stem":"What was the main square of a Greek city, bordered by important public buildings, called?","choices":["Agora","Forum","Acropolis","Platea","Peristyle"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The agora was the Greek city's central civic space. “Forum” is the corresponding Roman term.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":29,"stem":"With which architectural style is the Leaning Tower of Pisa associated?","choices":["Renaissance","Neoclassical","Gothic","Classical","Romanesque"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Its rounded arcades belong to the Pisan Romanesque tradition.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":30,"stem":"Who designed the Royal Palace of Caserta?","choices":["Giuseppe Piermarini","Filippo Raguzzini","Luigi Vanvitelli","Alessandro Galilei","Ferdinando Fuga"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Luigi Vanvitelli designed the palace for the Bourbon monarchy.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":31,"stem":"Which museum–city pairing is incorrect?","choices":["Hermitage – Moscow","Capodimonte – Naples","Metropolitan – New York","Bargello – Florence","Prado – Madrid"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Hermitage is in Saint Petersburg, not Moscow. The other city pairings are correct.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":32,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order of these four major poetry collections by Eugenio Montale?","choices":["Ossi di seppia; Satura; La bufera e altro; Le occasioni","Ossi di seppia; La bufera e altro; Le occasioni; Satura","Ossi di seppia; Le occasioni; La bufera e altro; Satura","Satura; Ossi di seppia; Le occasioni; La bufera e altro","Satura; Le occasioni; Ossi di seppia; La bufera e altro"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The publication sequence is Ossi di seppia, Le occasioni, La bufera e altro, then Satura.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":33,"stem":"Which of these energy sources is NOT renewable?","choices":["Geothermal energy","Solar energy","Wind energy","Energy produced using water","Energy produced from oil and its derivatives"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Oil is a finite fossil fuel that forms far more slowly than it is consumed.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":34,"stem":"Who is the head of the Church of England?","choices":["The most senior cardinal of the synod","The Patriarch of Edinburgh","The English monarch","The Pope","The Archbishop of Canterbury"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source refers to the monarch's role as Supreme Governor; the Archbishop of Canterbury is its senior bishop.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":35,"stem":"Match the works to their authors.\n1. Alcyone\n2. Zibaldone\n3. Ossi di seppia\n4. Myricae\n5. Il falso e il vero verde\na. Eugenio Montale\nb. Gabriele d'Annunzio\nc. Salvatore Quasimodo\nd. Giacomo Leopardi\ne. Giovanni Pascoli","choices":["1–b; 2–d; 3–a; 4–e; 5–c","1–b; 2–a; 3–e; 4–d; 5–c","1–a; 2–e; 3–c; 4–b; 5–d","1–c; 2–b; 3–d; 4–e; 5–a","1–e; 2–b; 3–d; 4–a; 5–c"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The authors are d'Annunzio, Leopardi, Montale, Pascoli and Quasimodo respectively.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":36,"stem":"From which language does the word “sponsor” originate?","choices":["Latin","English","Greek","German","French"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Although common in modern English, “sponsor” has a Latin origin.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":37,"stem":"In “Non capisco di che cosa parlino” (“I do not understand what they are talking about”), what kind of subordinate clause is present?","choices":["A clause of limitation","A declarative clause","A subject clause","An object clause","An indirect question"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"“Di che cosa parlino” expresses a question indirectly within the sentence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":38,"stem":"Match each building to its architect.\na. Robie House, Chicago\nb. School of Art, Glasgow\nc. Secession Building, Vienna\nd. AEG Turbine Factory, Berlin\ne. Villa Savoye, Poissy\n1. Charles Rennie Mackintosh\n2. Frank Lloyd Wright\n3. Peter Behrens\n4. Joseph Maria Olbrich\n5. Le Corbusier","choices":["a–2; b–1; c–4; d–3; e–5","a–5; b–4; c–2; d–1; e–3","a–3; b–4; c–1; d–2; e–5","a–5; b–1; c–4; d–3; e–2","a–2; b–3; c–5; d–1; e–4"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The correct architects are Wright, Mackintosh, Olbrich, Behrens and Le Corbusier, in that order.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":39,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Antonio Canova","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Michelangelo Buonarroti","Donatello","Alessandro Algardi"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The dramatic sculptural group is Bernini's Ecstasy of Saint Teresa.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":40,"stem":"Which event does NOT belong to the period of the Italian Risorgimento?","choices":["The First War of Independence","The signing of the Treaty of Campo Formio","The Expedition of the Thousand","The Second War of Independence","The Plombières Agreement"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Treaty of Campo Formio dates to the Napoleonic period, before the main Risorgimento events listed.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":41,"stem":"Which word is a synonym of “schernire” (“to mock”)?","choices":["To splinter","To dodge","To repair","To ridicule","To hide"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"“Schernire” and “canzonare” both mean to make fun of someone.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":42,"stem":"Which item does NOT belong with the others?","choices":["Aleutians","Andes","Andaman Islands","Caroline Islands","Maldives"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Andes are a mountain range; the other four are island groups.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":43,"stem":"In which year was the Berlin Wall built to divide East and West Berlin?","choices":["1946","1948","1951","1956","1961"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Construction of the Berlin Wall began in 1961.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":44,"stem":"The United Nations General Assembly:","choices":["Is democratically elected","Is a representative body of the member states","Is a meeting of the member states' prime ministers","Replaced the Security Council in 1996","Meets monthly in ordinary session"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The General Assembly brings together representatives of the UN member states.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":45,"stem":"In these lines, what is the grammatical subject of “orna”?\n“E invece tante foglie chiacchierine parlano\ndell'autunno che ritorna\ne che, sotto la pioggia fine fine, di pampini\ne di bacche agile s'orna”\n(The leaves speak of autumn, which returns and adorns itself with vine leaves and berries.)","choices":["che (“which”)","pioggia (“rain”)","autunno (“autumn”)","s' (“itself”)","foglie (“leaves”)"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The relative pronoun “che” is the grammatical subject of “orna”; its antecedent is “autunno”.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":46,"stem":"Which list contains only works written by Carlo Emilio Gadda?","choices":["La cognizione del dolore; La madonna dei filosofi; Quer pasticciaccio brutto de via Merulana","Quer pasticciaccio brutto de via Merulana; L'Adalgisa; Sorelle Materassi","Eros e Priapo; Novelle del Ducato in fiamme; Le menzogne della notte","L'Adalgisa; Conversazione in Sicilia; La cognizione del dolore","La madonna dei filosofi; Dialoghi con Leucò; I sogni e la folgore"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"All three works in the first list are by Gadda; each other list includes a work by another writer.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":47,"stem":"“What is actual is rational; what is rational is actual.” Which philosopher, also the author of Phenomenology of Spirit, made this statement?","choices":["Schopenhauer","Fichte","Aristotle","Vico","Hegel"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Hegel wrote Phenomenology of Spirit and is associated with this statement about rationality and actuality.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":48,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order?","choices":["Marx; Kant; Rousseau","Rousseau; Kant; Marx","Marx; Rousseau; Kant","Kant; Marx; Rousseau","Rousseau; Marx; Kant"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Rousseau was born before Kant; Marx belonged to the following century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":49,"stem":"The adjective “Magyar” refers to the people of which country?","choices":["India","Hungary","China","Korea","Russia"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"“Magyar” refers to Hungarians and their language.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":50,"stem":"“What special privilege has that little agitation of the brain which we call thought, that we must thus make it the model of the whole universe? Our partiality towards ourselves presents it on every occasion; but sound philosophy ought carefully to guard against so natural an illusion.” This passage is from Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion by which empiricist philosopher?","choices":["Pierre Bayle","David Hume","René Descartes","John Stuart Mill","Karl Marx"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"David Hume wrote Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion. The passage questions projecting human thought onto the universe.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":51,"stem":"What is meant by a “majority bonus” in an electoral system?","choices":["The winning coalition receives more parliamentary seats than it would under simple proportional allocation","Elected members of the winning coalition receive a cash prize","The winning coalition receives a cash prize","The winning coalition governs","The coalition containing the greatest number of parties receives at least 51% of parliamentary seats"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A majority bonus awards extra seats to the electoral winner; it is not a financial reward.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":52,"stem":"Who painted the work shown?","choices":["Wassily Kandinsky","Pablo Picasso","Georges Braque","Fernand Léger","Paul Klee"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The geometric face is Paul Klee's Senecio.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":53,"stem":"In which century was this depiction of an Ideal City painted?","choices":["Fifteenth","Sixteenth","Eighteenth","Nineteenth","Twentieth"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The ideal-city painting belongs to the fifteenth-century Renaissance tradition of perspective and ordered urban space.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":54,"stem":"Who painted this portrait?","choices":["Titian","Raphael","Michelangelo","Velázquez","El Greco"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The portrait is by Titian, the Venetian Renaissance painter.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":55,"stem":"When did Hitler become Chancellor of the Reich?","choices":["May 1928","October 1929","July 1932","January 1933","August 1934"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Hitler was appointed chancellor in January 1933; becoming chancellor and later consolidating power are different events.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":56,"stem":"To which artistic movement does the painting belong?","choices":["Pointillism","Symbolism","Romanticism","Realism","Impressionism"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The lively outdoor social scene, light and brushwork are characteristic of Impressionism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":57,"stem":"What was the “Battle for Grain”?","choices":["A revolt of German peasants led by Thomas Müntzer","A popular uprising in Paris that led to the storming of the Bastille","The climax of the Cossack revolt against the Russian army","The war between Venice and the Ottoman Empire for control of the Mediterranean","A Fascist campaign intended to make Italy less dependent on imports"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The campaign sought to increase domestic grain production and reduce Italy's dependence on imports.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":58,"stem":"Which of the following is NOT a type of vault?","choices":["Mirror vault (volta a schifo)","Pointed vault","Truss vault","Barrel vault","Cloister vault"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A truss is a framed roof structure, not a type of masonry vault.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":59,"stem":"Which four great Italians buried in Florence's Santa Croce are recalled by Foscolo in Dei Sepolcri to celebrate Italy's greatness?","choices":["Machiavelli, Michelangelo, Dante and Petrarch","Machiavelli, Michelangelo, Galileo and Alfieri","Dante, Petrarch, Alfieri and Galileo","Michelangelo, Galileo, Petrarch and Alfieri","Michelangelo, Alfieri, Petrarch and Dante"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The group is Machiavelli, Michelangelo, Galileo and Alfieri. Dante and Petrarch are not buried there.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":60,"stem":"Which writer and revolutionary founded Young Italy and inspired the Risorgimento?","choices":["Manzoni","Mazzini","Garibaldi","Cavour","Gioberti"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Giuseppe Mazzini founded Young Italy to promote national unity and a republic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":61,"stem":"Who was killed in the Capaci bombing?","choices":["Borsellino","Falcone","Dalla Chiesa","Fava","Mattarella"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Giovanni Falcone was killed at Capaci in 1992. Paolo Borsellino was killed in a separate bombing.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":62,"stem":"What does “protectionism” mean?","choices":["Tighter controls on people travelling between Schengen countries","A policy to expand a state's police forces","An economic policy opposed to free trade","An increase in military forces stationed at borders","A ban on the production, sale and transport of alcohol"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Protectionism shields domestic producers from foreign competition, commonly through trade barriers.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":63,"stem":"What is a palimpsest?","choices":["A simultaneous combination of distinct melodic lines performed by voices or instruments","A squared notepad fitted with a pencil holder","An exceptionally wide arch typical of late-antique architecture","An old manuscript on parchment from which an earlier text has been scraped away","A short musical interlude in a play with overt or concealed advertising purposes"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A palimpsest reuses a writing surface after an earlier text has been erased or scraped away.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":64,"stem":"What is syncretism?","choices":["Creating conditions that improve social life","Reconciling different cultural, philosophical or religious elements from different cultures","Strengthening the effect of an action","The need to overcome the chance nature of vision","The belief that everything an individual perceives is created by their own knowledge"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Syncretism combines or reconciles elements from different traditions or belief systems.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":65,"stem":"Who painted the view shown?","choices":["Giambattista Tiepolo","Tintoretto","Titian","Canaletto","Bernardo Bellotto"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The supplied work is a Venetian view by Canaletto, known for detailed cityscapes.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":66,"stem":"In which city is Delacroix's Liberty Leading the People located?","choices":["Rome","Lyon","Stockholm","Paris","London"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The painting belongs to the Louvre in Paris.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":67,"stem":"Which architectural order was developed by the Romans?","choices":["Tuscan","Corinthian","Doric","Ionic","Composite"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Composite order combines Ionic volutes with Corinthian acanthus-leaf decoration.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":68,"stem":"In which year did Joseph Paxton's Crystal Palace open for London's Great Exhibition?","choices":["1821","1851","1875","1889","1900"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Great Exhibition and its Crystal Palace opened in 1851.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":69,"stem":"What type of vault is represented in the diagram?","choices":["Barrel vault","Basin vault","Pavilion vault","Groin vault","Sail vault"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The drawing represents a cupola-like basin vault rising over a square bay.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":70,"stem":"An internationally renowned architect and designer died on 31 March 2016. Their Italian works include Rome's MAXXI museum and the Milan skyscraper nicknamed “Lo Storto”. Who was it?","choices":["Frank O. Gehry","Kazuyo Sejima","Zaha Hadid","Denise Scott Brown","Massimiliano Fuksas"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Zaha Hadid designed MAXXI and the twisting CityLife tower in Milan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":71,"stem":"What is the sculpture shown, and who made it?","choices":["Rondanini Pietà by Michelangelo Buonarroti","Vatican Pietà by Michelangelo Buonarroti","Pietà by Donatello","Palestrina Pietà by Michelangelo Buonarroti","Pietà by Antonello Gagini"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The upright, unfinished figures distinguish Michelangelo's Rondanini Pietà from his seated Vatican Pietà.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":72,"stem":"Who designed the Guggenheim Museum in Bilbao?","choices":["Zaha Hadid","Renzo Piano","Frank O. Gehry","Massimiliano Fuksas","Frank Lloyd Wright"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Frank Gehry designed the Bilbao museum; Frank Lloyd Wright designed the Guggenheim in New York.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":73,"stem":"Identify the title and artist of the painting shown.","choices":["The Cardsharps; Caravaggio","Girl Interrupted at Her Music; Jan Vermeer","The Triumph of Bacchus (The Drinkers); Diego Velázquez","Supper at Emmaus; Caravaggio","The Calling of Saint Matthew; Caravaggio"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The beam of light and Christ's gesture identify Caravaggio's Calling of Saint Matthew.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":74,"stem":"When was Filippo Tommaso Marinetti's Manifesto of Futurism published in Le Figaro?","choices":["1907","1909","1913","1925","1930"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The manifesto appeared in Le Figaro in 1909, marking Futurism's public launch.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":75,"stem":"What is the style of Victor Horta's Hôtel Tassel?","choices":["Art Nouveau","International Style","Baroque","Rococo","Rationalism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Its flowing plant-like ironwork is characteristic of Art Nouveau.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":76,"stem":"Who designed the building shown?","choices":["Pier Luigi Nervi","Giovanni Michelucci","Frank O. Gehry","Charles Garnier","Le Corbusier"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The curved roof and sculptural walls identify Le Corbusier's chapel at Ronchamp.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":77,"stem":"Which work is shown?","choices":["Oskar Kokoschka, The Bride of the Wind, 1914","John William Waterhouse, A Mermaid, 1900","Jackson Pollock, Autumn Rhythm, 1950","Marc Chagall, The Lovers, 1954–55","Egon Schiele, The Lovers, 1917"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The turbulent brushwork surrounding the reclining couple belongs to Kokoschka's The Bride of the Wind.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":78,"stem":"What happened on 27 January 1945?","choices":["The Allies entered Rome","The Allies landed at Salerno","Soviet troops liberated Auschwitz","Dresden was destroyed by bombing","The Red Cross denounced the genocide of the Jews"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Soviet troops liberated Auschwitz on 27 January 1945.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":79,"stem":"In which country did the Industrial Revolution begin?","choices":["United States","Netherlands","France","England","Russia"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Industrialisation began in England before spreading to other countries.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":80,"stem":"Which of these products was NOT designed by Philippe Starck?","choices":["1","2","3","4","5"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The supplied key identifies product 3, the winged corkscrew, as the exception.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":81,"stem":"Which peace treaty, signed on 28 June 1919, formally ended the First World War between Germany and the Allied powers?","choices":["Peace of Augsburg","Treaty of Versailles","Peace of Westphalia","Treaty of Vienna","Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Treaty of Versailles was signed on 28 June 1919; other treaties settled the war with other defeated powers.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":82,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Kazimir Malevich","Wassily Kandinsky","Joan Miró","Piet Mondrian","Paul Klee"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The abstract arrangement of lines and geometric forms is by Kandinsky.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":83,"stem":"Who composed the music used for the European Union's anthem?","choices":["Ludwig van Beethoven","Giuseppe Verdi","Frédéric Chopin","Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart","Richard Strauss"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The anthem uses the melody from Beethoven's Ode to Joy in his Ninth Symphony.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":84,"stem":"Which list gives the writers in correct chronological order?","choices":["Virgil; Pirandello; Leopardi; Boccaccio; Goldoni","Boccaccio; Leopardi; Pirandello; Goldoni; Virgil","Virgil; Boccaccio; Goldoni; Leopardi; Pirandello","Virgil; Goldoni; Boccaccio; Pirandello; Leopardi","Boccaccio; Virgil; Pirandello; Leopardi; Goldoni"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The order runs from ancient Rome through Boccaccio, Goldoni, Leopardi and finally Pirandello.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":85,"stem":"The area around the Cape of Good Hope is conventionally associated with the transition between which two oceans?","choices":["Atlantic and Arctic","Pacific and Atlantic","Indian and Pacific","Indian and Atlantic","Pacific and Arctic"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The correct pair is Indian and Atlantic; the formal ocean boundary lies farther east at Cape Agulhas, so the cape wording is approximate.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":86,"stem":"Which is the odd one out?","choices":["Danube","Rhine","Euphrates","Seine","Volga"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Euphrates is in Asia; the other four rivers are in Europe.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":87,"stem":"To which philosopher is the saying “Know thyself” associated?","choices":["Freud","Kant","No particular philosopher, because it is only an inscription on the temple at Delphi","Socrates","Epicurus"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The maxim predates Socrates and was inscribed at Delphi, but the source tests its strong association with Socratic philosophy.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":88,"stem":"Which film famously uses Richard Wagner's Ride of the Valkyries in a sequence?","choices":["The Deer Hunter","Good Morning, Vietnam","Platoon","Apocalypse Now","Full Metal Jacket"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Apocalypse Now uses Wagner's music in its helicopter-attack sequence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":89,"stem":"Match each redevelopment, restoration or renovation project to its contemporary architect.\n1. Genoa harbour (1992)\n2. Reichstag, Berlin (1992–99)\n3. Musée d'Orsay, Paris (1980–86)\n4. La Scala, Milan (2002–04)\n5. Palazzo della Triennale, Milan (2002)\na. Mario Botta\nb. Renzo Piano\nc. Norman Foster\nd. Gae Aulenti\ne. Michele De Lucchi","choices":["1–c; 2–b; 3–e; 4–a; 5–d","1–b; 2–c; 3–d; 4–a; 5–e","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d; 5–e","1–a; 2–c; 3–b; 4–e; 5–d","1–c; 2–a; 3–d; 4–b; 5–e"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The correct sequence is Piano, Foster, Aulenti, Botta and De Lucchi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":90,"stem":"Which event gave Hitler's government a pretext for a campaign of repression against communists in 1933?","choices":["The Reichstag fire","The Night of the Long Knives","The book burnings in Berlin","The failure to annex Austria to Nazi Germany","Stalin's rise to power in Russia"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Reichstag fire was used to justify emergency repression in 1933.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":91,"stem":"Who made the sculpture of Blessed Ludovica Albertoni shown, which is in Rome?","choices":["Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Antonio Canova","Antonio Raggi","Benvenuto Cellini","Francesco Borromini"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Bernini sculpted Blessed Ludovica Albertoni as a dramatic, emotionally expressive reclining figure.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":92,"stem":"Which term has no connection with the Greek temple?","choices":["Peristyle","Altar","Pronaos","Naos","Proscenium"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The proscenium belongs to a theatre; the other terms can be associated with a temple and its precinct.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":93,"stem":"Who painted the work shown?","choices":["Diego Velázquez","Eugène Delacroix","Rembrandt","Peter Paul Rubens","Francisco Goya"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The image is Goya's Saturn Devouring His Son.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":94,"stem":"In the Doryphoros, tense and relaxed parts of the body correspond in a crossed arrangement. What is this arrangement called?","choices":["Antithesis","Chiasmus","Counterpoint","Parallelism","Golden section"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Chiasmus balances the supporting leg with the opposite active arm, creating a diagonal correspondence.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":95,"stem":"In the 1942 fighting at El Alamein in Egypt, British forces commanded by Montgomery fought against:","choices":["Russian forces commanded by Stalin","Spanish forces commanded by Francisco Franco","Italian forces commanded by Mussolini","Italian and German forces commanded by Field Marshal E. J. Rommel","French forces commanded by General de Gaulle"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The opposing Axis force combined German and Italian units under Rommel's command.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":96,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Braque","Magritte","Mondrian","Monet","Modigliani"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The orthogonal arrangement of rectangular fields is associated with Mondrian's abstract compositions.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":97,"stem":"To which period does the painting belong?","choices":["Neoclassicism","Mannerism","Renaissance","Baroque","Gothic"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The profile portrait and carefully constructed landscape belong to Renaissance painting.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":98,"stem":"What is a rose window?","choices":["A large circular window in the centre of the façade of many churches","An architectural element at the centre of the apse","A decorative element on the shafts of Byzantine columns","A compass rose decorating many civic buildings","A mosaic depicting the miracle of Saint Francis and the thornless rose garden"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A rose window is a circular window, often divided by radiating stone tracery.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":99,"stem":"An encyclopedia defines this as a reduction in the value of a country's currency relative to a fixed parity expressed in foreign currencies or gold, changing its exchange rate. Which term is being defined?","choices":["Inflation","Contraction","Deflation","Devaluation","Stagnation"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Devaluation lowers a currency's official value relative to other currencies or a fixed standard.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":100,"stem":"What does the Italian term “galaverna” mean?","choices":["A deposit of tiny ice crystals on objects in fog when the temperature is below freezing","A mass of ice slowly moving down a valley towards the sea under gravity","A fracture in the Earth's crust along which movement has displaced the rock layers","A line on a map joining places with equal average cloud cover over a period","A cave containing many stalactites and stalagmites"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"“Galaverna” means rime: ice deposited on exposed surfaces in freezing fog.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":101,"stem":"Which definition matches “limitrofo”?","choices":["Affected by a contagious disease","A person with limited abilities","A worker who uses a file","A construction tool","Bordering or adjoining"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"“Limitrofo” describes something that borders or adjoins another territory.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":102,"stem":"What is the function of an uninterruptible power supply (UPS)?","choices":["To increase a PC's processing power in special circumstances","Only to connect groups of networked PCs","To encourage users' active participation in social networks","To protect a computer from power failures or voltage fluctuations","To run software without interruption in a particular environment"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A UPS supplies temporary backup power and helps protect equipment when the mains supply fails or fluctuates.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":103,"stem":"The Constitutio de feudis of Emperor Conrad II recognised that lesser vassals' fiefs could not be arbitrarily revoked and could be inherited. Without direct heirs, a fief could pass to close relatives; removal required serious grounds approved by a council of lesser vassals. Greater lords could not exchange, transfer or lease those fiefs without the holders' consent.\nWhat appears to have been the aim of this edict?","choices":["To deprive the great feudal lords of their lands and income","To reduce the power of the great feudal lords by strengthening lesser vassals","To strengthen lesser vassals' subordination to great lords","To preserve the hereditary rights of all feudal society established by the Capitular of Quierzy","To extend feudalism through delegated management that reduced the emperor's duties"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Protecting lesser vassals against arbitrary action weakened the great lords' control over them.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":104,"stem":"In computing, what is an algorithm?","choices":["A procedure that solves a problem in a number of steps that may be infinite","A procedure that solves a problem in a finite number of steps","A computer programming language","A computer hardware component","A synonym for an instruction"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"An algorithm is a defined procedure with a finite sequence of steps for solving a problem.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":105,"stem":"Who designed and led the construction of the first nuclear fission reactor, producing the first controlled nuclear chain reaction?","choices":["Julius Robert Oppenheimer","Enrico Fermi","Marie Curie","Albert Einstein","Niels Bohr"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Enrico Fermi led the team that achieved the controlled chain reaction at Chicago Pile-1.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":106,"stem":"Who devised the Modulor, a scale of proportions based on human measurements, illustrated below?","choices":["Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Le Corbusier","Alvar Aalto","Frank Lloyd Wright","Frank Gehry"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Le Corbusier developed the Modulor as a system connecting human dimensions and architectural proportions.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":107,"stem":"Which image shows a trifora, a three-light window?","choices":["1","2","3","4","5"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A trifora has three openings separated by small columns or supports, as in image 1.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":108,"stem":"Which architect is associated with the Bauhaus?","choices":["Walter Gropius","Frank Lloyd Wright","Le Corbusier","Giles Gilbert Scott","Alvar Aalto"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Gropius founded the Bauhaus and designed its Dessau building shown here.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":109,"stem":"Which place was the site of a famous battle of the Second Italian War of Independence?","choices":["Anzio","Montecassino","Caporetto","Lepanto","Solferino"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Battle of Solferino was fought during the Second Italian War of Independence in 1859.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":110,"stem":"What was the so-called “Fiume Question”?","choices":["A dispute between Fiume and other Dalmatian cities over dominance in the Adriatic after the First World War","An international dispute over sovereignty of Fiume arising after the Second World War","A dispute between Napoleon III and the Austrian Empire following the Armistice of Villafranca","An international dispute over sovereignty of Fiume arising after the First World War","A conflict between Italy and Tito's Yugoslavia over persecution of Italians in Istria and Dalmatia"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Fiume Question concerned control of the city after the First World War.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":111,"stem":"The dome shown belongs to:","choices":["San Lorenzo, Turin, by Guarino Guarini","The Chapel of the Holy Shroud, Turin, by Guarino Guarini","The Basilica of Superga near Turin, by Filippo Juvarra","Santa Chiara, Bra, Cuneo, by Bernardo Vittone","San Gregorio, Messina, by Filippo Juvarra"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The dome is Guarini's Chapel of the Holy Shroud in Turin.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":112,"stem":"In which interval was the People's Republic of China founded?","choices":["1915–1922","1923–1933","1934–1944","1956–1968","1945–1955"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The People's Republic of China was proclaimed in 1949, within 1945–1955.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":113,"stem":"Which writer formulated these three laws of robotics in works about robots?\n1. A robot may not injure a human being or, through inaction, allow a human being to come to harm.\n2. A robot must obey human orders unless they conflict with the First Law.\n3. A robot must protect its own existence unless that conflicts with the First or Second Law.","choices":["J. R. R. Tolkien","Isaac Asimov","J. K. Rowling","C. S. Lewis","Vladimir Nabokov"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Isaac Asimov introduced the fictional Three Laws of Robotics in his robot stories.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":114,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order of these three events?\nE1: Giorgio de Chirico paints The Disquieting Muses.\nE2: Roy Lichtenstein produces Crying Girl.\nE3: Van Gogh paints The Starry Night.","choices":["E2; E3; E1","E2; E1; E3","E3; E2; E1","E1; E2; E3","E3; E1; E2"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Van Gogh's painting comes first, followed by de Chirico's Metaphysical painting and Lichtenstein's Pop Art work.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":115,"stem":"Complete Article 76 of the Italian Constitution: “The exercise of legislative power may not be delegated to the … unless principles and guiding criteria have been established, and only for a limited time and for specified purposes.”","choices":["President of the Republic","High Council of the Judiciary","President of the Council of Ministers","Minister of Justice","Government"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The article refers to delegation to the Government, subject to the stated limits.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":116,"stem":"Which Italian word fits both definitions?\n• A way of living in relation to one's economic circumstances and means.\n• The highest of the male singing voices.","choices":["Livello","Baritono","Tenore","Basso","Tasso"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"“Tenore di vita” means standard of living; “tenore” also means tenor, the high male singing voice.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":117,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Joan Miró","Salvador Dalí","Pablo Picasso","Wassily Kandinsky","René Magritte"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The playful biomorphic forms and fine connecting lines belong to Miró's visual language.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":118,"stem":"Which diplomat represented the Austrian Empire at the Congress of Vienna?","choices":["Bismarck","Metternich","Waldheim","Radetzky","Bülow"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Metternich was Austria's leading diplomat at the Congress of Vienna.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":119,"id":"ALPHA-B002-Q119","major":"Art History","era":"Modern","label":"Pollock's dripping technique","stem":"Which artist shown in the photograph is famous for developing large-scale paintings with the dripping technique?","choices":["Andy Warhol","Roy Lichtenstein","Jackson Pollock","Piet Mondrian","Marcel Duchamp"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The photograph shows Pollock working on a canvas laid on the floor. He made dripping central to his practice, but was not the first person ever to drip paint.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3438.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"a4eeb1fef117b7a1eec7b67374a268d3d78db2bcc740f35138893942c5cc128f","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3438.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[175,758,477,1054]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":302,"height":296},"visualSubject":"Pollock's dripping technique","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants."},{"qid":120,"stem":"Who competed alongside Brunelleschi in the competition for a relief panel for the doors of the Florence Baptistery?","choices":["Domenico Ghirlandaio","Leon Battista Alberti","Giovanni Pisano","Lorenzo Ghiberti","Andrea del Verrocchio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Lorenzo Ghiberti and Brunelleschi submitted the famous competing reliefs; Ghiberti won the commission.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":121,"stem":"Which party was founded in Livorno on 21 January 1921?","choices":["The Radical Party","The Communist Party of Italy","Christian Democracy","The Italian Liberal Party","The Italian Socialist Party"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Communist Party of Italy was founded at Livorno in January 1921.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":122,"id":"ALPHA-B002-Q122","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Nineteenth-century chronology","stem":"Put these events in chronological order, using the end of the Congress of Vienna: 1. Crimean War; 2. First Sino-Japanese War; 3. Battle of Waterloo; 4. End of the Congress of Vienna; 5. American Civil War.","choices":["3; 1; 5; 2; 4","2; 1; 5; 4; 3","4; 3; 1; 5; 2","1; 2; 5; 3; 4","4; 3; 5; 1; 2"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Congress ended on 9 June 1815, before Waterloo on 18 June. The Crimean War began in 1853, the American Civil War in 1861 and the First Sino-Japanese War in 1894.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3438.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"2da323d84c64ad457322b1de3cb4aee00d5671e56755123304ec7aaf6faf2d1d"},{"qid":123,"stem":"De architectura, composed in the first century BCE, is the only architectural treatise to survive intact from antiquity. Who wrote it?","choices":["Strabo","Vitruvius","Tacitus","Virgil","Ptolemy"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Vitruvius wrote De architectura, the ten-book ancient architectural treatise.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":124,"stem":"Which artist–patron pairing is incorrect?","choices":["Filippo Juvarra – Victor Amadeus II of Savoy","Giulio Romano – Federico II Gonzaga","Giuseppe Piermarini – Louis XIV of Bourbon","Donato Bramante – Ludovico Sforza","Apollodorus of Damascus – Trajan"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Piermarini worked in the eighteenth century, after Louis XIV's reign; the pairing is chronologically incompatible.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":125,"stem":"Which architect did Giorgio Vasari NOT discuss in Lives of the Artists?","choices":["Filippo Brunelleschi","Galeazzo Alessi","Donato Bramante","Michelangelo Buonarroti","Francesco Borromini"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Borromini was born after Vasari's death, so he could not appear in Vasari's Lives.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":126,"stem":"The term “Early Middle Ages” commonly refers to which period?","choices":["From the fall of the Western Roman Empire to about the year 1000","Between the fall of Constantinople and the discovery of America","From the eleventh to the fifteenth century","The period when Gothic forms became established","The period when the Hellenistic kingdoms broke up"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The source uses the conventional division from the late fifth century to around 1000.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":127,"stem":"Which term has no specific architectural meaning?","choices":["Pendentive","Rudder","Sail","Spindle","Rib"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The other words have architectural uses; “rudder” is a steering device for a vessel.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":128,"stem":"Which list contains only works by Caravaggio?","choices":["The Lute Player; David and Goliath; The Conversion of Saint Paul","Madonna of the Goldfinch; Christ at the Column; The Beheading of Saint John the Baptist","Saint John at the Spring; Judith and Holofernes; Portrait of Maddalena Doni","Supper at Emmaus; Young Bacchus; The Three Philosophers","The Calling of Saint Matthew; David and Goliath; The Marriage of the Virgin"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The first list contains three Caravaggio works. Each other list mixes in a work by another artist.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":129,"stem":"Which group of buildings was designed by Le Corbusier?","choices":["Fagus Factory; Sommerfeld House; Siemensstadt housing estate","Villa Savoye; La Tourette monastery; La Roche–Jeanneret House","Villa Mairea; Paimio Sanatorium; Viipuri Library","Guggenheim Museum; Kaufmann House; Millard House","Schröder House; Van Gogh Museum; Dutch Pavilion at the Venice Biennale"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Villa Savoye, La Tourette and La Roche–Jeanneret are all by Le Corbusier.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":130,"stem":"What is the correct chronological order of the sculptures shown?","choices":["Venus; Dawn; Laocoön; Medusa; Hebe","Laocoön; Dawn; Hebe; Venus; Medusa","Venus; Laocoön; Dawn; Medusa; Hebe","Venus; Medusa; Laocoön; Dawn; Hebe","Laocoön; Venus; Medusa; Hebe; Dawn"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source's sequence runs from ancient Venus and Laocoön to Renaissance Dawn, Baroque Medusa and Neoclassical Hebe.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":131,"stem":"Cupid and Psyche, shown here, is a sculpture by:","choices":["Michelangelo Buonarroti","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Bertel Thorvaldsen","Antonio Canova","Donatello"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Canova sculpted the celebrated Neoclassical group of Cupid reviving Psyche.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":132,"stem":"What is the chronological order of these paintings?\n1. The Death of Marat (Jacques-Louis David)\n2. The Sacrifice of Iphigenia (Giambattista Tiepolo)\n3. Saint Francis in Ecstasy (Giovanni Bellini)\n4. The Removal of the Body of Saint Mark (Tintoretto)\n5. Return to the Fold (Giovanni Segantini)","choices":["3; 5; 4; 2; 1","1; 2; 4; 3; 5","1; 5; 3; 4; 2","4; 2; 1; 3; 5","3; 4; 2; 1; 5"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Bellini precedes Tintoretto, followed by Tiepolo, David and finally Segantini.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":133,"stem":"Why is Leonardo's painting technique called “sfumato”?","choices":["Because he worked with bare hands directly on the canvas to blur the paint, a very widespread oil-painting technique","Because he used imperceptible gradations while omitting preparatory drawing and shading","Because his painting consists of extremely rapid strokes and often dazzling, almost incandescent light","Because the subjects are enveloped in subtle light and shade, forming a unity without sharp internal boundaries","Because the technique uses extremely strong and sharply defined chiaroscuro"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Sfumato creates soft transitions between light and shade without hard outlines.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":134,"stem":"Of which style is this building a typical example?","choices":["Rococo","Neoclassical","Baroque","Futurist","Art Nouveau"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The flowing decorative façade is characteristic of Art Nouveau.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":135,"stem":"Which list places these European architects in the correct chronological order?","choices":["Herrera; Bramante; Bernini; Gaudí; Viollet-le-Duc","Bernini; Bramante; Viollet-le-Duc; Gaudí; Herrera","Gaudí; Viollet-le-Duc; Bramante; Herrera; Bernini","Viollet-le-Duc; Bernini; Gaudí; Herrera; Bramante","Bramante; Herrera; Bernini; Viollet-le-Duc; Gaudí"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The order runs from Renaissance Bramante and Herrera through Baroque Bernini to Viollet-le-Duc and Gaudí.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":136,"id":"ALPHA-B002-Q136","major":"Architecture","era":"Historical","label":"Louis XIV and Versailles","stem":"Under which king was the palace of Versailles shown in the photograph extensively enlarged into a royal residence?","choices":["Louis XIII","Louis XIV","Louis XV","Louis XVI","Louis XVIII"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Louis XIII built the original hunting lodge; Louis XIV carried out the major palace expansion. The stem distinguishes these two stages.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3440.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"4d9566a9cb871290e52517fc7f2464769f77fba11b8323c6eb2554bf0f46aab0","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3440.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[122,1126,542,1365]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":420,"height":239},"visualSubject":"Louis XIV and Versailles","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants."},{"qid":137,"stem":"Place the buildings represented by these plans in chronological order, from oldest to most recent.","choices":["2; 3; 1; 5; 4","4; 2; 3; 1; 5","4; 3; 2; 5; 1","2; 3; 1; 4; 5","2; 4; 1; 3; 5"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The source orders the Roman theatre, ancient central-plan building, Gothic church, Renaissance central-plan church and modern building.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":138,"stem":"What is the architectural style of Siena Cathedral?","choices":["Gothic","Renaissance","Eclectic","Romanesque","Baroque"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Siena Cathedral is a major example of Italian Gothic architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":139,"stem":"In which year did Japan attack Pearl Harbor?","choices":["1938","1939","1940","1941","1944"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The attack on Pearl Harbor took place on 7 December 1941.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":140,"stem":"In which year were Hiroshima and Nagasaki struck by atomic bombs?","choices":["1943","1945","1948","1940","1947"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Both atomic bombings took place in August 1945.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":141,"stem":"Which leaders attended the Yalta Conference?","choices":["Stalin, Ribbentrop, Churchill","Stalin, Truman, Chamberlain","Molotov, Roosevelt, Churchill","Stalin, Truman, Churchill","Stalin, Roosevelt, Churchill"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Yalta in February 1945 was attended by Stalin, Roosevelt and Churchill. The duplicated incorrect option has been replaced.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":141,"stem":"Which leaders attended the Yalta Conference?","choices":["Stalin, Ribbentrop, Churchill","Stalin, Truman, Chamberlain","Molotov, Roosevelt, Churchill","Stalin, Truman, Chamberlain","Stalin, Roosevelt, Churchill"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The three leaders at Yalta were Stalin, Roosevelt and Churchill. The source repeats one incorrect option.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B002-Q141","major":"History & Politics","label":"Yalta Conference participants","era":"20th century","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3441.jpeg","scoringKey":"E","contentFingerprint":"c7d431111d9fc0872616f1558aefb8ef3359b9bc0abf2a1dcc91704a0dc8720c"},{"qid":142,"stem":"To which year is the fall of the Western Roman Empire traditionally assigned?","choices":["800","313","476","625","576"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"476 is the conventional date, associated with the deposition of Romulus Augustulus.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":143,"stem":"What is the slight swelling of a column shaft about one-third of the way up called?","choices":["Abacus","Echinus","Entasis","Fluting","Tapering"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Entasis is the subtle convex curvature of a column shaft; fluting is its vertical grooves.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":144,"stem":"The Domus Aurea was built as the residence of:","choices":["Caligula","Trajan","Nero","Vespasian","Hadrian"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Nero commissioned the Domus Aurea, or Golden House, in Rome.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":145,"stem":"Who painted the Allegory of Good Government in Siena's Palazzo Pubblico?","choices":["Simone Martini","Ambrogio Lorenzetti","Duccio di Buoninsegna","Giotto","Cimabue"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Ambrogio Lorenzetti painted the civic fresco cycle on good and bad government.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":146,"stem":"Which artist created this famous cover in 1967 for the debut album of The Velvet Underground, a group whose album he also produced?","choices":["Robert Rauschenberg","Roy Lichtenstein","Christo","Andy Warhol","Keith Haring"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The banana cover is associated with Andy Warhol and The Velvet Underground & Nico.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":147,"stem":"What is the most appropriate term for a funerary monument that does not contain the deceased's remains?","choices":["Sarcophyll","Sarcophagus","Cenotaph","Cenobium","Mausoleum"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A cenotaph is an empty commemorative tomb; a sarcophagus is a container for a body.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":148,"stem":"The Crimean War (1853–56) was fought between:","choices":["Tsarist Russia and a European coalition allied with the Ottoman Empire","Britain, France and the Kingdom of Sardinia against the Ottoman Empire","Austria and Prussia against a coalition allied with Russia","The Ottoman Empire against a coalition allied with Russia","Britain and the Kingdom of Sardinia against the Ottoman Empire"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Britain, France and Sardinia supported the Ottoman Empire against Russia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":149,"stem":"The term “simony” derives from:","choices":["Simon Peter's refusal to admit his discipleship","The ban on worshipping sacred images decided at the Council of Nicaea","The Church's opposition to interference by sovereign princes","Simon Magus's wish to buy miraculous powers from the Apostles","Exorcisms performed by those called Simonians in the sixteenth century"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The name comes from Simon Magus's attempt to buy spiritual power; simony means trading in spiritual offices or benefits.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":150,"id":"ALPHA-B002-Q150","major":"Architecture","era":"Medieval","label":"Venetian Gothic architecture","stem":"Which listed building is a characteristic example of Venetian Gothic civic architecture?","choices":["Bell tower of Pisa Cathedral","Doge's Palace, Venice","Basilica of Sant'Ambrogio, Milan","Saint-Remi, Reims","Orvieto Cathedral"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Doge's Palace is Venetian Gothic. The original question asking for a non-Romanesque building also allowed Orvieto Cathedral, so the criterion is clarified.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3442.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"b40e73af6af0319c0fa7efa90774dfe99cf5a684903aa25f876d7859f11d0c96"},{"qid":151,"stem":"Who painted The Dance Class, shown here?","choices":["Édouard Manet","Claude Monet","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","Camille Pissarro","Edgar Degas"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Degas repeatedly depicted dancers, rehearsals and ballet classes.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":152,"stem":"Where is Michelangelo's David kept?","choices":["Florence, Galleria dell'Accademia","Paris, Louvre Museum","Rome, Saint Peter's Basilica","Milan, Sforza Castle","London, Victoria and Albert Museum"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The original David is in Florence's Galleria dell'Accademia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":153,"stem":"Which of these Pietà works is NOT attributed to Michelangelo?","choices":["Rondanini Pietà","Vatican Pietà","Palestrina Pietà","Bandini Pietà (Pietà of the Museo dell'Opera del Duomo)","Pietà of Nouans"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Pietà of Nouans is associated with Jean Fouquet, not Michelangelo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":154,"stem":"Who designed the Unité d'Habitation in Marseille?","choices":["Aalto","Le Corbusier","Mies van der Rohe","Gropius","Starck"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Le Corbusier designed the Marseille housing block as a large collective residential unit.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":155,"stem":"Who wrote the short story Il treno ha fischiato (The Train Has Whistled)?","choices":["Edmondo De Amicis","Luigi Pirandello","Grazia Deledda","Giovanni Verga","Gabriele d'Annunzio"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Il treno ha fischiato is a short story by Luigi Pirandello.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":156,"stem":"What is the technique of converting readable data into a coded form that can only be read or processed after deciphering called?","choices":["Calligraphy","Haplography","Stenography","Cryptography","Typing"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Cryptography protects information through encoding; decryption recovers its readable form.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":157,"stem":"Which pair does NOT consist of synonyms?","choices":["Mirage – Fata Morgana","Clear – Ringing (limpido – argentino)","Rough – Smooth","Strait – Channel","Fort – Bastion"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"“Scabro” means rough and “liscio” means smooth: they are opposites.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":158,"stem":"Which Roman building contains the tombs of the Italian kings Umberto I and Victor Emmanuel II?","choices":["Ara Pacis","Pantheon","Basilica of Santa Maria in Aracoeli","Basilica of Saint John Lateran","Altare della Patria"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Both kings are buried in the Pantheon in Rome.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":159,"stem":"What is the commonly used name for the psychosomatic reaction described as rapid heartbeat, dizziness, confusion and hallucinations when confronted with exceptionally beautiful art?","choices":["West syndrome","Stockholm syndrome","Stendhal syndrome","Arts syndrome","Down syndrome"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The cultural term tested by the source is “Stendhal syndrome”, named after the writer Stendhal.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":160,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order?\nE1: Beginning of the October Revolution.\nE2: Foundation of the Fascist Party.\nE3: Foundation of the German National Socialist Party.","choices":["E3; E1; E2","E1; E2; E3","E1; E3; E2","E2; E1; E3","E3; E2; E1"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The order is the 1917 revolution, the 1920 NSDAP and the 1921 National Fascist Party; the latter is distinct from the earlier Fascist movement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":161,"stem":"In architecture, what does “duplex” mean in the usage tested here?","choices":["A dwelling intended for two people","A dwelling with two rooms plus a bathroom","A two-family villa","A dwelling on two levels connected by an internal staircase","A hotel room with two beds"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Here “duplex” means a single dwelling arranged across two internally connected floors.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":162,"stem":"Which project was NOT designed by Renzo Piano?","choices":["Centre Pompidou, Paris","Auditorium Parco della Musica, Rome","Redevelopment of Potsdamer Platz, Berlin","Padre Pio Church, San Giovanni Rotondo","New headquarters of the Lombardy Region, Milan"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Lombardy regional headquarters is the exception; the other projects are associated with Piano.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":163,"stem":"Which product was NOT designed by the Castiglioni designers?","choices":["1","2","3","4","All the objects were designed by the Castiglioni brothers"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The supplied key identifies all four pictured objects as Castiglioni designs.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":164,"stem":"Which is NOT a work by Sigmund Freud?","choices":["The Interpretation of Dreams","Beyond Good and Evil","Totem and Taboo","The Ego and the Id","On Narcissism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Beyond Good and Evil is by Friedrich Nietzsche; the other works are by Freud.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":165,"stem":"If it is 19:00 in Rome on 4 September 2022, in which city is it already 5 September 2022?","choices":["Baku","Bombay","Lima","Cape Town","Seoul"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Seoul is far enough ahead of Rome for the local date to have passed midnight.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":166,"stem":"Summer in the Northern Hemisphere began on 20 June. Where was the Sun directly overhead at the solstice?","choices":["South Pole","Equator","Tropic of Capricorn","Tropic of Cancer","North Pole"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"At the June solstice, the Sun is overhead at the Tropic of Cancer.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":167,"stem":"Which archipelago is in the Atlantic Ocean?","choices":["Aegadian Islands","Cyclades","Sporades","Balearic Islands","Azores"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Azores lie in the Atlantic; the other island groups are in the Mediterranean.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":168,"stem":"Who founded Young Italy in 1831?","choices":["Giuseppe Garibaldi","Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour","Giuseppe Mazzini","Vincenzo Gioberti","Carlo Cattaneo"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Giuseppe Mazzini founded the movement to promote Italian national unity and republicanism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":169,"stem":"Which cereal was absent from the tables of the ancient Romans?","choices":["Barley","Wheat","Emmer","Rye","Maize"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Maize originated in the Americas and reached Europe long after ancient Roman times.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":170,"stem":"Which Italian verb-form label is incorrect?","choices":["Present gerund – avente","Present infinitive – avere","Past participle – avuto","Present participle – abbiente","Past infinitive – avere avuto"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The present gerund of “avere” is “avendo”, not “avente”.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":171,"stem":"Who designed the famous chaise longue shown?","choices":["Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Philippe Starck","Ron Arad","Le Corbusier","Joe Colombo"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The source credits Le Corbusier; the LC4 was developed with Pierre Jeanneret and Charlotte Perriand.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":172,"stem":"Which artist lived from 1881 to 1973?","choices":["Pablo Picasso","Vincent van Gogh","Antonio Canova","Eugène Delacroix","Édouard Monet"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"1881–1973 are Picasso's birth and death years.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":173,"id":"ALPHA-B003-Q173","major":"Architecture","era":"Renaissance","label":"Brunelleschi's lifespan","stem":"Which artist was born in 1377 and died in 1446?","choices":["Leonardo da Vinci","Francesco Borromini","Michelangelo Buonarroti","Filippo Brunelleschi","Raphael Sanzio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Brunelleschi lived from 1377 to 1446. The source prints 1466 as the death year, which is corrected here.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3444.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"f5910c2a405cbb09ab00cf22d70de8aca8d70ad2f0a6fa55ffe21399efa31ea0"},{"qid":174,"stem":"Who painted The Death of Marat?","choices":["Jean-Louis-Théodore Géricault","Jacques-Louis David","Francisco Goya","Gustave Courbet","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Jacques-Louis David painted the revolutionary martyr Marat in 1793.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":175,"stem":"In which year did Picasso paint Les Demoiselles d'Avignon?","choices":["1879","1944","1907","1935","1890"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Les Demoiselles d'Avignon dates to 1907 and anticipates Cubism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":176,"stem":"In which city did the government of the Italian Social Republic establish its seat?","choices":["Milan","Salò","Turin","Salerno","Rome"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The regime is commonly called the Republic of Salò after the town associated with its government.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":177,"stem":"Which artist described natural forms in terms of geometric solids, laying foundations for Cubism and twentieth-century avant-garde art?","choices":["Braque","Picasso","Matisse","Cézanne","Gauguin"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Cézanne's treatment of forms as geometric volumes strongly influenced the Cubists.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":178,"stem":"Who painted The Raft of the Medusa (1819), shown here?","choices":["Claude Monet","Édouard Manet","Gustave Courbet","Eugène Delacroix","Théodore Géricault"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Raft of the Medusa is a major Romantic painting by Théodore Géricault.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":179,"stem":"Which group correctly matches the cultural institutions to their cities? (The source labels the New York institution “Metropolitan Museum of Modern Art”.)","choices":["Centre Pompidou – Paris; Metropolitan Museum of Modern Art – Los Angeles; Tate Gallery – London; Prado Museum – Madrid","Centre Pompidou – Paris; Metropolitan Museum of Modern Art – New York; Tate Gallery – London; Prado Museum – Madrid","Centre Pompidou – Paris; Metropolitan Museum of Modern Art – New York; Tate Gallery – Edinburgh; Prado Museum – Madrid","Centre Pompidou – Lyon; Metropolitan Museum of Modern Art – San Francisco; Tate Gallery – Paris; Prado Museum – Barcelona","Centre Pompidou – Paris; Metropolitan Museum of Modern Art – Los Angeles; Tate Gallery – London; Prado Museum – Barcelona"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The correct city sequence is Paris, New York, London and Madrid. The source conflates the names of New York's Metropolitan Museum and Museum of Modern Art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":180,"stem":"Which pair does NOT show a stylistic parallel between two artists?","choices":["Klimt – Olbrich","Balla – Fortunato Depero","Mondrian – Van Doesburg","Dalí – Gaudí","David – Canova"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Dalí is associated with Surrealism and Gaudí with Catalan Modernisme; the other pairs share closer movement affiliations.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":181,"stem":"Which pair of artists were NOT contemporaries?","choices":["Van Gogh – Eiffel","Renoir – Piermarini","Mantegna – Bramante","Velázquez – Borromini","Kandinsky – Le Corbusier"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Piermarini died before Renoir was born; the other pairs' lifetimes overlapped.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":182,"stem":"What type of temple is represented by this plan?","choices":["Pseudoperipteral","Prostyle","Peripteral","Dipteral","In antis"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The two porch columns stand between the projecting side walls, the arrangement called “in antis”.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":183,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Marc Chagall","Wassily Kandinsky","Henri Matisse","Yves Klein","Paul Klee"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The silhouetted Icarus against a field of stars is by Henri Matisse.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":184,"stem":"Who founded Futurism?","choices":["Umberto Boccioni","Gabriele d'Annunzio","Luigi Russolo","Gino Severini","Filippo Tommaso Marinetti"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Marinetti launched Futurism through its founding manifesto.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":185,"stem":"Who introduced the “readymade”?","choices":["Andy Warhol","Marcel Duchamp","Man Ray","Francis Picabia","Joan Miró"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Duchamp presented selected ordinary manufactured objects as artworks, calling them readymades.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":186,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Kandinsky","Matisse","Miró","Klee","Braque"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The supplied painting, with its bird-like forms and stylised plants, is by Paul Klee.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":187,"stem":"Saint Helena was the mother of:","choices":["Constantine","Napoleon","Nero","Charlemagne","Frederick Barbarossa"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Saint Helena was the mother of Emperor Constantine.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":188,"id":"ALPHA-B003-Q188","major":"History & Politics","era":"Historical","label":"Anglo-Dutch maritime rivalry","stem":"Which nation contested England's maritime power in the North Sea during the seventeenth century?","choices":["Poland","The Dutch Republic","Denmark","Russia","Prussia"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"England and the Dutch Republic fought the Anglo-Dutch Wars. The source's Sweden option is replaced by the missing correct nation.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3445.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"ab51869a1fd16405e0b82332c834e119f8c17ef4f045019d4980b8b4090d7c58"},{"qid":189,"stem":"The Ten Days of Brescia belong to which conflict?","choices":["Second World War","First Italian War of Independence","Second Italian War of Independence","Third Italian War of Independence","First World War"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The uprising took place in 1849, during the First Italian War of Independence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":190,"stem":"Who painted the work shown?","choices":["Botero","Gauguin","Van Gogh","Cézanne","Munch"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Tahitian figures and broad areas of colour are characteristic of Paul Gauguin.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":191,"stem":"Match each painter to a work.\n1. Rembrandt\n2. Monet\n3. Matisse\n4. Dante Gabriel Rossetti\n5. Munch\na. La Ghirlandata\nb. The Scream\nc. The Anatomy Lesson of Dr Nicolaes Tulp\nd. The Dance\ne. Poppies","choices":["1–c; 2–a; 3–d; 4–e; 5–b","1–b; 2–e; 3–d; 4–a; 5–c","1–b; 2–a; 3–e; 4–d; 5–c","1–c; 2–e; 3–d; 4–a; 5–b","1–a; 2–e; 3–c; 4–d; 5–b"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Rembrandt painted the anatomy lesson, Monet Poppies, Matisse The Dance, Rossetti La Ghirlandata and Munch The Scream.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":192,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order of these people?\nGiotto (a), Leopardi (b), De Gasperi (c), Giovanni Giolitti (d), Beccaria (e).","choices":["a; e; b; d; c","a; b; e; c; d","e; a; c; b; d","b; a; c; d; e","e; b; a; d; c"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Giotto is earliest, followed by Beccaria, Leopardi, Giolitti and De Gasperi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":193,"stem":"Which term means “a word with substantially the same meaning as another, although its emotional, cultural or social associations may differ”?","choices":["Corresponding word","Similar word","Equivalent","Synonym","Related word"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A synonym has the same or nearly the same meaning, though usage and connotations may differ.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":194,"stem":"What subject did Giotto paint on the entrance wall of the Scrovegni Chapel in Padua?","choices":["The Death of Saint Francis","Saint Francis Preaching to the Birds","The Last Judgement","The Crucifixion","The Entombment"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Last Judgement fills the chapel's entrance wall.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":195,"stem":"On which date is International Holocaust Remembrance Day observed?","choices":["25 April","2 June","27 January","27 May","14 March"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"27 January marks the anniversary of the liberation of Auschwitz.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":196,"stem":"Which Italian word fits both definitions?\n• A sustained speculative rise in share or security prices beyond reasonable valuation.\n• A raised skin lesion containing fluid.","choices":["Depressione","Ferita","Contusione","Crollo","Bolla"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"“Bolla” means a financial bubble and also a blister.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":197,"stem":"Which is the correct chronological order?\nE1: The atomic bomb is dropped on Hiroshima.\nE2: The FIAT car company is founded.\nE3: The Barbie doll is created.","choices":["E3; E1; E2","E1; E2; E3","E3; E2; E1","E2; E1; E3","E2; E3; E1"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"FIAT was founded before the 1945 bombing, and Barbie was introduced later, in 1959.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":198,"stem":"What term matches this definition: “A soldier who voluntarily fights for pay for whoever temporarily hires them”?","choices":["Conspirator","Hired assassin","Janissary","Mercenary","Plotter"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A mercenary serves for payment under a temporary hiring arrangement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":199,"stem":"Which statement about Pop Art is incorrect?","choices":["Leo Castelli was a major gallery owner and promoter of Pop artists","Andy Warhol was one of its leading figures","Pop Art gained international recognition through the 1964 Venice Biennale","It is a form of abstract art","New Dada in 1950s America formed a transition from Action Painting to Pop Art"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Pop Art typically uses recognisable imagery from mass culture rather than being defined as abstract art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":200,"stem":"How did Umberto Boccioni represent figures in action?","choices":["With separate figures placed beside one another","Through rhythmic repetition of forms","With a single, simultaneous form","With a new form resembling the figures","With a form repeated in different colours"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Boccioni sought to integrate movement, body and surrounding space into one simultaneous form.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":201,"stem":"Match each person to the century most closely associated with them.\na. Titian\nb. Cavour\nc. Churchill\nd. Masaccio\ne. Petrarch\n1. Fourteenth\n2. Fifteenth\n3. Sixteenth\n4. Nineteenth\n5. Twentieth","choices":["a–1; b–5; c–4; d–2; e–3","a–2; b–4; c–5; d–1; e–3","a–3; b–2; c–5; d–4; e–1","a–3; b–4; c–5; d–2; e–1","a–1; b–5; c–2; d–3; e–4"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Titian belongs mainly to the sixteenth century, Cavour the nineteenth, Churchill the twentieth, Masaccio the fifteenth and Petrarch the fourteenth.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":202,"stem":"The wastepaper basket shown is called In attesa (“Waiting”). Its tilted opening is ready to receive thrown rubbish; this inclination is achieved by cutting the cylinder obliquely. Who designed it?","choices":["Lucio Fontana","Enzo Mari","Renzo Piano","Man Ray","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Enzo Mari designed In attesa, giving the wastebasket its characteristic tilted form.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":203,"stem":"Which statement about Picasso's Guernica is true?","choices":["It was painted in response to First World War bombing","It was displayed at New York's MoMA until the early 1980s","It was intended for the 1942 Universal Exposition in Rome","It was commissioned by Francisco Franco","Picasso painted it in the 1970s"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Guernica remained at MoMA until its return to Spain in 1981.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":204,"stem":"Who made The Sleep of Reason Produces Monsters, shown here?","choices":["Salvador Dalí","El Greco","Joan Miró","Francisco Goya","Frida Kahlo"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The print belongs to Francisco Goya's Los Caprichos series.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":205,"stem":"Christo is a major representative of:","choices":["Cubism","Art Informel","Realism","Futurism","Land Art"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source places Christo's large-scale environmental interventions within Land Art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":206,"stem":"Which of these works did Michelangelo begin last?","choices":["David","Doni Tondo","Sistine Chapel ceiling in Rome","Vatican Pietà","The Last Judgement"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Last Judgement came after the Pietà, David, Doni Tondo and the Sistine ceiling.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":207,"stem":"Which element does NOT appear in the conventional Roman temple plan?","choices":["Opisthodomos","Cornice","Tympanum","Podium","Pronaos"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Roman temple emphasises a frontal entrance and podium; the rear opisthodomos belongs to the Greek temple arrangement tested here.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":208,"stem":"Who is the principal deity in Zoroastrianism?","choices":["Allah","Yahweh","Janus","Ahura Mazda","Jehovah"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Ahura Mazda is the central deity of Zoroastrianism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":209,"stem":"Hellenistic culture developed the concept of the liberal arts. Which category was conceptually opposed to them?","choices":["Common arts","Martial arts","Illiberal arts","Sciences","Religions"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The source contrasts liberal studies with the practical or common arts.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":210,"stem":"The Wars of the Roses were fought in fifteenth-century England between which factions?","choices":["Tudor – Windsor","Lancaster – York","Windsor – Stuart","Tudor – Lancaster","York – Stuart"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The rival royal houses were Lancaster and York.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":211,"stem":"Who was king of the Kingdom of Italy whose capital was Milan?","choices":["Maxentius","Constantine","Charles Albert","Napoleon","Charlemagne"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The question refers to Napoleon's Kingdom of Italy, whose capital was Milan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":212,"stem":"Who was the last Tsar of all the Russias, who abdicated in 1917?","choices":["Ivan VI","Alexander III","Nicholas II","Alexei I","Stephen"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Nicholas II abdicated during the Russian Revolution of 1917.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":213,"stem":"What is the commonly used name for the protests and unrest that began in winter 2010–11 in North Africa and the Middle East?","choices":["Gulf War","Arab Spring","October Revolution","Pan-Arabism","Arab League"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The wave of uprisings is known as the Arab Spring.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":214,"stem":"Which Italian word is incorrectly defined?","choices":["Xilofago: an animal that feeds on wood","Verro: a small device for lifting weights","Ustorio: burning","Sinossi: a summary or overview of a subject","Talassocrazia: supremacy at sea"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"“Verro” means a male pig; a lifting device is a “verricello”.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":215,"stem":"The name of Austrian physicist Mach is used to describe:","choices":["Supersonic speed","Repulsion between electric charges of the same sign","The temperature that turns matter into plasma","The pressure that turns matter into plasma","The speed at which apples fall"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Mach number compares speed with the local speed of sound; a value above 1 is supersonic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":216,"stem":"What is the characteristic shape of a spire?","choices":["Spherical","Conical","A truncated pyramid","A short stone marker","Pyramidal"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source identifies the spire's pointed, pyramidal form.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":217,"stem":"Which term is synonymous with “spire” in the architectural usage tested here?","choices":["Pinnacle","Impluvium","Lantern","Hypogeum","Pilaster strip"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A pinnacle is a small pointed architectural feature; the other terms describe different elements.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":218,"stem":"Where was the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party founded in 1898?","choices":["London","Rome","Moscow","Saint Petersburg","Minsk"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Its founding congress was held in Minsk in 1898.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":219,"stem":"What was the political orientation of the Mensheviks?","choices":["Socialist","Liberal","Communist","Reactionary","Bourgeois"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Mensheviks were a socialist faction of the Russian social-democratic movement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":220,"stem":"In which century did Mannerism become established?","choices":["Fifteenth","Fourteenth","Sixteenth","Thirteenth","Twelfth"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Mannerism developed in the sixteenth century after the High Renaissance.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":221,"stem":"When did the last Ottoman siege of Vienna take place?","choices":["1754","1320","1431","1683","1745"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The second and final Ottoman siege of Vienna took place in 1683.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":222,"stem":"Which city was never a major political-administrative centre of the Roman Empire in the sense tested here?","choices":["Thessaloniki","Pavia","Trier","Milan","Ravenna"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The source identifies Pavia as the exception; it is particularly associated with the later Lombard kingdom.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":223,"stem":"Which event did NOT occur in the sixteenth century?","choices":["The circumnavigation of the globe","The Council of Trent","Luther's excommunication","The death of Lorenzo the Magnificent","The Sack of Rome"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Lorenzo de' Medici died in 1492, in the fifteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":224,"stem":"The Great Depression began in 1929 with:","choices":["The adoption of the dollar as the sole currency of international trade","The start of US federal banking reform and creation of the Federal Reserve","The Wall Street stock-market crash","The establishment of the gold standard","The establishment of GATT"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Wall Street crash of 1929 is the event associated with the beginning of the Great Depression.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":225,"stem":"Which writer is known for using the stream-of-consciousness technique?","choices":["Joyce","Kafka","Dostoevsky","Camus","De Beauvoir"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"James Joyce is a major practitioner of stream-of-consciousness narration.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":226,"stem":"Title V of the Italian Constitution was reformed in 2001. What does it concern?","choices":["Safeguards protecting the Constitution","The judicial system","Relations between the Italian Government and the European Union","The organisation of local and regional authorities","Citizens' rights and duties in ethical-social relations"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Title V concerns regions, provinces and municipalities and their place in the Republic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":227,"stem":"Which politician did NOT live to see Rome become the capital of Italy?","choices":["Urbano Rattazzi","Giuseppe Zanardelli","Camillo Cavour","Francesco Crispi","Giovanni Giolitti"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Cavour died in 1861, before Rome became Italy's capital.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":228,"stem":"The Neolithic period takes its name from:","choices":["The geological properties of new rock strata rather than human activity","People learning to use new kinds of stone","The domestication of animals such as the dog","The use of fire for essential daily activities","The use of polishing in stone-working"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The “New Stone Age” is conventionally associated with polished stone tools.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":229,"stem":"Who were called “Huguenots”?","choices":["French Protestants","Swiss confederates","English Puritans","Followers of Hugh the Conqueror","Followers of Cromwell"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Huguenots were French Protestants, especially those of the Reformed tradition.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":230,"stem":"In 1293 Giano della Bella secured approval of Florence's Ordinances of Justice. What was their most important consequence?","choices":["Only the White Guelphs were excluded from city government","The magnates, traditional nobles and great landowners, were excluded from city government","Both Black and White Guelphs were excluded from city government","The lower working population gained access to city government","The bourgeoisie of artisans, merchants and professionals was excluded from city government"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The ordinances limited the political power of the magnates in Florence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":231,"stem":"Which term does NOT belong with the others?","choices":["Stylobate","Peristasis","Amphitryon","Pronaos","Opisthodomos"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The other terms describe Greek temple features; Amphitryon is a mythological name.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":232,"stem":"Who painted The Song of Love, shown below?","choices":["René Magritte","Marc Chagall","Giorgio de Chirico","Carlo Carrà","Salvador Dalí"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The unusual juxtaposition of a classical head, glove and ball is de Chirico's The Song of Love.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":233,"stem":"Which pair from Greco-Roman mythology is represented in Bernini's sculpture shown here?","choices":["Cupid and Psyche","Apollo and Daphne","Perseus and Andromeda","Pluto and Proserpina","Orpheus and Eurydice"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Daphne's transformation into a laurel tree identifies Bernini's Apollo and Daphne.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":234,"stem":"Cast iron is:","choices":["Hardened mild iron","An iron alloy containing copper","An alloy of iron and tin","An iron alloy containing lead","An alloy of iron and carbon"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Cast iron is an iron–carbon alloy with a higher carbon content than typical steel.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":235,"stem":"Which country is NOT part of the Schengen Area?","choices":["Finland","Iceland","Switzerland","United Kingdom","Norway"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The United Kingdom is outside Schengen; Schengen membership and EU membership are different things.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":236,"stem":"Which invention is NOT attributed to Benjamin Franklin?","choices":["The lightning rod","The glass harmonica","The phonograph","Bifocal lenses","The idea of daylight saving time"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Thomas Edison developed the phonograph. Franklin is associated with the other inventions or, in the case of daylight saving, an early satirical proposal.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":237,"stem":"Which maritime city was governed by a doge?","choices":["Genoa","Amalfi","Pisa","Bari","Taranto"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Genoa's republic was governed by a doge; Venice also used the title but is not an option here.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":238,"stem":"Which philosopher does NOT belong to the Renaissance period?","choices":["Pico della Mirandola","Roger Bacon","Marsilio Ficino","Giordano Bruno","Tommaso Campanella"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Roger Bacon lived in the thirteenth century, before the Renaissance figures listed.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":239,"stem":"Which is an antonym of “placid”?","choices":["Wicked","Perverse","Calm","Phlegmatic","Restless"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"“Placid” means calm or peaceful; “restless” expresses the opposite.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":240,"stem":"Which of these is NOT one of Descartes's rules of method?","choices":["Evidence","Analysis","Synthesis","Enumeration and review","Verisimilitude"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The four rules concern evidence, analysis, orderly synthesis, and comprehensive enumeration; verisimilitude is not one of them.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":241,"stem":"Who made this Surrealist work?","choices":["Salvador Dalí","René Magritte","Paul Klee","Max Ernst","Marcel Duchamp"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The melting watches identify Dalí's The Persistence of Memory.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":242,"stem":"A work by Marcel Duchamp appears in this photograph. What is its title?","choices":["Fountain (1917)","The Large Glass (1923)","The Chess Players (1911)","L.H.O.O.Q. (1919)","Wheel (1951)"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The urinal visible in the photograph is Duchamp's Fountain.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":243,"stem":"In architecture, what is a “parasta”?","choices":["A pier integrated into masonry with a load-bearing function","An interior church decoration used on solemn occasions","A terracotta ornament crowning a roof","A part of the roofing","A column integrated into masonry without a load-bearing function"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The source distinguishes the structural engaged pier, or parasta, from a purely decorative wall strip.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":244,"stem":"Which sequence gives an island, a peninsula and a strait, in that order?","choices":["Sicily; Italy; Dardanelles","Caprera; Gibraltar; Denmark","Peloponnese; Rhodes; English Channel","Elba; Strait of Bonifacio; Ustica","Gibraltar; Italy; Sicily"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Sicily is an island, Italy a peninsula, and the Dardanelles a strait.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":245,"stem":"The summer Salzburg Festival in Austria is dedicated to:","choices":["Music","Poetry","Cinema","Athletics","Gastronomy"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Salzburg Festival is best known for music, including opera and concerts.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":246,"stem":"Which word is opposite in meaning to “exposed”?","choices":["Hidden","Displayed","Inhibited","Arranged","Put away"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"“Hidden” is the direct opposite of exposed or visible.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":247,"stem":"What is the characteristic verse form of the Italian epic poem?","choices":["Sonnet","A tercet of hendecasyllables","Ottava rima","Ode","Canzone"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Ottava rima uses eight-line stanzas and is characteristic of the Italian literary epic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":248,"stem":"In which Italian region is the Sila?","choices":["Apulia","Campania","Sicily","Calabria","Sardinia"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Sila is a mountainous plateau in Calabria.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":249,"stem":"Which musical instrument does NOT belong to the same group as the others?","choices":["Trumpet","Violin","Viola","Cello","Guitar"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The trumpet is a brass instrument; the others are string instruments.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":250,"stem":"Sigmund Freud is famous as:","choices":["A Nobel laureate","The founder of the New Age movement","The leading figure of the Protestant Reformation","A protagonist of the French Revolution","The father of psychoanalysis"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Freud founded psychoanalysis.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":251,"stem":"Which artist used a bicycle wheel in one of their works?","choices":["Salvador Dalí","René Magritte","Paul Klee","Max Ernst","Marcel Duchamp"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Duchamp's Bicycle Wheel combines a wheel with a stool as an early readymade.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":252,"stem":"Which was NOT an Etruscan city?","choices":["Populonia","Salerno","Capua","Cumae","Chiusi"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Cumae was founded as a Greek colony and is the exception in the source's list.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":253,"stem":"At the Battle of Magenta (1859), the … army fought the … army, which emerged victorious.","choices":["Franco-Piedmontese; Austrian","None of the other answers is correct","Austrian; Franco-Piedmontese","Piedmontese; French","Austrian; Russian"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The French and Piedmontese side defeated the Austrians at Magenta.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":254,"id":"ALPHA-B003-Q254","major":"History & Politics","era":"Modern","label":"Risiera di San Sabba in Trieste","stem":"Which listed Italian city was the location of the Nazi detention and killing site Risiera di San Sabba during the Second World War?","choices":["Auschwitz","Sachsenhausen","Trieste","Trento","Dachau"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Risiera di San Sabba was in Trieste. The original broad claim about the absence of any camp is replaced by a verifiable site-location question.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3452.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"8cc8534ad0e8b3552a37e0f977a3d95822fc544f7b5ce92b88548ddf7cf428ec"},{"qid":255,"stem":"The ruins of Herculaneum and Pompeii provide examples of:","choices":["Greek architecture","Late-antique settlements","Religious architecture","Prehistoric settlements","Residential architecture"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Their preserved houses are major evidence for ancient Roman residential architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":256,"stem":"Who wrote the novel Mastro-don Gesualdo?","choices":["Alessandro Manzoni","Luigi Pirandello","Leonardo Sciascia","Giovanni Verga","Ignazio Silone"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Mastro-don Gesualdo is a novel by Giovanni Verga.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":257,"stem":"What is the normal term of office of the elected members of the Italian Parliament?","choices":["As many years as the government remains in office","Four years for both chambers","Seven years for both chambers","Four years for senators and five for deputies","Five years for both chambers"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The normal parliamentary term is five years for both chambers, unless they are dissolved earlier.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":258,"stem":"Where does the Baroque style lie chronologically?","choices":["Between Renaissance art and Mannerism","Between the end of Mannerism and the beginning of Rococo","Between Mannerism and the Renaissance","Between Rococo and Neoclassicism","Between Impressionism and Expressionism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The conventional sequence is Renaissance, Mannerism, Baroque, then Rococo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":259,"stem":"Construction of the Parthenon began in:","choices":["1444 BCE","447 BCE","823 CE","1700 CE","2478 BCE"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Work on the classical Parthenon began in 447 BCE.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":260,"stem":"Match each twentieth-century novelist to the novel.\n1. Robert Musil\n2. Franz Kafka\n3. Jean-Paul Sartre\n4. Henry Miller\n5. Günter Grass\na. Nausea\nb. The Tin Drum\nc. Tropic of Cancer\nd. The Trial\ne. The Confusions of Young Törless","choices":["3–a; 1–e; 5–b; 2–c; 4–d","4–c; 2–a; 1–e; 3–d; 5–b","5–d; 1–e; 2–a; 3–c; 4–b","1–c; 3–a; 5–e; 2–b; 4–d","2–d; 4–c; 1–e; 5–b; 3–a"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Kafka wrote The Trial, Miller Tropic of Cancer, Musil Young Törless, Grass The Tin Drum and Sartre Nausea.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":261,"stem":"Match each country to the capital or seat of government used in the list.\n1. Bolivia\n2. Colombia\n3. Paraguay\n4. Ecuador\na. La Paz\nb. Quito\nc. Asunción\nd. Bogotá","choices":["1–c; 2–b; 3–a; 4–d","1–d; 2–a; 3–b; 4–c","1–b; 2–c; 3–d; 4–a","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d","1–a; 2–d; 3–c; 4–b"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source uses La Paz, Bolivia's seat of government; its constitutional capital is Sucre. The other matches are Bogotá, Asunción and Quito.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":262,"stem":"What does “domotics” mean?","choices":["Science applied to construction techniques","Applied science studying machines, tools and workplaces to maximise their functionality","The study of measurements made with a domograph","The study and application of technologies to improve quality of life in built environments","The history of material culture relating to dwellings"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Domotics concerns home automation and technologies that improve comfort, control and efficiency in buildings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":263,"stem":"Which is a characteristic building type of classical Roman architecture?","choices":["Temple","Broletto","Obelisk","Basilica","Cathedral"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The basilica was a major Roman civic building type used for public and legal activities.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":264,"stem":"The diagram represents which type of theatre?","choices":["Celtic","Roman","Etruscan","Greek","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The open circular orchestra and the relation of seating to the stage identify the Greek theatre type.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":265,"stem":"Which statement is incorrect?","choices":["A Greek theatre is cut into the slope of a natural hill","A Roman theatre is an entirely built structure","A Greek theatre is typically an urban building","Greek theatre seating is divided into sectors by radial stairways","Roman theatre seating is built up on supporting structures"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source contrasts the Greek theatre's hillside setting with the Roman theatre's more independent urban structure.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":266,"stem":"When did the Council of Trent take place?","choices":["1545–1563","1945–1948","1600–1630","1280–1310","1915–1918"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Council of Trent met in several sessions between 1545 and 1563.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":267,"stem":"Match each US state to its capital.\n1. California\n2. Tennessee\n3. Indiana\n4. Arizona\na. Nashville\nb. Phoenix\nc. Sacramento\nd. Indianapolis","choices":["1–a; 2–b; 3–c; 4–d","1–b; 2–c; 3–d; 4–a","1–c; 2–a; 3–d; 4–b","1–a; 2–b; 3–d; 4–c","1–d; 2–a; 3–c; 4–b"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The capitals are Sacramento, Nashville, Indianapolis and Phoenix respectively.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":268,"stem":"Which of these Italian words is a proper name?","choices":["Lieto","Fausto","Ameno","Piacevole","Allegro"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Fausto is a personal name; the list otherwise consists of descriptive adjectives.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":269,"stem":"Who wrote Buddenbrooks?","choices":["Lord Byron","Molière","Thomas Mann","J. W. Goethe","Fyodor Dostoevsky"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Buddenbrooks is Thomas Mann's novel about the decline of a family.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":270,"stem":"Which civilisation built the Mexican archaeological site of Chichén Itzá?","choices":["Aztec","Maya","Inca","Quechua","Guaraní"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Chichén Itzá is a Maya site on the Yucatán Peninsula.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":271,"stem":"“Fordism” refers to a system of production that made extensive use of:","choices":["The assembly line","A limited division of labour","New technologies","Extensive specialisation of labour","New production tools"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The moving assembly line is the defining production method associated with Fordism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":272,"stem":"The echinus is part of which architectural element?","choices":["Column","Frieze","Capital","Tympanum","Architrave"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The echinus is the curved member of a capital, particularly recognisable in the Doric order.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":273,"stem":"Which Greek architectural order has a capital characterised by two symmetrical volutes?","choices":["Doric","Ionic","Corinthian","Tuscan","Composite"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Ionic capital is distinguished by its scroll-like volutes.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":274,"stem":"Which architectural orders have acanthus leaves carved on the capital?","choices":["Doric and Ionic","Ionic and Corinthian","Corinthian and Tuscan","Tuscan and Doric","Composite and Corinthian"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Both Corinthian and Composite capitals use acanthus-leaf ornament.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":275,"stem":"The Chicago School explored which new architectural building type?","choices":["Shopping centre","Skyscraper","Detached house","Terraced house","Auditorium"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Chicago School is associated with the early development of the skyscraper.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":276,"stem":"Who painted the Camera Picta, or Camera degli Sposi, in the north-east tower of Mantua's Castle of San Giorgio?","choices":["Andrea Mantegna","Giotto","Giambattista Tiepolo","Sandro Botticelli","Masaccio"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Mantegna painted the room, including its illusionistic ceiling opening.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":277,"stem":"In which chronological order were these buildings constructed?\na. Palazzo Pitti, Florence, attributed in the source to Brunelleschi\nb. Piazza dei Miracoli complex, Pisa\nc. Sant'Agnese by Borromini, Rome\nd. Sacconi's Vittoriano, Rome\ne. Pantheon, Rome","choices":["e; a; c; d; c","a; b; e; c; d","e; b; a; c; d","e; c; a; d; b","b; a; e; c; d"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The sequence is ancient Pantheon, medieval Pisa, Renaissance Pitti, Baroque Sant'Agnese, then the modern Vittoriano.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":278,"stem":"A monumental complex beside the Capitoline Hill celebrates Italian unity and centres on a huge bronze equestrian statue of a king. Before installation in 1911, the hollow horse reportedly hosted a meal for twenty-one people. Which king gives the monument its name?","choices":["Umberto I","Victor Emmanuel II","Emanuele Filiberto","Umberto II","Victor Emmanuel I"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Vittoriano commemorates Victor Emmanuel II, the first king of unified Italy.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":279,"stem":"Which king ruled France after Louis XVIII?","choices":["Prince Polignac","Louis Philippe of Orléans","Louis Napoleon","Charles X","Charles Albert of Savoy-Carignano"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Charles X succeeded Louis XVIII during the Bourbon Restoration.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":280,"stem":"Match each pictured product to its designer.\na. Philippe Starck\nb. Ron Arad\nc. Gio Ponti\nd. Bruno Munari\ne. Marco Zanuso","choices":["1–e; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d; 5–b","1–a; 2–b; 3–d; 4–c; 5–e","1–e; 2–d; 3–a; 4–c; 5–b","1–b; 2–c; 3–a; 4–d; 5–e","1–a; 2–d; 3–b; 4–c; 5–d"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The designers are Zanuso, Munari, Starck, Ponti and Arad, in the order shown.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":281,"stem":"Which list places the historical figures in correct chronological order?","choices":["Girolamo Savonarola; Theodoric; Conradin of Swabia; Bismarck; Napoleon I; Peter the Great","Theodoric; Conradin of Swabia; Girolamo Savonarola; Peter the Great; Napoleon I; Bismarck","Conradin of Swabia; Theodoric; Girolamo Savonarola; Napoleon I; Peter the Great; Bismarck","Theodoric; Girolamo Savonarola; Conradin of Swabia; Peter the Great; Bismarck; Napoleon I","Conradin of Swabia; Girolamo Savonarola; Theodoric; Peter the Great; Napoleon I; Bismarck"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The sequence runs from late-antique Theodoric to medieval Conradin, Renaissance Savonarola, Peter the Great, Napoleon and Bismarck.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":282,"stem":"Which list is in correct chronological order?","choices":["Giovanni Bellini; Masaccio; Giuseppe Piermarini; Andrea Palladio; Gio Ponti","Masaccio; Andrea Palladio; Giovanni Bellini; Giuseppe Piermarini; Gio Ponti","Andrea Palladio; Masaccio; Giovanni Bellini; Gio Ponti; Giuseppe Piermarini","Masaccio; Giovanni Bellini; Andrea Palladio; Giuseppe Piermarini; Gio Ponti","Giovanni Bellini; Andrea Palladio; Masaccio; Gio Ponti; Giuseppe Piermarini"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The sequence moves from early Renaissance Masaccio to Bellini, Palladio, Piermarini and twentieth-century Ponti.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":283,"stem":"What is the main purpose of virtual memory?","choices":["To allow antivirus programs to run","To prevent the processor from overheating","To identify unauthorised access to a computer","To simulate a larger main memory by using secondary storage","To simulate an unattainable degree of efficiency"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Virtual memory uses storage to extend the addressable memory beyond the physical RAM available.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":284,"stem":"Match each painter to the work.\na. Klimt\nb. Matisse\nc. Botticelli\nd. Rembrandt\ne. Titian\n1. Primavera\n2. The Kiss\n3. Goldfish\n4. The Night Watch\n5. Sacred and Profane Love","choices":["a–3; b–5; c–2; d–4; e–1","a–2; b–3; c–1; d–4; e–5","a–2; b–3; c–1; d–5; e–4","a–1; b–2; c–4; d–3; e–5","a–5; b–4; c–1; d–3; e–2"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The matches are Klimt–The Kiss, Matisse–Goldfish, Botticelli–Primavera, Rembrandt–The Night Watch and Titian–Sacred and Profane Love.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":285,"stem":"What does the acronym ENEA stand for in the formulation used by the source?","choices":["National organisation for alternative energy","Atomic wind nuclear energy","Organisation for the new environmental ecology","Organisation for new technologies, energy and the environment","European nuclear energy atomic"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The source uses the historical name referring to new technologies, energy and the environment.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":286,"stem":"In which Roman fountain did Anita Ekberg appear with Marcello Mastroianni in the famous scene from Fellini's La Dolce Vita?","choices":["Neptune Fountain in Piazza Navona","Barcaccia Fountain in Piazza di Spagna","Fountain of the Naiads in Piazza della Repubblica","Trevi Fountain in Piazza di Trevi","Triton Fountain in Piazza Barberini"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The celebrated fountain scene takes place at the Trevi Fountain.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":287,"stem":"Filarete (a), Donatello (b), Michelucci (c), Mondrian (d) and Canova (e) comprise two architects, two sculptors and one painter. Which matching is correct?","choices":["a – painter; b – sculptor; c – architect; d – sculptor; e – architect","a – architect; b – painter; c – architect; d – sculptor; e – sculptor","a – sculptor; b – painter; c – sculptor; d – architect; e – architect","a – architect; b – sculptor; c – architect; d – painter; e – sculptor","a – sculptor; b – architect; c – architect; d – painter; e – sculptor"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Filarete and Michelucci are the architects, Donatello and Canova the sculptors, and Mondrian the painter.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":288,"id":"ALPHA-B004-Q288","major":"Science & Tech","era":"Renaissance","label":"Copernicus's heliocentric model","stem":"Who published a mathematical heliocentric model of the planetary system in De revolutionibus orbium coelestium in 1543?","choices":["Isaac Newton","Tycho Brahe","Galileo Galilei","Johannes Kepler","Nicolaus Copernicus"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Copernicus published the model in 1543. A mathematical model is not the same as the original stem's claim of a rigorous physical proof.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3455.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"E","contentFingerprint":"b709ef7b3c19da21bdf04ae9de39101afdcad9e43e001a70f82b88a5a1647178"},{"qid":289,"stem":"Which artist worked in the seventeenth century?","choices":["Pietro da Cortona","Leonardo da Vinci","Andrea del Sarto","Giambattista Tiepolo","Paolo Uccello"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Pietro da Cortona was a leading seventeenth-century Baroque artist.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":290,"stem":"In which building can one NOT see a work by Giotto?","choices":["Upper Basilica of Assisi","Santa Croce, Florence","Scrovegni Chapel, Padua","Santa Maria Novella, Florence","San Bernardino, Urbino"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"San Bernardino in Urbino is the exception in the source's list.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":291,"stem":"Which word is synonymous with “homology”?","choices":["Differentiation","Opposition","Generalisation","Discrimination","Correspondence"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Homology refers to a correspondence between structures or features.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":292,"stem":"What is the motto of the House of Savoy?","choices":["Ubi commoda, ibi incommoda","Nei secoli fedele","Avanti!","FERT","Neminem laedere"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"FERT is the traditional motto associated with the House of Savoy.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":293,"stem":"To which period does the Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio, shown here, belong?","choices":["Fifteenth century – Renaissance","Seventeenth century – Baroque","Sixteenth century – Renaissance","Sixteenth century – Baroque","Eighteenth century – Neoclassicism"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Bramante's Tempietto belongs to the early sixteenth-century High Renaissance.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":294,"stem":"Match each artist to a work.\n1. Boccioni\n2. Mondrian\n3. Van Gogh\n4. Ingres\n5. Picasso\na. Harlequin\nb. Composition with Yellow, Blue and Red\nc. Dynamism of a Cyclist\nd. Self-Portrait with Bandaged Ear\ne. The Turkish Bath","choices":["1–b; 2–e; 3–d; 4–c; 5–a","1–c; 2–b; 3–d; 4–e; 5–a","1–a; 2–c; 3–b; 4–e; 5–d","1–c; 2–b; 3–d; 4–a; 5–e","1–a; 2–b; 3–e; 4–c; 5–d"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Boccioni painted the cyclist, Mondrian the geometric composition, Van Gogh the self-portrait, Ingres the bath, and Picasso Harlequin.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":295,"stem":"To which twentieth-century movement does this Red and Blue Chair belong?","choices":["Art Nouveau","Futurism","Rationalism","Neoplasticism","Expressionism"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Rietveld's chair expresses the planes and primary-colour vocabulary of De Stijl, or Neoplasticism.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":296,"stem":"Which is NOT a characteristic of Neoplasticism?","choices":["In painting, elimination of subject matter and natural forms","Use of primary and neutral colours","Absence of perspective","Use of triangular forms, points and cusps","In architecture, planes do not meet in a closed, sharply defined corner"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Neoplasticism emphasises orthogonal lines and rectangular planes rather than triangular forms and cusps.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":297,"stem":"Which term is NOT an element of an Early Christian church?","choices":["Apse","Presbytery","Cardo","Narthex","Nave"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A cardo is a principal north–south street in a Roman town, not a church element.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":298,"stem":"Who appears at the centre of Raphael's School of Athens?","choices":["Christ and the Virgin Mary","Saint Sebastian and Saint Thomas","Plato and Aristotle","Bacchus and Ariadne","Two Venuses"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Plato and Aristotle occupy the central position in Raphael's fresco.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":299,"stem":"Ron Howard's 2001 film A Beautiful Mind concerns John Forbes Nash Jr. In which field did Nash receive the Nobel Memorial Prize in 1994?","choices":["Mathematics","Physics","Chemistry","Medicine","Economics"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Nash received the economics prize for work on non-cooperative game theory.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":300,"stem":"Travelling eastward between which pair of cities would you cross the International Date Line?","choices":["Paris – Beijing","London – Tehran","Buenos Aires – Madrid","San Francisco – Berlin","Tokyo – Vancouver"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"An eastward journey from Tokyo to Vancouver crosses the Pacific and the International Date Line.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":301,"stem":"Which term is the odd one out?","choices":["Broletto (town hall)","Hermitage","Monastery","Convent","Abbey"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A broletto is a civic building; the other terms refer to religious communities or their buildings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":302,"stem":"Which nuclear power plant was damaged, releasing radioactive material, following the tsunami of 11 March 2011?","choices":["Fukushima","Kyshtym","Goiânia","Chernobyl","Windscale"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The tsunami disabled cooling systems at Fukushima Daiichi in Japan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":303,"stem":"Which artistic movement originated before the Second World War?","choices":["Surrealism","Op Art","Spatialism","Art Informel","Hyperrealism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Surrealism emerged in the 1920s; the other movements developed after the war.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":304,"stem":"Complete Article 58 of the Italian Constitution: “Senators are elected by ... suffrage. Voters who have reached their ... year are eligible to be senators.”","choices":["Universal and direct; fortieth","Universal and property-qualified; twenty-fifth","Property-qualified and direct; fortieth","Universal and indirect; fiftieth","Regional and direct; fiftieth"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The article specifies universal direct suffrage and a minimum age of forty for election as senator.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":305,"stem":"Which English mathematician helped the British decode Nazi codes during the Second World War?","choices":["John E. Littlewood","Bertrand A. W. Russell","Godfrey H. Hardy","Alan M. Turing","Harold Jeffreys"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Alan Turing played a central role in wartime codebreaking at Bletchley Park.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":306,"stem":"Which Italian physician is also famous for an educational method bearing her name?","choices":["Rita Levi Montalcini","Maria Montessori","Camillo Golgi","Ferdinando Aiuti","Umberto Veronesi"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Maria Montessori developed a child-centred educational method.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":307,"stem":"Which of these coins was NOT Roman?","choices":["Denarius","As","Sestertius","Drachma","Quadrans"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The drachma was a Greek monetary unit; the others are Roman denominations.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":308,"stem":"Which event occurred during Leonardo da Vinci's lifetime?","choices":["Richard the Lionheart's participation in the Third Crusade","The Thirty Years' War","Columbus's arrival in America","The French Revolution","Dante Alighieri's death"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Leonardo lived from 1452 to 1519; Columbus crossed the Atlantic in 1492.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":309,"stem":"Put these paintings in chronological order.\n1. The Triumph of Bacchus (Velázquez)\n2. Liberty Leading the People (Delacroix)\n3. The Dance (Matisse)\n4. Primavera (Botticelli)\n5. Doni Tondo (Michelangelo)","choices":["4; 5; 2; 1; 3","1; 5; 4; 3; 2","4; 5; 1; 2; 3","5; 4; 2; 1; 3","5; 4; 1; 2; 3"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Botticelli precedes Michelangelo, then Velázquez, Delacroix and Matisse.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":310,"stem":"According to legend, who was suckled by a she-wolf?","choices":["Attila","Remus","Constantine","Shapur","Jupiter"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The twins Romulus and Remus were said to have been suckled by a she-wolf.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":311,"stem":"Match each painter to the technique used to apply colour.\n1. Monet; 2. Jackson Pollock; 3. Van Gogh; 4. Seurat; 5. De Chirico\na. Paint poured and dripped from above\nb. Free, rapid brushstrokes\nc. Flat, even areas of colour\nd. Forceful, thick brushstrokes\ne. Small dots of pure colour","choices":["1–e; 2–c; 3–d; 4–b; 5–a","1–b; 2–a; 3–d; 4–e; 5–c","1–e; 2–b; 3–d; 4–a; 5–c","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–e; 5–d","1–b; 2–c; 3–d; 4–e; 5–a"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Pollock dripped paint, Seurat used dots, Van Gogh thick expressive strokes, and Monet rapid strokes; De Chirico used flatter fields.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":312,"stem":"Who painted The Nude Maja and The Clothed Maja?","choices":["René Magritte","Jacques-Louis David","Francisco Goya","Henri Matisse","Joan Miró"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Both paintings are by Francisco Goya.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":313,"stem":"Put these architectural periods in chronological order.\n1. Ravenna's early Byzantine architecture\n2. Early Christian architecture\n3. Romanesque architecture\n4. Carolingian architecture\n5. Gothic architecture","choices":["2; 1; 3; 5; 4","1; 2; 4; 5; 3","4; 2; 1; 5; 3","1; 3; 4; 2; 5","2; 1; 4; 3; 5"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Early Christian and Ravennate architecture precede Carolingian, Romanesque and Gothic. The printed key gives A, but its placement of Carolingian architecture is incorrect.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":314,"stem":"When the Roman Empire was divided under the Tetrarchy, its imperial residences were:","choices":["Rome, Carthage, Byzantium and Athens","Rome, Milan, Byzantium and Caesarea","Milan, Lyon, Smyrna and Seleucia","Nicomedia, Sirmium, Milan and Trier","Rome, Alexandria, Byzantium and Babylon"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"These four centres served the tetrarchic rulers; Rome was no longer the sole governing centre.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":315,"stem":"The Five Days of Milan belong to which conflict?","choices":["Second World War","First Italian War of Independence","Second Italian War of Independence","Third Italian War of Independence","First World War"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The uprising occurred in March 1848, at the beginning of the First War of Independence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":316,"stem":"René Magritte's style makes extensive use of which representational technique?","choices":["Collage","Frottage","Flocking","Trompe-l'œil","Pointillism"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Magritte used convincing illusionistic representation to create impossible or unsettling situations.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":317,"stem":"Which principle requires an arrested person to be brought promptly before a judge who can examine the detention and order release?","choices":["Habeas corpus","Placet","Dictatus papae","Non expedit","Exequatur"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Habeas corpus provides judicial review of the lawfulness of detention.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":318,"stem":"1978 is remembered as the year of three popes. Which three?","choices":["John Paul I, John Paul II and Benedict XVI","John XXIII, John Paul I and John Paul II","John XXIII, Paul VI and John Paul I","Paul VI, John Paul I and John Paul II","Pius XII, John Paul I and John Paul II"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Paul VI died in 1978; John Paul I briefly succeeded him, followed by John Paul II.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":319,"stem":"What subjects were favoured by the seventeenth-century Bergamasque painter Evaristo Baschenis?","choices":["Still lifes with musical instruments","Still lifes of natural subjects","Taxidermied animals","Animals","Court ladies"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Baschenis is particularly known for still lifes featuring musical instruments.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":320,"stem":"Which artist never worked directly on St Peter's Basilica in Rome?","choices":["Donato Bramante","Leonardo da Vinci","Raphael","Michelangelo","Gian Lorenzo Bernini"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Bramante, Raphael, Michelangelo and Bernini all contributed to the basilica's design or decoration.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":321,"id":"ALPHA-B004-Q321","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"The Creation of Adam","stem":"Identify the fresco from which the pictured detail is taken.","choices":["Camera degli Sposi — Andrea Mantegna","The Creation of Adam — Michelangelo","Primavera — Sandro Botticelli","The Last Judgment — Michelangelo","Stanza della Segnatura — Raphael"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"The two nearly touching hands belong to Michelangelo's Creation of Adam on the Sistine Chapel ceiling. The missing correct title replaces The Deluge.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3458.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"77a09cf87d495a4b69bd9c05b7ad2b558ca2e4b3ca7ac8451dca8dfea869ec66","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3458.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[574,748,899,959]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":325,"height":211},"visualSubject":"The Creation of Adam","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants."},{"qid":322,"stem":"Which is NOT a characteristic feature of Art Nouveau?","choices":["Use of curved lines","Extensive use of iron and cast iron","Widespread floral motifs","Attention to decoration","Priority of structural design over form"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Art Nouveau integrates structure with expressive, often organic decoration rather than subordinating form to structure.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":323,"stem":"Which European capital was NOT occupied by the Nazis during the Second World War?","choices":["Paris","Brussels","Moscow","Rome","Prague"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"German forces approached Moscow but did not occupy it.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":324,"stem":"Which French king made Versailles his favourite residence and promoted the construction of its palace and gardens?","choices":["Francis I","Louis the Pious","Philip the Fair","Louis XIV","Louis XVII"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Louis XIV transformed Versailles into the principal centre of the French royal court.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":325,"stem":"Which Italian irredentist was executed in 1882, the year Garibaldi died?","choices":["Silvio Pellico","Scipio Slataper","Guglielmo Oberdan","Cesare Battisti","Giani Stuparich"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Oberdan was executed by the Austrian authorities in 1882.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":326,"stem":"Who is identified here as the leading exponent of the Roman Baroque?","choices":["Filippo Juvarra","Giuseppe Piermarini","Francesco Borromini","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Andrea Palladio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Bernini's architecture and sculpture were central to the Roman Baroque; Borromini was another major exponent.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":327,"stem":"Which feature is NOT presented here as characteristic of the Baroque?","choices":["Flowing rhythms of forms","Spatial effects within very small spaces","Theatrical devices","Frequent use of precious materials such as marble, gold and silver","Revival and elaboration of classical canons"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source contrasts Baroque movement and theatricality with a classicising emphasis. Baroque artists nevertheless also used classical elements.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":328,"stem":"What causes the succession of seasons at a given place on Earth?","choices":["Earth's rotation about its axis","Changes in Earth's distance from the Sun","The duration of Earth's orbit around the Sun","The tilt of Earth's axis relative to its orbital plane","The place's distance from the equator"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Axial tilt changes the angle and duration of sunlight during Earth's annual orbit.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":329,"stem":"Which feature is NOT characteristic of Expressionism?","choices":["Emphasis on form","Attention to technical and constructional functionality","Attention to visual and communicative function","Intensified colour in painting","Dramatic subject matter"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Expressionism prioritises expressive impact rather than technical functionalism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":330,"stem":"Which artist adopted Rubens's use of a diagonal composition linking opposite corners of the painting?","choices":["Pierre-Auguste Renoir","Claude Monet","Eugène Delacroix","Gustave Courbet","Antonio Canova"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Delacroix used dynamic diagonals, drawing on Rubens's dramatic compositions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":331,"stem":"According to the source, which was NOT used by Andy Warhol as a subject?","choices":["Elvis Presley","Campbell's soup cans","The Coca-Cola logo","Coca-Cola bottles","Sophia Loren"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source identifies Sophia Loren; Elvis and Campbell's and Coca-Cola imagery are established Warhol subjects.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":332,"stem":"Which movement treated time and movement as elements of the artwork?","choices":["Surrealism","Neoplasticism","Futurism","Abstractionism","Expressionism"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Futurism explored speed, simultaneity and movement through time.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":333,"stem":"Why did the Bauhaus cease operating in 1933?","choices":["Lack of funding","Lack of student participation","Too few artists on its teaching staff","Suppression by the Nazi regime, which accused it of harbouring communist intellectuals","The death of its director, Walter Gropius"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Bauhaus closed under Nazi pressure in 1933; Gropius was still alive.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":334,"stem":"Which architect never worked in the United States?","choices":["Frank Lloyd Wright","Ludwig Mies van der Rohe","Antoni Gaudí","Renzo Piano","Richard Meier"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Gaudí's built work is associated with Spain, especially Catalonia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":335,"stem":"Gavrilo Princip's assassination of the heir to the Austrian throne:","choices":["Occurred at the end of the nineteenth century","Was instigated by the Countess of Castiglione to promote Italian unification","Triggered the First World War","Triggered a socialist revolt in Vienna","Was commissioned by his lover Maria Vetsera"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The assassination of Franz Ferdinand in June 1914 precipitated the July Crisis and the outbreak of war.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":336,"stem":"Who founded political Zionism, when did the movement develop, and when and where was the state envisaged by it established?","choices":["The French Jewish journalist Bernard Lazare founded it in France in 1894 after the Dreyfus affair; an Israeli state was established in Egypt in 1967 during the Six-Day War.","It had no founder and arose after the Nazi extermination of the Jews; Israel was founded in 1948 to save survivors.","Primo Levi founded it in Italy in 1938 after Fascist racial laws; his plan for a Jewish state was realised in 1948.","The Hungarian journalist and writer Theodor Herzl published The Jewish State in 1896 and convened the first Zionist Congress in Basel in 1897; Israel was proclaimed in Palestine in May 1948.","None of the other answers is correct."],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Herzl organised modern political Zionism in the late nineteenth century; Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":337,"stem":"Which Italian word can mean both a commercial document and an act of witchcraft?","choices":["Fattura","Bolla","Sortilegio","Incantesimo","Nota"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Fattura can mean an invoice or a spell; the language-specific ambiguity is retained.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":338,"stem":"Which extract from the Italian Constitution is quoted incorrectly?","choices":["The public prosecutor has the duty to initiate criminal proceedings (Article 112).","All judicial decisions must state their reasons (Article 111, paragraph 6).","Justice is administered in the name of the people. Judges are subject only to Parliament (Article 101).","The judicial authorities have direct access to the judicial police (Article 109).","Judges cannot be removed from office (Article 107, paragraph 1)."],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Article 101 says judges are subject only to the law, not to Parliament.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":339,"stem":"Which of these late-twentieth-century US presidents completed two consecutive terms?","choices":["Richard Nixon","John Fitzgerald Kennedy","Bill Clinton","Jimmy Carter","George Herbert Walker Bush"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Clinton served from 1993 to 2001. Nixon won re-election but resigned during his second term.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":340,"stem":"Who painted the Portrait of Agnolo Doni?","choices":["Michelangelo","Jacopo Tintoretto","Pietro da Cortona","Raphael","Leonardo da Vinci"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Raphael painted the paired portraits of Agnolo and Maddalena Doni.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":341,"stem":"Match the museums to their cities.\na. MART; b. Victoria and Albert Museum; c. Capitoline Museums; d. Guggenheim Museum; e. MoMA\n1. Rovereto; 2. Rome; 3. London; 4. New York; 5. Bilbao","choices":[" a–3; b–2; c–4; d–1; e–5","a–1; b–3; c–2; d–5; e–4","a–5; b–4; c–3; d–1; e–2","a–4; b–1; c–5; d–2; e–3","a–2; b–3; c–4; d–1; e–5"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"MART is in Rovereto, the V&A in London, the Capitoline Museums in Rome, this Guggenheim in Bilbao, and MoMA in New York.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":342,"stem":"Which work is by Niccolò Machiavelli?","choices":["On Crimes and Punishments","On the Duties of Men","The Prince","On the Prince and Letters","Dialogue on Nobility"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Machiavelli wrote The Prince, a treatise on political power.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":343,"stem":"Which architect is especially known for bridges, communication towers and station and airport canopies?","choices":["Ron Arad","Santiago Calatrava","Gae Aulenti","Norman Foster","Frank O. Gehry"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Calatrava combines architecture and structural engineering in expressive infrastructure projects.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":344,"stem":"Which is NOT a work by Gian Lorenzo Bernini?","choices":["St Peter's Baldachin","Sant'Andrea al Quirinale","Palazzo Montecitorio","Palazzo Odescalchi","San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"San Carlo alle Quattro Fontane is by Francesco Borromini.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":345,"stem":"Which term does NOT refer to an arch structure?","choices":["Voussoir","Architrave","Keystone","Pier","Intrados"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"An architrave is a horizontal beam; the other terms identify parts or supports of an arch.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":346,"stem":"Who completed the architectural treatise De re aedificatoria in 1452, defining harmonious proportion?","choices":["Filippo Brunelleschi","Benvenuto Cellini","Donato Bramante","Leon Battista Alberti","Andrea Palladio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Alberti's treatise systematically discusses architecture and proportion.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":347,"stem":"In four of these Italian words, “-teca” has the same meaning. Which is different?","choices":["Emeroteca","Pinacoteca","Biblioteca","Fonoteca","Zapoteca"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The first four denote collections or repositories; Zapoteca refers to the Zapotec people or language.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":348,"stem":"After the Second World War, the United States strengthened its links with Western Europe through the Marshall Plan. What was it?","choices":["A plan to extend US influence into the Soviet Union","A motorway-building programme from northern Europe to Sicily","A plan to divide Europe into spheres of influence","An economic aid programme for European economies","An anti-Soviet defence project"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Marshall Plan funded European economic recovery after the war.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":349,"id":"ALPHA-B004-Q349","major":"Architecture","era":"Modern","label":"Chronology of five photographed buildings","stem":"Put the pictured buildings in chronological order: a. Eiffel Tower; b. Chrysler Building; c. Crystal Palace; d. Casa Milà; e. Fallingwater.","choices":["a; b; c; d; e","a; d; b; c; e","c; d; e; a; b","c; a; d; b; e","d; b; a; c; e"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":null,"explanation":"Crystal Palace dates to 1851, Eiffel Tower to 1889, Casa Milà to 1906–1912, Chrysler Building to 1930 and Fallingwater to 1936–1939. The source's sequence is corrected.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","sourceImage":"IMG_3461.jpeg","examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Printed source key and factual review; corrected answer is separately recorded when the source is defective.","correctionNote":"Original wording or choices corrected to ensure a single defensible answer. The original photograph remains the internal source reference.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"2325d7e40e16176a112eca7542749777b0ab12b799a8970fe72bd1359b316b2a","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3461.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[667,140,784,303],[843,153,1030,298],[660,326,969,470],[657,476,895,635],[657,638,950,793]],"method":"labelled_source_crop_composite","essential":true,"width":930,"height":600},"visualSubject":"Chronology of five photographed buildings","visualConcept":"Visual identification or ordering","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a verified public-domain/licensed reproduction or an original labelled schematic for generated variants."},{"qid":350,"stem":"Put the sculptures shown in chronological order.","choices":["c; a; b; e; d","a; b; c; d; e","c; d; b; e; a","b; a; c; e; d","b; d; c; e; a"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Rodin's Thinker precedes Boccioni's 1913 figure, Moore's reclining figure, Picasso's 1967 Chicago sculpture and Moore's Nuclear Energy, unveiled later in 1967.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B004-Q350","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3461.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[680,1027,775,1145],[788,1027,885,1145],[896,1090,1036,1148],[748,1183,853,1302],[855,1184,954,1304]],"method":"clean_labelled_visual_tiles","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3461.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","correctionNote":"Choice A restored to five source references.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"a14df5d0bb7800f4c385972075edb1f661a4c2ed0fcdc347cd838049aa2c5aa9"},{"qid":351,"stem":"Match the architectural representations from left to right.","choices":["Elevation; section; plan","Section; plan; elevation","Plan; elevation; section","Elevation; elevation; section","Section; elevation; plan"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"An elevation shows a face, a section cuts through the building, and a plan shows its horizontal layout.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":352,"stem":"Match the numbered parts of the classical order to their names.","choices":["1 – pediment; 2 – frieze; 3 – architrave; 4 – shaft; 5 – stylobate","1 – architrave; 2 – pediment; 3 – shaft; 4 – stylobate; 5 – frieze","1 – shaft; 2 – stylobate; 3 – frieze; 4 – pediment; 5 – architrave","1 – pediment; 2 – stylobate; 3 – architrave; 4 – shaft; 5 – frieze","1 – architrave; 2 – stylobate; 3 – frieze; 4 – shaft; 5 – pediment"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The frieze sits above the architrave; the column shaft rises from the stylobate.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"original_diagram_and_all_five_numbers_visually_checked_v84","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B004-Q352","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3464.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[],"method":"original_photo_clipped_diagram","essential":true,"cropRecipe":"crop_source84.py","numberedLabelsRetained":[1,2,3,4,5]},"sourceImage":"IMG_3464.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"a5cdf1a78429e788b90f75578b1e1850e0e390fc399a495046f14bea1cb85458"},{"qid":353,"stem":"What was Antonio Ligabue's favourite pictorial subject?","choices":["Crowds","Animals","Flowers","Houses","Dancers"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Ligabue is especially known for expressive animal paintings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":354,"stem":"Theo van Doesburg's journal De Stijl, founded in 1917, is associated with which movement?","choices":["Surrealism","Dadaism","Neoplasticism","Minimalism","Mannerism"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"De Stijl promoted the geometric abstraction of Neoplasticism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":355,"stem":"What is a particularly evident characteristic of Flemish painting?","choices":["Gold backgrounds","Expressive figures","Perspective","Cobalt-blue backgrounds","A detailed observation of reality"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Flemish painting is noted for meticulously rendered surfaces, objects and visual detail.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":356,"stem":"Which characteristic is NOT associated with Dadaism?","choices":["Irony","Chance","Reuse of everyday objects","Rigour","Provocation"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Dada challenged conventional order through chance, irony and provocative use of ordinary objects.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":357,"stem":"Which characteristic best describes Neoplasticism?","choices":["Irony","Chance","Reuse of everyday objects","Mathematical rigour and balance","Provocation"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Neoplasticism seeks balanced geometric relationships, especially through orthogonal lines and primary colours.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":358,"stem":"According to this source, which element receives fundamental attention when designing new cultural buildings for heritage protection and social activities?","choices":["Aesthetics","Lighting design","Functionality","Historical preservation of existing spaces","Use of innovative materials"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The source highlights lighting, which affects the presentation and conservation of exhibits. This is a source-specific emphasis, not a universal hierarchy of design priorities.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":359,"stem":"For which event was the Eiffel Tower built?","choices":["The 1889 World's Fair","The Queen of England's visit in 1852","The fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989","The Paris Commune in 1871","The death of Louis XIV in 1715"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The tower was built for the 1889 Exposition Universelle in Paris.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":360,"stem":"Who founded the architecture magazine Domus in 1928?","choices":["Giuseppe Terragni","Bruno Munari","Enzo Mari","Angelo Mangiarotti","Gio Ponti"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Gio Ponti founded Domus in 1928.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":361,"stem":"Turner, Friedrich, Delacroix and Hayez are associated with which movement?","choices":["Neoclassicism","Impressionism","Expressionism","Romanticism","Fauvism"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"These painters belong to Romanticism, with its emphasis on emotion, imagination and dramatic experience.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":362,"stem":"According to Surrealism, what should an artwork express?","choices":["Free relationships of forms and colours","The fragmentation and volumetric synthesis of an object","The symbolic value of represented figures and objects","The actual functioning of thought through pure psychic automatism","The simultaneity of movement"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Surrealism sought access to thought and the unconscious beyond rational control.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":363,"stem":"Who made Cupid and Psyche, shown here?","choices":["Antonio Canova","Michelangelo","Myron","Auguste Rodin","Phidias"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Canova created Neoclassical sculptures of Cupid and Psyche, including this standing group.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":364,"stem":"Who co-founded the Expressionist group Der Blaue Reiter (The Blue Rider)?","choices":["Kandinsky","Klee","Mondrian","Chagall","Grosz"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Wassily Kandinsky co-founded the group with Franz Marc.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":365,"stem":"Match each work to its patron.\n1. Colosseum; 2. St Peter's Baldachin; 3. Palazzo Te; 4. Palace of Versailles\na. Urban VIII; b. Vespasian; c. Federico II Gonzaga; d. Louis XIV","choices":["1–b; 2–a; 3–d; 4–c","1–b; 2–d; 3–c; 4–a","1–c; 2–a; 3–b; 4–d","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d","1–a; 2–b; 3–c; 4–d"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Vespasian initiated the Colosseum; Urban VIII commissioned the baldachin, Federico II Gonzaga Palazzo Te, and Louis XIV the enlargement of Versailles. A duplicate distractor is replaced.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":365,"stem":"Match each work to its patron.\n1. Colosseum; 2. St Peter's Baldachin; 3. Palazzo Te; 4. Palace of Versailles\na. Urban VIII; b. Vespasian; c. Federico II Gonzaga; d. Louis XIV","choices":["1–b; 2–a; 3–d; 4–c","1–b; 2–d; 3–c; 4–a","1–c; 2–a; 3–b; 4–d","1–b; 2–a; 3–c; 4–d","1–c; 2–a; 3–b; 4–d"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Vespasian initiated the Colosseum; Urban VIII commissioned the baldachin, Federico Gonzaga Palazzo Te, and Louis XIV the expansion of Versailles.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B004-Q365","major":"Architecture","label":"Works and patrons","era":"Early modern","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3465.jpeg","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"7e0bdc7897d351d7d965ee8facace35017f8bdee937fb39e6b3c36f386618fd8"},{"qid":366,"stem":"Why is Ferrara's Palazzo dei Diamanti so named?","choices":["The square where it stands","Its designer","The rusticated stonework of its external walls","Precious stones kept inside","Its patrons"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Its exterior is covered with diamond-pointed rustication.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":367,"stem":"In which year was the Compasso d'Oro design award established?","choices":["1911","1928","1954","1971","1988"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Compasso d'Oro was established in 1954.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":368,"stem":"Following a victory over a Saracen fleet, Pisa began rebuilding its cathedral in which century?","choices":["Seventh","Tenth","Eleventh","Fifteenth","Sixteenth"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Construction of Pisa Cathedral began in 1064, in the eleventh century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":369,"stem":"What are the reinforcing members dividing a ribbed groin vault into compartments called?","choices":["Lunettes","Conches","Spindles","Ribs","Groins"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The projecting ribs define and support the vault's compartments.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":370,"stem":"What is each of the four compartments of a groin vault called in Italian?","choices":["Vela","Padiglione","Cilindretto","Cresta","Lunetta"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Vela denotes one of the vault's web-like compartments.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":371,"stem":"A temple is described as “in antis” when:","choices":["The cella's side walls extend forward to enclose the lateral limits of the porch and its columns","It has a colonnade at the front","It has colonnades at both front and rear","A colonnade surrounds all four sides of the cella","A double colonnade surrounds all four sides of the cella"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Columns stand between the projecting ends, or antae, of the cella walls.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":372,"stem":"What does “devolution” mean in political language?","choices":["Transfer of powers from central government to regional or local government","Reduction of taxation during severe economic crises","An ideology favouring extensive state intervention in all sectors","A policy primarily considering two major parties or states","Formation of composite parliamentary majorities"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Devolution decentralises powers to regional or local institutions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":373,"stem":"Which act does NOT require an absolute or other qualified majority under the Italian Constitution?","choices":["Adopting constitutional laws","Adopting amnesty and pardon laws","Converting decree-laws into law","Electing the President of the Republic","Authorising borrowing under Article 81 beyond the balanced-budget requirement"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Conversion of a decree-law is ordinary legislation. Constitutional laws, amnesty or pardon, presidential election and the specified borrowing authorization have constitutional majority requirements.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":373,"stem":"According to the printed source, which parliamentary act does NOT require a qualified majority in the Italian Chamber and Senate?","choices":["Adopting constitutional laws","Adopting amnesty and pardon laws","Converting decree-laws into law","Electing the President of the Republic","Adopting the budget law"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The printed key selects conversion of decree-laws. The question is ambiguous: ordinary budget legislation also does not generally require a qualified majority; special authorisations to borrow are a separate matter.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","verificationSources":["https://www.senato.it/show-doc?id=982075&leg=17&part=dossier_dossier1-sezione_sezione8-h4_h41&tipodoc=DOSSIER"],"id":"ALPHA-B005-Q373","major":"Geography & Civics","label":"Italian Senate qualified-majority cases","era":"Early modern","examLanguages":["it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3466.jpeg","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"444b025deb291d83c52b2d4bcb68147d2250c95c9e6af8a6e2c7426b4d2947c5"},{"qid":374,"stem":"In which century was the First Peloponnesian War fought?","choices":["First century CE","First century BCE","Second century BCE","Third century BCE","Fifth century BCE"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The First Peloponnesian War occurred in the fifth century BCE.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":375,"stem":"What does the illustration represent?","choices":["An obelisk","A pyramid","A ziggurat","A town hall","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A ziggurat is a stepped temple platform of ancient Mesopotamia.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":376,"stem":"What is a “flat tax”?","choices":["A very low tax applying only to poorer groups","A progressive tax system with three rates and no deductions or credits","A non-progressive tax system based on a fixed rate","An income tax close to 0%, combined with 25% VAT","A 15% tax replacing VAT and income tax, together with abolition of the IRA"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A flat tax applies one nominal rate rather than increasing marginal rates.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":377,"stem":"The prisoner's dilemma is a well-known example in which field?","choices":["Chaos theory","Asymptotic theory","Game theory","Network theory","Quantum theory"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"It illustrates how individually rational choices can produce a worse collective outcome.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":378,"stem":"Syracuse was a colony of:","choices":["Athens","Corinth","Sparta","Argos","Thebes"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Greek settlers from Corinth founded Syracuse in Sicily.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":379,"stem":"Which is a type of Egyptian tomb?","choices":["Groma","Gnomon","Mastaba","Rambla","Cavea"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A mastaba is a flat-roofed tomb with sloping sides.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":380,"stem":"What architectural feature is shown?","choices":["A three-light window","A splayed window","A pilaster","A Serlian window","An aedicule"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A serliana has an arched central opening flanked by lower rectangular openings.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":381,"stem":"Who designed the Casa del Fascio in Como?","choices":["Ignazio Gardella","Giuseppe Pagano","Aldo Rossi","Giuseppe Terragni","Franco Albini"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Terragni designed this landmark of Italian Rationalism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":382,"stem":"Which listed museum houses a version of Leonardo da Vinci's Virgin of the Rocks?","choices":["The refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan","The Louvre, Paris","The Uffizi, Florence","The Vatican Museums, Rome","The Basilica of Santo Spirito, Florence"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"One version is in the Louvre; another is in London's National Gallery.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":383,"stem":"Which building has the plan shown?","choices":["Petit Trianon at Versailles","Villa Rotonda","Crystal Palace","University of Virginia Rotunda","Temple of Minerva Medica"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Palladio's Villa Rotonda has a central circular hall and four porticoes.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":384,"stem":"Which work in Rome is NOT attributed to Donato Bramante?","choices":["Cloister of Santa Maria della Pace","Villa Farnesina","Belvedere Courtyard","Tempietto of San Pietro in Montorio","St Peter's Basilica"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Villa Farnesina was designed by Baldassare Peruzzi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":385,"stem":"Put the buildings shown in chronological order.","choices":["A; C; B; D; E","B; C; A; E; D","A; B; E; D; C","B; A; C; E; D","B; A; E; C; D"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The late-19th-century building B precedes A (1929), E (mid-20th century), C and the later D.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B005-Q385","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3467.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[725,351,875,446],[880,351,983,446],[701,457,818,530],[846,457,924,530],[934,457,1055,530]],"method":"clean_labelled_visual_tiles","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3467.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"E","contentFingerprint":"9a45039d1bfaad2cd7edf7f56d33307320eb5ff935fa04a6c4624f50bf058b82"},{"qid":386,"stem":"What is a transept in a church?","choices":["The central passage along the nave","The passage behind the high altar","A space crossing the nave at right angles","A room before the entrance","The system of side chapels"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The transept forms the transverse arm of a cruciform church.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":387,"stem":"Who is the odd one out?","choices":["Le Corbusier","Giuseppe Terragni","Walter Gropius","Alessandro Antonelli","Ludwig Mies van der Rohe"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Antonelli was a nineteenth-century architect; the others are associated with twentieth-century Modernism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":388,"stem":"Who painted the Life of St John the Baptist and Life of St Francis fresco cycles in Florence's Peruzzi and Bardi chapels at Santa Croce?","choices":["Domenico Ghirlandaio","Fra Angelico","Ambrogio Lorenzetti","Cimabue","Giotto"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Giotto painted these major narrative fresco cycles at Santa Croce.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":389,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Fausto Melotti","Lucio Fontana","Alberto Burri","Giorgio de Chirico","Piero Manzoni"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Fontana punctured and cut surfaces as part of his Spatialist exploration of real space.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":390,"stem":"Which is NOT a twentieth-century artistic avant-garde?","choices":["Expressionism","Eclecticism","De Stijl","Die Brücke","Constructivism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Eclecticism combines styles from different sources and is especially associated with nineteenth-century architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":391,"stem":"Which person worked for a time as a mathematics teacher?","choices":["Giuseppe Garibaldi","Alessandro Manzoni","Giuseppe Mazzini","Carlo Cattaneo","Melchiorre Gioia"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Garibaldi taught mathematics during his time in South America.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":392,"stem":"Near which city is CERN located?","choices":["Geneva","Strasbourg","Zurich","Basel","Bern"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"CERN is near Geneva on the Franco-Swiss border. The printed answer E (Bern) is incorrect.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":393,"stem":"Who presented evolutionary theory in On the Origin of Species?","choices":["Johann Gregor Mendel","Epicurus","Charles Robert Darwin","Isaac Newton","Dmitri Ivanovich Mendeleev"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":394,"stem":"Where did the suffragette movement demanding women's voting rights originate?","choices":["Canada","United Kingdom","France","United States","Germany"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The term suffragette is particularly associated with the militant British women's suffrage movement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":395,"stem":"What is the film The Via Panisperna Boys, set in the 1930s, about?","choices":["A group of young physicists and mathematicians","A group of young doctors","A group of young dancers","A group of young artists","A group of young anti-Fascist writers"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"It concerns Enrico Fermi and the group of physicists working in Rome.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":396,"stem":"Which work is NOT by Luigi Pirandello?","choices":["The Jar","Les Misérables","Right You Are (If You Think So)","Six Characters in Search of an Author","Liolà"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Les Misérables is by Victor Hugo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":397,"stem":"Which film was directed by Pier Paolo Pasolini?","choices":["Rome, Open City","Germany, Year Zero","La ricotta","The Rubber Wall","I vitelloni"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"La ricotta is Pasolini's contribution to the anthology film Ro.Go.Pa.G.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":398,"stem":"What is outsourcing?","choices":["Financialisation of a company's activities","Securitisation of receivables","Use of occasional labour","Transfer of previously internal activities to external companies","Heavy reliance on borrowing to finance production"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Outsourcing contracts work to an external provider.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":399,"stem":"In art, what are “grotesques”?","choices":["Underground rooms imitating caves","Outdoor spaces created in parks","Roman pools and fountains intended for nymphs","A pictorial decorative subject","Unreal, usually humorous sculptures"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Grotesque decoration combines fantastic figures, foliage and other ornamental motifs.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":400,"stem":"In which century did the genocide of Assyrian Christians occur?","choices":["Second century BCE","Sixteenth century","Twentieth century","Second century CE","Fourth century BCE"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Assyrian genocide occurred during the First World War.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":401,"stem":"Which battle was NOT fought in the American Civil War?","choices":["Bull Run","Lexington","Chancellorsville","Fredericksburg","Appomattox"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The question refers to Lexington in 1775, associated with the American Revolution, rather than the later Civil War engagements sharing that name.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":402,"stem":"Put the buildings shown in chronological order.","choices":["b; a; c; e; d","a; b; e; d; c","b; c; a; e; d","c; b; a; d; e","a; b; d; e; c"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The sequence goes from earlier B through modern A, then C, the Pompidou Centre E and later D.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B005-Q402","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3469.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[240,578,377,679],[418,580,551,681],[241,703,377,794],[418,704,558,793],[242,812,377,919]],"method":"clean_labelled_visual_tiles","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3469.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"5b15ba9a72c9800b0b8ffe3fb8771eefcd54b3afb7e4f26863ebce00c646a9b5"},{"qid":403,"stem":"Which was NOT a cavalry force in nineteenth-century armies?","choices":["Uhlans","Hussars","Grenadiers","Dragoons","Cossacks"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Grenadiers were infantry; the other terms are associated with mounted troops.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":404,"stem":"Which battle was NOT fought in the American War of Independence?","choices":["Bunker Hill","Gettysburg","Long Island","Fort Washington","Trenton"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Gettysburg was fought in 1863 during the American Civil War.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":405,"stem":"Identify the organisation described in this source passage.\n“Founded in Rome in 1977, the association soon recruited members across Italy, opened local branches and protested against animal experiments, zoos, animal circuses, fur clothing and hunting. Recognised by Italy's Ministry of Health as an organisation protecting interests harmed by crimes against animals, it has 56 local branches and thousands of members.”","choices":["Federazione Italiana Associazioni Diritti Animali e Ambiente","Ente Nazionale Protezione Animali","Federazione Nazionale Pro Natura","Lega Anti Vivisezione","Lega Italiana Difesa Ambiente e Animali"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"LAV stands for Lega Anti Vivisezione. The membership and branch figures belong to the quoted source, not a current count.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":406,"stem":"What is Constantinople, capital of the Eastern Roman Empire, called today?","choices":["Damascus","Beirut","Baghdad","Istanbul","Tehran"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Constantinople is now Istanbul.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":407,"stem":"On which date is Italy's Day of Legality held to commemorate the Capaci and Via D'Amelio bombings?","choices":["27 January","25 March","22 April","23 May","4 November"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"23 May is the anniversary of the 1992 Capaci bombing.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":408,"stem":"Which car manufacturer is associated with introducing the moving assembly line?","choices":["Buick","Peugeot","Ford","Aston Martin","Cadillac"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Ford introduced the moving assembly line into automobile mass production in 1913.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":409,"stem":"Who was the first woman elected President of the Italian Senate?","choices":["Nilde Iotti","Emma Bonino","Maria Elisabetta Casellati","Laura Boldrini","Irene Pivetti"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Maria Elisabetta Casellati became President of the Senate in 2018.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":410,"stem":"Which country fielded soldiers known as winged hussars?","choices":["Poland","Russia","Austria","Hungary","Italy"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Winged hussars were an elite cavalry formation of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":411,"stem":"What was the Chicago School?","choices":["An architectural movement designing tall buildings supported by metal frames","The most important US public building from Roosevelt's presidency","The first US architecture faculty, opened after the Civil War","The university where the most important American architects trained","A Chicago fine-arts academy spreading European eclecticism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Chicago School helped develop the metal-framed skyscraper.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":412,"stem":"Match each artist to his birthplace.\n1. Dürer; 2. Rembrandt; 3. Van Eyck\na. Leiden; b. Nuremberg; c. Maaseik","choices":["1b; 2a; 3c","1b; 2c; 3a","1a; 2b; 3c","1c; 2a; 3b","1c; 2b; 3a"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Dürer was born in Nuremberg, Rembrandt in Leiden, and Van Eyck is traditionally associated with Maaseik.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":413,"stem":"What was Roman centuriation?","choices":["Formation of units of 100 armed men","Paving imperial roads","Increasing the height of urban defences","Dividing agricultural land into equal square plots","Marking a territory with cardines and decumani through artificial canals"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Centuriation organised surveyed land into a regular grid of agricultural plots.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":414,"stem":"Which Italian architectural term does NOT describe an arch?","choices":["Cieco (blind)","Sghembo (skew)","A schifo (cloister-vaulted)","Lobato (lobed)","Scemo (segmental)"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A schifo describes a type of vault rather than an arch.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":415,"stem":"Which institution is correctly paired with its headquarters?","choices":["Presidency of the Republic – Farnesina","Foreign Ministry – Palazzo Madama","Chamber of Deputies – Quirinale","Interior Ministry – Viminale","Senate – Montecitorio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Italian Interior Ministry is housed in the Viminale Palace.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":416,"stem":"Who painted the work shown?","choices":["Henri Matisse","Paul Gauguin","Paul Cézanne","Vincent van Gogh","Claude Monet"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"This is Monet's Poppy Field, an Impressionist landscape.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":417,"stem":"Who is the odd one out?","choices":["Washington","Jefferson","Adams","Franklin","Nelson"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The first four are American Founding Fathers; Horatio Nelson was a British admiral.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":418,"stem":"Which feature did Palladio add when redesigning Vicenza's Palazzo della Ragione as the Basilica?","choices":["A new function as a religious building","An inverted ship-hull roof","The building's monumental size","Loggias with Serlian openings","Division into three naves"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Palladio surrounded the existing civic building with two-storey loggias using serlianas.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":419,"stem":"Which city's name means “new city”?","choices":["Mantua","Naples","Sulmona","Crotone","Populonia"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Naples derives from the Greek Neapolis, meaning new city.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":420,"stem":"Who was the Roman god of doorways?","choices":["Apollo","Janus","Aius Locutius","Mercury","Hades"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Janus presided over doors, passages and beginnings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":421,"stem":"Which illustrated product was NOT designed jointly by Marco Zanuso and Richard Sapper?","choices":["1","2","3","4","5"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Lettera 22 typewriter was designed by Marcello Nizzoli, unlike the illustrated Zanuso–Sapper products.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":422,"stem":"Order these buildings by the start of their construction.\na. Torre Velasca, Milan\nb. Uffizi, Florence\nc. Milan Cathedral\nd. Sagrada Família, Barcelona\ne. San Vitale, Ravenna","choices":["e; b; c; d; a","c; e; b; a; d","e; b; d; c; a","e; c; b; d; a","c; e; b; d; a"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"San Vitale precedes Milan Cathedral, the Uffizi, the Sagrada Família and the twentieth-century Torre Velasca.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":423,"stem":"D'Annunzio led the occupation of Fiume in 1919. In which country is the city now located?","choices":["Albania","Croatia","Italy","Montenegro","Slovenia"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Fiume is now Rijeka, Croatia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":424,"stem":"What is the narrative technique that freely represents thoughts as they arise in a person's mind?","choices":["Flashback","Stream of consciousness","Cinematic techniques","Flashforward","Serialism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Stream of consciousness follows the flow of a character's inner experience.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":425,"stem":"“Rome, 18 November. Fine weather has returned: a clear day, pleasantly warm. At the Farnesina I saw the story of Psyche, whose colour reproductions have long brightened my rooms; then Raphael's Transfiguration at San Pietro in Montorio. All old acquaintances, almost friendships, formed at a distance through correspondence, which we now see in person.”\nWhat type of text is this extract from?","choices":["A newspaper report","A sonnet","A travel diary","An epic poem","A historical novel"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The dated first-person record of visits and impressions is characteristic of a travel diary.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":426,"stem":"Which is the odd one out?","choices":["Apology of Socrates","Logic","Metaphysics","Physics","Ethics"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Apology is a specific work by Plato; the other terms are major areas associated with Aristotle's writings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":427,"stem":"Who addressed the United Nations on 12 July 2013 to demand the right to education?","choices":["Aung San Suu Kyi","Benazir Bhutto","Kofi Annan","Malala Yousafzai","Barack Obama"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Malala delivered the address on her sixteenth birthday.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":428,"stem":"Which archipelago–country pairing is incorrect?","choices":["Azores – Portugal","Cape Verde – Brazil","Canary Islands – Spain","Galápagos – Ecuador","Hawaii – United States"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Cape Verde is an independent country, not part of Brazil.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":429,"stem":"A 2022 newspaper report says the European Commission reduced its GDP-growth forecasts for the EU and euro area to 2.7% in 2022 and 2.3% in 2023, and Italy's to 2.4% and 1.9%. What is GDP?","choices":["The value of a country's industrial output during a period","The combined value of labour costs in a country during a period","The increase in industrial production during a period","The value of goods and services produced within a country during a period","The expected increase in a country's well-being during a period"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"GDP measures the value of final goods and services produced domestically; it is not limited to industry.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":430,"stem":"What is Italy's SPID service used for?","choices":["Only businesses can access participating public and private online services","Access to a well-known online shopping service","Only public employees can access government online services","Citizens and businesses access participating public and private online services with a single digital identity","Only citizens contacting the tax agency or INPS can use it"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"SPID is Italy's public digital-identity system for accessing participating services.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":431,"stem":"What happens when an email recipient is entered in Bcc?","choices":["All recipients except Bcc recipients receive no attachments","Bcc recipients receive a copy but their addresses are hidden from other recipients","Every recipient must confirm receipt","Bcc recipients receive no attachments","The message is more likely to enter recipients' spam folders"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Bcc means blind carbon copy; it hides those recipient addresses from other recipients.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":432,"stem":"Where were the Wars of the Roses fought?","choices":["France","Germany","Spain","England","Italy"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The conflict involved the English houses of Lancaster and York.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":433,"stem":"Trajan's Column commemorates Roman wars against whom?","choices":["Dacians","Numidians","Greeks","Thracians","Carthaginians"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Its spiral relief narrates Trajan's Dacian campaigns.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":434,"stem":"According to the source, the baptistery's architectural type derives from which model?","choices":["A thermal caldarium","A mausoleum","A circular-plan temple","A water cistern","A Roman basilica"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source points to the circular temple as a central-plan precedent; baptisteries also share forms with other Roman centralised buildings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":435,"stem":"Which work was NOT designed by Michelangelo?","choices":["Dome of St Peter's","Laurentian Library, Florence","Porta Pia, Rome","Palazzo della Signoria, Florence","New Sacristy of San Lorenzo, Florence"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Florence's Palazzo della Signoria, or Palazzo Vecchio, is associated with Arnolfo di Cambio.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":436,"stem":"Which designer devoted especially extensive work to childhood?","choices":["Marco Zanuso","Bruno Munari","Giorgetto Giugiaro","Sergio Pininfarina","Ron Arad"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Munari created books, games and educational activities exploring children's creativity.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":437,"stem":"Which pair is most comparable stylistically and culturally?","choices":["Borromini and Petrarch","Borromini and Pietro da Cortona","Borromini and Puccini","Borromini and Mantegna","Borromini and Pirandello"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Borromini and Pietro da Cortona both worked in the seventeenth-century Baroque context.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":438,"stem":"Which currency–country pairing is incorrect in the source's context?","choices":["Peso – Argentina","Baht – Thailand","Rand – South Africa","Rupee – India","Euro – Hungary"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Hungary uses the forint, not the euro.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":439,"stem":"Which structure was NOT built in the twentieth century?","choices":["Eiffel Tower, Paris","Unité d'Habitation, Marseille","Empire State Building, New York","Torre Velasca, Milan","Centre Pompidou, Paris"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Eiffel Tower opened in 1889, in the nineteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":440,"stem":"Which Italian word means both a converging optical system and a goal to be achieved?","choices":["Traguardo","Obiettivo","Microscopio","Bersaglio","Proiettore"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Obiettivo can mean a lens or an objective/goal.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":441,"stem":"According to the source, post-unification nineteenth-century Italian architecture is characterised by:","choices":["A return to Mannerism","A return to Classicism","A return to the Baroque","The triumph of Art Nouveau","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The source emphasises classicising architectural language; the period also included other historicist styles.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":442,"stem":"What is the canon of classical Greek architecture?","choices":["A constant iconographic motif","A uniformly repeated image","A system of proportions between parts and between the parts and the whole","The fixed nature of an image","Realism"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A canon regulates proportional relationships rather than prescribing one repeated image.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":443,"stem":"In ancient theatre architecture, what is the orchestra?","choices":["The stage on which actors perform","The fixed architectural structure beyond the proscenium","A group of musicians","A circular space at the foot of the cavea","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The orchestra is the performance area below the seating, especially associated with the chorus.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":444,"stem":"Put these twentieth-century events in chronological order.\n1. Russian Revolution\n2. Sarajevo assassination\n3. Normandy landings\n4. Nuremberg trials\n5. March on Rome","choices":["2; 1; 4; 3; 5","1; 2; 5; 3; 4","1; 5; 2; 4; 3","5; 2; 1; 3; 4","2; 1; 5; 3; 4"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The dates are 1914, 1917, 1922, 1944 and 1945–46.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":445,"stem":"Who wrote the article “We Metaphysicians” in 1919?","choices":["Giacomo Balla","Umberto Boccioni","Pablo Picasso","Giorgio de Chirico","Gabriele D'Annunzio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"De Chirico developed and explained Metaphysical painting.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":446,"stem":"In which year did the French Revolution begin?","choices":["1776","1789","1793","1799","1800"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Revolution began in 1789, the year of the Estates-General and the storming of the Bastille.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":447,"stem":"In which city did Palladio carry out much of his work?","choices":["Verona","Treviso","Belluno","Vicenza","Padua"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Vicenza contains many of Palladio's principal urban buildings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":448,"stem":"What was garum in ancient Rome?","choices":["A weapon","A garment","A chariot","A poetic composition","A food"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Garum was a fermented fish sauce used as a condiment.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":449,"stem":"What is shown in the image?","choices":["Etruscan vase painting","A Persian frieze","Greek tomb painting","A Pompeian fresco","A Roman mosaic"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The figures belong to Persian glazed-brick architectural decoration.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":450,"stem":"Which Roman writer died during the eruption of Vesuvius in 79 CE?","choices":["Pliny the Younger","Seneca","Pliny the Elder","Suetonius","Livy"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Pliny the Elder died during the eruption; his nephew Pliny the Younger described it.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":451,"stem":"Against whom was the League of Cambrai, promoted by Julius II in 1508, directed?","choices":["England","The Holy Roman Empire","Spain","The Republic of Venice","France"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The alliance sought to curb Venetian power.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":452,"stem":"Which power defeated the Spanish Armada in 1588?","choices":["The Netherlands","England","Portugal","France","Sweden"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Armada's campaign against England failed in 1588.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":453,"stem":"What happened during the St Bartholomew's Day massacre in France on 23–24 August 1572?","choices":["About 500 Benedictine monks were killed","About 2,000 Huguenots were slaughtered","Margaret of Valois married Henry of Bourbon","Philip II, future king of Spain, was born","Numerous Catholics were massacred by Huguenots"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"French Huguenots, who were Protestants, were massacred in August 1572. Death estimates vary; the source's duplicate description is replaced.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":453,"stem":"What happened during the St Bartholomew's Day massacre in France on 23–24 August 1572?","choices":["About 500 Benedictine monks were killed","About 2,000 Huguenots were slaughtered","Margaret of Valois married Henry of Bourbon","Philip II, future king of Spain, was born","Numerous Protestants were massacred"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Huguenots were French Protestants. Both B and E describe the massacre; the printed key selects B, but E is also valid. Exact death estimates vary.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B005-Q453","major":"History & Politics","label":"St Bartholomew's Day Massacre","era":"Early modern","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3474.jpeg","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"0ebf3ea3dc53482aecaa7fb26212b4f720a08f49fc64514580966a8c7187a525"},{"qid":454,"stem":"Which parties were involved in the Venetian Interdict crisis beginning in 1605–06?","choices":["Genoa and the Holy See","Venice and Milan","Florence and the Holy See","Venice and the Holy See","Naples and Rome"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The dispute concerned the Republic of Venice and papal authority.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":455,"stem":"Which dynasties respectively ruled Naples, Lombardy–Venetia and Tuscany in the mid-nineteenth century?","choices":["Savoy; Spanish Bourbons; Austrian Habsburgs","Austrian Habsburgs; Habsburg-Lorraine; Spanish Bourbons","Spanish Bourbons; Austrian Habsburgs; Habsburg-Lorraine","Spanish Bourbons; Austrian Habsburgs; Savoy","Spanish Habsburgs; Austrian Lorraine; Habsburg-Savoy"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source identifies the Bourbon dynasty in Naples, Austrian Habsburgs in Lombardy–Venetia, and Habsburg-Lorraine in Tuscany.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":456,"stem":"Which countries fought at Trafalgar on 21 October 1805?","choices":["Britain against France and Spain","Britain and Spain against France","France against Britain","Spain against France and Britain","Britain against Russia and the Netherlands"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Nelson's British fleet defeated the combined Franco-Spanish fleet.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":457,"stem":"Which sequence of historical figures matches the periods of these paintings, in the listed order?\na. Guernica, Picasso\nb. Las Meninas, Velázquez\nc. Luncheon on the Grass, Monet\nd. Primavera, Botticelli\ne. Giotto's Crucifix","choices":["Giuseppe Garibaldi; Cardinal Richelieu; Franklin D. Roosevelt; Giovanni Boccaccio; Girolamo Savonarola","Franklin D. Roosevelt; Cardinal Richelieu; Giuseppe Garibaldi; Giovanni Boccaccio; Girolamo Savonarola","Franklin D. Roosevelt; Girolamo Savonarola; Giuseppe Garibaldi; Cardinal Richelieu; Giovanni Boccaccio","Franklin D. Roosevelt; Cardinal Richelieu; Giuseppe Garibaldi; Girolamo Savonarola; Giovanni Boccaccio","Giuseppe Garibaldi; Cardinal Richelieu; Franklin D. Roosevelt; Girolamo Savonarola; Giovanni Boccaccio"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The intended broad periods run from twentieth-century Roosevelt through seventeenth-century Richelieu, nineteenth-century Garibaldi, Renaissance Savonarola and medieval Boccaccio; the question uses broad eras, not exact simultaneous dates.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":458,"stem":"In Masaccio's Trinity at Santa Maria Novella, the figures are framed within what kind of space?","choices":["Natural perspective space","Interior architectural perspective space","Exterior architectural perspective space","Artificial perspective space","Axonometric space"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The fresco creates the illusion of a vaulted architectural interior using linear perspective.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":459,"stem":"The dates 1431–1506 are associated with which artist?","choices":["Caravaggio","Titian","Andrea Mantegna","Giambattista Tiepolo","Piero della Francesca"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Mantegna was a fifteenth-century painter who died in 1506.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":460,"stem":"Which artist belongs to Surrealism?","choices":["Carrà","Manet","Picasso","Piet Mondrian","Dalí"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Salvador Dalí was a Surrealist. Miró, another Surrealist in the original choices, is replaced by Mondrian to ensure a single correct answer.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":460,"stem":"Which artist belongs to Surrealism?","choices":["Carrà","Manet","Picasso","Miró","Dalí"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Dalí is the printed answer, but Miró was also a major Surrealist. Both D and E are accepted.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B005-Q460","major":"Art History","label":"Surrealist artist","era":"19th century","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3474.jpeg","scoringKey":"E","contentFingerprint":"fadb87d1dd39fbb8752d37444419532fd5261487a4776a7850db0b795b3242f1"},{"qid":461,"stem":"Which Roman complex has a particularly strong landscape-scale relationship to its site?","choices":["Tomb of Caecilia Metella on the Appian Way","Arch of Augustus in Rimini","Sanctuary of Fortuna Primigenia at Palestrina","Baths of Diocletian","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The sanctuary's monumental terraces reshape and organise the hillside.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":462,"stem":"What is a trifora?","choices":["A pipe with three holes","A portico with three bays","An opening in a wall","A three-way hydraulic connection","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A trifora is a three-light window. C is broad but true; the printed key E overlooks that a window is an opening in a wall.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":463,"stem":"Identify the work from the detail shown.","choices":["I and the Village, Marc Chagall","Composition VII, Wassily Kandinsky","The Dance, Henri Matisse","Monochrome, Yves Klein","Chief of the Barbarians, Paul Klee"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The linked dancing figures belong to Matisse's The Dance.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":464,"stem":"Which pair of artists were NOT contemporaries?","choices":["Brunelleschi – Giotto","Vignola — Michelangelo","Titian – Sansovino","Raphael – Bramante","Ictinus – Phidias"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Giotto died in 1337 and Brunelleschi was born in 1377. The other pairs had overlapping lifetimes; the second non-contemporary pair in the original is corrected.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":464,"stem":"Which pair of artists were NOT contemporaries?","choices":["Brunelleschi – Giotto","Vignola – Canova","Titian – Sansovino","Raphael – Bramante","Ictinus – Phidias"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The printed key selects A, but B is also valid: Giotto died before Brunelleschi was born, and Vignola died long before Canova was born.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B005-Q464","major":"Art History","label":"Non-contemporary artist pair","era":"Medieval","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3475.jpeg","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"d51cb8c3535f0e9ee25a56b99e77c12669854ad5145cf05232e55c4a56176ba3"},{"qid":465,"stem":"Where does the term “grotesque decoration” come from?","choices":["Decorations discovered in Nero's Domus Aurea","Decorations in the Vatican grottoes","The German word Grot","The caricatural nature of its subjects","Rock engravings"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The buried rooms of the Domus Aurea were encountered as grotto-like spaces, giving the decoration its name.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":466,"stem":"Match the cultural terms to their representative centuries.\na. Early Christian; b. Humanism; c. Romanticism; d. Rationalism; e. Rococo\n1. Fifth; 2. Fifteenth; 3. Eighteenth; 4. Nineteenth; 5. Twentieth","choices":["a–3; b–2; c–5; d–4; e–1","a–4; b–5; c–1; d–2; e–3","a–2; b–3; c–5; d–4; e–1","a–1; b–2; c–4; d–5; e–3","a–1; b–3; c–2; d–5; e–4"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Here Rationalism refers to modern architecture, while Humanism refers to the Renaissance.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":467,"stem":"Alberti's De re aedificatoria, foundational to Renaissance architectural classicism:","choices":["Follows Vitruvius purely as theoretical reflection","Follows Vitruvius, supported by studies carried out in Rome","Is a completely new theoretical and practical elaboration","Is essentially a reworking of Vitruvius","None of the other answers is correct"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Alberti drew on Vitruvius and his study of ancient buildings. The printed key A's “purely theoretical” qualification is misleading.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":468,"stem":"What characterises the exterior of Parma Baptistery?","choices":["Successive tiers of trabeated loggias","Successive loggias with pointed arches","Absence of openings","Half-columns with Corinthian capitals","Mosaic-decorated niches and aedicules"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The upper exterior has stacked open galleries with horizontal lintels.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":469,"stem":"Which sequence lists these Italian presidents from most recent to earliest?","choices":["Mattarella; Scalfaro; Ciampi; Napolitano","Mattarella; Ciampi; Napolitano; Scalfaro","Mattarella; Napolitano; Scalfaro; Ciampi","Mattarella; Napolitano; Ciampi; Scalfaro","Mattarella; Ciampi; Scalfaro; Napolitano"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Their first terms began in 2015, 2006, 1999 and 1992 respectively.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":470,"stem":"What is Rococo?","choices":["A stylistic degeneration of the Baroque","An art historically preceding the Baroque and following Neoclassicism","An art attentive to furnishings and decorative objects such as porcelain, tapestries and furniture","An art with characteristics similar to the Baroque","A new conception of architectural space"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source's most specific definition emphasises refined interior decoration and decorative arts.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":471,"stem":"Order the first publications of these literary works: The Late Mattia Pascal; The Betrothed; The House by the Medlar Tree; Myricae.","choices":["The Late Mattia Pascal; The Betrothed; The House by the Medlar Tree; Myricae","The Betrothed; The House by the Medlar Tree; Myricae; The Late Mattia Pascal","The Betrothed; The Late Mattia Pascal; Myricae; The House by the Medlar Tree","The Late Mattia Pascal; The Betrothed; Myricae; The House by the Medlar Tree","The Betrothed; The Late Mattia Pascal; The House by the Medlar Tree; Myricae"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Betrothed appeared in 1827, The House by the Medlar Tree in 1881, Myricae in 1891 and The Late Mattia Pascal in 1904. The printed key B matches this sequence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":472,"stem":"Which drug kills pathogenic germs or stops them multiplying?","choices":["Antihistamine","Antibiotic","Analgesic","Antipyretic","Anabolic agent"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Antibiotics treat bacterial infections by killing bacteria or inhibiting their growth.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":473,"stem":"Article 84 of Italy's Constitution requires a presidential candidate to have reached what age?","choices":["Forty","Forty-five","Fifty","Twenty-five","Fifty-five"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The minimum age for election as President of Italy is fifty.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":474,"stem":"Which factor chiefly determines the amount payable on an electricity bill?","choices":["The voltage delivered","Resistance of appliances","The work that the energy consumed could perform","The meter's flow rate","The number of family members"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Electricity consumption is energy, often billed in kilowatt-hours.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":475,"stem":"Which city has an important Etruscan necropolis?","choices":["Cerveteri","Florence","Pisa","Pontevedra","Lucca"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Cerveteri's Banditaccia is a major Etruscan necropolis.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":476,"stem":"By which surname was Perugino known?","choices":["Baldassare Peruzzi","Alessandro Varotari","Pietro Vannucci","Andrea Mantegna","Pietro da Cortona"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Perugino was the painter Pietro Vannucci.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":477,"stem":"When was the capital of the Kingdom of Italy transferred to Rome?","choices":["1870","1861","1848","1871","1860"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Rome became Italy's capital in 1871, after its capture in 1870.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":478,"stem":"Which Italian city was ruled by the Gonzaga family in the sixteenth century?","choices":["Verona","Modena","Mantua","Ferrara","Padua"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Mantua was the Gonzagas' seat.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":479,"stem":"Which Roman emperor inaugurated the Colosseum?","choices":["Augustus","Hadrian","Tiberius","Titus","Diocletian"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Titus inaugurated the amphitheatre in 80 CE.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":480,"stem":"Alexander the Great defeated Darius III and extended his conquests as far as which present-day area?","choices":["Anatolia","Iran","Pakistan","India","Bangladesh"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Alexander campaigned in the Indus region of what is now northwestern India and Pakistan; the source's intended answer is India.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":481,"stem":"Who designed the Royal Palace of Caserta?","choices":["Joachim Murat","Édouard Manet","Luigi Vanvitelli","Giuseppe Piermarini","Antonio Canova"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Vanvitelli designed the vast eighteenth-century royal palace.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":482,"stem":"Which range best describes the Italian Risorgimento?","choices":["1922–1946","1789–1815","1492–1648","1815–1870","1799–1859"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The conventional political chronology runs from the post-Napoleonic era to the capture of Rome in 1870.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":483,"stem":"Where did the 1994–96 anti-Russian uprising led by Dzhokhar Dudayev take place?","choices":["Georgia","Ukraine","Chechnya","Belarus","Ossetia"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Dudayev led Chechen separatists in the First Chechen War.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":484,"stem":"Which event–date pairing is correct?","choices":["Queen Victoria's accession – 1837","Battle of Hastings – 1166","Execution of Charles I — 1659","Beginning of the Hundred Years' War – 1455","Approximate start of the Industrial Revolution — 1660"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Victoria became queen in 1837. Charles I was executed in 1649 and the Industrial Revolution is conventionally dated from around 1760; conflicting correct distractors are repaired.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":484,"stem":"Which event–date pairing is correct?","choices":["Queen Victoria's accession – 1837","Battle of Hastings – 1166","Execution of Charles I – 1649","Beginning of the Hundred Years' War – 1455","Approximate start of the Industrial Revolution – 1760"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Victoria's accession was in 1837. However C (1649) and E (approximately 1760) are also defensible, so this question has multiple correct options.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B007-Q484","major":"History & Politics","label":"Detecting an incorrect event–year pairing","era":"Medieval","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3477.jpeg","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"05c50f961bce38bde4e532849b79914dd23b23466001521a5d618fd3d7f9a73b"},{"qid":485,"stem":"In which period was France's École Polytechnique founded?","choices":["Late eighteenth century","Mid-nineteenth century","Late nineteenth century","Early nineteenth century","Late twentieth century"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The École Polytechnique was founded in 1794, in the late eighteenth century. The duplicate distractor is replaced and the stem uses a precise historical event.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":485,"stem":"According to this source, which period saw the educational division between architecture and engineering associated with the founding of France's École Polytechnique?","choices":["Late eighteenth century","Mid-nineteenth century","Late nineteenth century","Mid-nineteenth century","Late twentieth century"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The École Polytechnique was founded in 1794. The original has duplicate mid-nineteenth-century options.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B007-Q485","major":"Architecture","label":"Separation of architecture and engineering education at École Polytechnique","era":"Early modern","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3477.jpeg","scoringKey":"A","contentFingerprint":"a201a56ca7bd5507a1420f302c0c930fe3ac78b31de8938d8f1d37ae3f26b5db"},{"qid":486,"stem":"Who sculpted the statue of Perseus in Florence?","choices":["Benvenuto Cellini","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Antonio Canova","Sassu","Michelangelo"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Cellini sculpted Perseus with the Head of Medusa for the Loggia dei Lanzi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":487,"stem":"In which style was Milan Cathedral built?","choices":["Gothic","Baroque","Neoclassical","Mannerist","Romanesque"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The cathedral's principal form is Gothic.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":488,"stem":"What supplies High-Tech architecture's expressive aesthetic?","choices":["Traditional building techniques","Free sculptural architectural form","Distortion of the geometry of classical modern architecture","Monumental traditional elements such as roofs and columns","Emphatic display of construction elements derived from structural engineering"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"High-Tech architecture exposes and celebrates structure, services and engineered components.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":489,"stem":"Which tradition does the painting shown belong to?","choices":["Etruscan painting","Roman painting","Carthaginian painting","Greek painting","Persian painting"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Its figures and composition correspond to Etruscan tomb painting.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":490,"stem":"Which sequence places these historical figures in chronological order?","choices":["Alexander; William the Conqueror; Boccaccio; Charlemagne; Erasmus; Mozart; Galileo; Bismarck; Marconi","Alexander; William; Boccaccio; Charlemagne; Erasmus; Mozart; Galileo; Marconi; Bismarck","Alexander; William; Charlemagne; Boccaccio; Mozart; Erasmus; Galileo; Marconi; Bismarck","Alexander; Charlemagne; William; Boccaccio; Erasmus; Galileo; Mozart; Bismarck; Marconi","William; Alexander; Charlemagne; Boccaccio; Mozart; Erasmus; Galileo; Marconi; Bismarck"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Alexander (4th century BCE), Charlemagne (8th–9th), William (11th), Boccaccio (14th), Erasmus (15th–16th), Galileo (16th–17th), Mozart (18th), Bismarck (19th) and Marconi (19th–20th).","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":491,"stem":"Forests shrank markedly in Europe between the eleventh and thirteenth centuries. Which proposed cause is NOT valid?","choices":["Timber used for construction","Clearance for farmland","Timber fuelled craft activities","A widespread epidemic disease of plants","Wood used for domestic heating"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Land clearance and many uses of timber contributed; the proposed plant epidemic is not a standard explanation.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":492,"stem":"What was the medieval Hanseatic League?","choices":["An association of Danish football clubs","An Austrian opposition party","A commercial alliance of northern European cities dating to the twelfth century","A combination of precious metals","A seventeenth-century European non-aggression pact"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Hanseatic League was a trade network of northern European towns.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":493,"stem":"What was the Battle of the Bulge?","choices":["A Roman–Carthaginian battle in the First Punic War","A battle between Charles V's Spain and Francis I's France","Partisans' successful defence of Paris in World War II","A costly German counteroffensive at the end of World War II","An Italo-British Mediterranean air and naval battle in World War I"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Ardennes offensive began in December 1944 and failed to reverse Germany's defeat.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":494,"stem":"Which war ended with the Peace of Westphalia?","choices":["Third Italian War of Independence","Hundred Years' War","Thirty Years' War","First World War","Wars of the Roses"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The treaties of Westphalia ended the Thirty Years' War in 1648.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":495,"stem":"Which event did NOT take place in the nineteenth century?","choices":["American Civil War","Crimean War","Franco-Prussian War","Italian War of Independence","Spanish Civil War"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Spanish Civil War was fought in 1936–39.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":496,"stem":"What is a temple surrounded by one row of columns on all sides called?","choices":["Prostyle","Pseudoperipteral","Peripteral","Dipteral","Amphiprostyle"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A peripteral temple has a single surrounding colonnade.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":497,"stem":"What character is associated with icon painting?","choices":["Allegorical","Symbolic","Realist","Decorative","Calligraphic"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Icons use conventional sacred imagery with symbolic meaning.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":498,"stem":"Which invention predates the Counter-Reformation, Baroque period and Thirty Years' War?","choices":["Microscope","Pendulum clock","Telescope","Pantograph","Astrolabe"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The astrolabe dates to antiquity, well before the sixteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":499,"stem":"Which figure was born after all the others?","choices":["Francisco Franco","Adolf Hitler","Benito Mussolini","Pol Pot","Joseph Stalin"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Pol Pot was born in 1925; the others were born in the nineteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":500,"stem":"What was a Byzantine exarchate?","choices":["A department of the Italian Finance Ministry","A region of central France","A territory governed under Byzantine authority","A system of government among early Arab peoples","A special-status Middle Eastern province"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"An exarchate was an administrative territory governed by an exarch.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":501,"stem":"Which event culminated in the 1937–45 Sino-Japanese War?","choices":["Japan's conquest of Hong Kong","Japan's conquest of Nanjing","Japan's conquest of Beijing","China's conquest of Kyoto","China's conquest of Hong Kong"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Japan seized Nanjing in December 1937; the original uses “culminated” imprecisely because the war continued until 1945.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":502,"stem":"Who overthrew the Egyptian monarchy in 1952 and soon became president?","choices":["Farouk","Sadat","Nasser","Mubarak","Atatürk"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Gamal Abdel Nasser emerged as leader of the Free Officers; he became president in 1956, not immediately after the coup.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":503,"stem":"When did the Allied Normandy landings take place?","choices":["10 June 1940","25 July 1943","8 September 1943","6 June 1944","25 April 1945"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"D-Day was 6 June 1944.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":504,"stem":"Which event–year match is correct?","choices":["Defeat of the French at Sedan – 1848","Battle of Waterloo – 1815","Soviet invasion of Czechoslovakia — 1956","Battle of Marathon — 480 BCE","Korean War armistice — 1950"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Waterloo was fought in 1815. Sedan was in 1870, the invasion of Czechoslovakia in 1968, Marathon in 490 BC and the Korean armistice in 1953.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":504,"stem":"Which event–year match is correct?","choices":["Defeat of the French at Sedan – 1848","Battle of Waterloo – 1815","Soviet invasion of Czechoslovakia – 1968","Battle of Marathon – 490 BCE","End of the Korean War – 1953"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The printed answer is B, but C, D and E are also historically correct. This item must accept all four.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B007-Q504","major":"History & Politics","label":"Incorrect event–year pairing","era":"19th century","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3479.jpeg","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"70a16137cbe4d98a889f26ecb7521bfa871fdf192e7a7e6b64a7621930198b0c"},{"qid":505,"stem":"In which Italian city did Piero della Francesca paint The Legend of the True Cross?","choices":["Florence","Genoa","Venice","Urbino","Arezzo"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The cycle is in the Basilica of San Francesco in Arezzo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":506,"stem":"Match each building to its city.\nA. Teatro degli Arcimboldi; B. Ice Hockey Stadium; C. Neues Museum; D. Palazzo della Rovere\n1. Tokyo; 2. Milan; 3. Berlin; 4. Turin","choices":["A–1; B–4; C–3; D–2","A–1; B–2; C–4; D–3","A–2; B–4; C–3; D–1","A–2; B–3; C–4; D–1","A–1; B–2; C–3; D–4"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Arcimboldi theatre is in Milan, the referenced hockey stadium in Turin, Neues Museum in Berlin, and this Palazzo della Rovere in Tokyo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":507,"stem":"When World War I began on 28 July 1914, Italy:","choices":["Initially remained neutral, then joined the Entente powers","Immediately joined Germany and Austria-Hungary","Immediately joined the Entente","Initially stayed neutral, then joined Germany and Austria-Hungary","Remained neutral until 1917, then joined the United States"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Italy entered the war in May 1915 on the Entente side.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":508,"stem":"Who wrote the stage work Mistero buffo?","choices":["Giorgio Gaber","Dario Fo","Giorgio Strehler","Bertolt Brecht","Eduardo De Filippo"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Dario Fo created Mistero buffo, a solo theatrical work based on popular performance traditions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":509,"stem":"What was the ARMIR?","choices":["Fascist Ministry of Popular Culture","The original name of the Italian military air force","A Fascist heavy cruiser","Italy's expeditionary army on the Russian front in World War II","A local Fascist Party branch"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"ARMIR denotes the Italian Army in Russia during World War II.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":510,"stem":"In what year did Agostino Depretis come to power through Italy's “parliamentary revolution”?","choices":["1870","1878","1866","1887","1876"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The historical Left took office under Depretis in 1876.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":511,"stem":"What is Italy's certified email service (PEC)?","choices":["A service for sending email at high speed","An email service granting a message legal evidentiary value similar to registered post with receipt","A service for attachments over 2 GB","A service for attachments over 1 GB","A service for securely paying taxes and fines"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"PEC certifies sending and delivery with legal effect under Italian rules.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":512,"stem":"What does Ctrl+Z do in Microsoft Word?","choices":["Undo the last action","Delete a macro","Save recent changes","Insert an image","Copy previously selected text"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Ctrl+Z undoes the most recent action.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":513,"stem":"Complete this phrase from Article 33 of Italy's Constitution: “... are free, and the teaching of them is free.”","choices":["Literature and technology","Art and culture","Literature and science","Culture and scientific research","Art and science"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Article 33 begins: “Art and science are free, and teaching them is free.”","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":514,"stem":"In which century did Denis Diderot live?","choices":["Eighteenth","Sixteenth","Fifteenth","Seventeenth","Nineteenth"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Diderot lived from 1713 to 1784.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":515,"stem":"Which European territorial colonial venture in Africa began in 1830?","choices":["Italy in Eritrea","France in Algeria","Britain in South Africa","Belgium in Congo","Germany in Morocco"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"France invaded Algeria in 1830.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":516,"stem":"Where did Giuseppe Mazzini briefly participate directly in government?","choices":["Kingdom of Savoy","Grand Duchy of Tuscany","Habsburg Empire","Republic of Venice","Roman Republic"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Mazzini served in the Roman Republic of 1849.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":517,"stem":"What characterised Stalin's Great Purge?","choices":["Mass deportations and executions of party officials, state employees and military officers","Execution of thousands of Americans and Britons","A more liberal and democratic internal policy","Full press freedom for party-controlled newspapers","Elimination of the last supporters of Nicholas II"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The purges targeted perceived enemies across the Communist Party, state and army.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":518,"stem":"Who painted The Sea of Ice?","choices":["Eugène Delacroix","William Turner","Francesco Hayez","Caspar David Friedrich","Johann H. Füssli"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Friedrich's The Sea of Ice depicts a wreck amid Arctic ice.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":519,"stem":"Which military operation was called Enduring Freedom?","choices":["The US-led 1991 Iraq operation","The US-led 2001 Afghanistan operation","NATO action against Milošević in 1999","NATO action in Bosnia in 1995","The US-led 2003 Iraq operation"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Operation Enduring Freedom began in Afghanistan in 2001.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":520,"stem":"Who designed the Constitution Bridge over Venice's Grand Canal, opened in 2008?","choices":["Renzo Piano","Santiago Calatrava","Gae Aulenti","Massimiliano Fuksas","Zaha Hadid"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Calatrava designed the bridge near Piazzale Roma.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":521,"stem":"Where and when did Expressionism develop as an artistic movement?","choices":["Germany in the early twentieth century","Austria in the late nineteenth century","Italy in the early twentieth century","Germany in the late nineteenth century","France in the early nineteenth century"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"German Expressionist movements gained prominence in the early 1900s.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":522,"stem":"Identify the movement and artist of the painting shown.","choices":["Futurism, Carlo Carrà","Metaphysical painting, Carlo Carrà","Metaphysical painting, Giorgio de Chirico","Futurism, Umberto Boccioni","Dadaism, Marcel Duchamp"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The photographed painting is Carlo Carrà's The Metaphysical Muse (1917), identified by the Pinacoteca di Brera as a work from his Metaphysical period; the printed answer A is incorrect.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":523,"stem":"Which movement–key concept pairing is correct?","choices":["Futurism — rejection of technology","Art Nouveau — abolition of ornament","Impressionism — rigid geometric idealization","Expressionism — emphasis on subjective emotion and distortion","Impressionism — rejection of observed light and colour"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Expressionism prioritizes emotional experience, often through distorted forms and intense colours. The vague and overlapping pairings are replaced with distinct alternatives.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":523,"stem":"Which movement–key concept pairing is correct?","choices":["Futurism – the phenomenal world","Art Nouveau – emphasis on forms","Impressionism – lyrical abstraction","Expressionism – lyrical abstraction","Impressionism – the phenomenal world"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The printed key selects D, but E also accurately describes Impressionism's attention to perceived reality. Accept both if offered.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B007-Q523","major":"Art History","label":"Art movement and concept pairing","era":"Early modern","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3480.jpeg","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"52f734d839a4eab8aac85a469199f3e94761d385829983f8943e48e54611a252"},{"qid":524,"stem":"Which painting is by Vincent van Gogh?","choices":["Rouen Cathedral, Full Sun (1894)","The White Horse (1898)","The Card Players (1890–92)","Wheatfield with Crows (1890)","The Large Bathers (1898–1905)"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Van Gogh painted Wheatfield with Crows; Monet painted Rouen Cathedral, Cézanne The Card Players and The Large Bathers.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":525,"stem":"In architecture, what does Functionalism emphasise?","choices":["Heliothermic architecture","Harmony between colours and forms","Futuristic buildings under construction","Unity of an object's form and function","Technical applications of an object"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Functionalism derives form from intended use.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":526,"stem":"During which conflict did Arkan's Tigers operate in the last decade of the twentieth century?","choices":["Rwanda","Chechnya","The former Yugoslavia","The Gulf War","The former Belgian Congo"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Arkan's paramilitary unit operated during the wars in former Yugoslavia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":527,"stem":"Striped façades alternating light and coloured (green or pink) marble are typical of which architecture?","choices":["Early Christian","Renaissance","Romanesque","Baroque","Contemporary"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Polychrome marble façades are characteristic of many Italian Romanesque buildings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":528,"stem":"Which architect was NEVER involved in designing airports, stations or ports according to the source?","choices":["Renzo Piano","Santiago Calatrava","Bruno Munari","Nicholas Grimshaw","Zaha Hadid"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Munari was primarily an artist and product designer rather than an infrastructure architect.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":529,"stem":"What is galena?","choices":["A mineral","A boat","A medicine","A garment","A depiction"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Galena is a lead sulfide mineral.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":530,"stem":"Which Italian word is a synonym of indigeno (“indigenous”)?","choices":["Selvaggio (“wild”)","Straniero (“foreign”)","Nemico (“enemy”)","Nativo (“native”)","Mendico (“beggar”)"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Indigeno means native to a place.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":531,"stem":"Which novelist was also a mathematician?","choices":["Lewis Carroll","Mikhail Bulgakov","George Eliot","Gustave Flaubert","George Bernard Shaw"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Lewis Carroll was the pen name of mathematician Charles Lutwidge Dodgson.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":532,"stem":"Who designed the New York Guggenheim Museum and the Bilbao Guggenheim Museum respectively?","choices":["Wright; Calatrava","Mies van der Rohe; Niemeyer","Wright; Gehry","Gropius; Gehry","Mies van der Rohe; Calatrava"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Frank Lloyd Wright designed the New York museum and Frank Gehry the Bilbao museum.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":533,"stem":"In which year was the Oslo accord signed, for which Rabin and Arafat later received the Nobel Peace Prize?","choices":["1995","1989","1993","1987","2001"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Oslo Accord was signed in 1993; the Peace Prize was awarded in 1994.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":534,"stem":"Which listed people conquered Egypt during its long history?","choices":["Gauls","Etruscans","Carthaginians","Parthians","Assyrians"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Assyrians invaded and ruled Egypt in the seventh century BCE.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":535,"stem":"Which project shows the three skyscrapers in the photograph?","choices":["CityLife, Milan","Burj Dubai, Dubai","CityLife, London","Sears Tower, Chicago","Empire State Building, New York"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The three towers form the CityLife redevelopment in Milan.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":536,"stem":"What figure of speech conveys an idea through an image drawn from another domain?","choices":["Metaphor","Hyperbole","Allegory","Zeugma","Analogy"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A metaphor transfers meaning through a figurative comparison.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":537,"stem":"Which person lived during Dante's lifetime?","choices":["Giotto","Mantegna","Praxiteles","Filarete","Brunelleschi"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Giotto (c.1267–1337) and Dante (1265–1321) were contemporaries.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":538,"stem":"Which term is a synonym for a cupola?","choices":["Nave","Frieze","Tiburio","Apse","Ambulatory"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A tiburio is the exterior structure enclosing or covering a dome. It is related to but not strictly synonymous with the cupola.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":539,"stem":"Who designed the Superleggera chair?","choices":["Giuseppe Terragni","Bruno Munari","Angelo Mangiarotti","Gio Ponti","Franco Albini"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Gio Ponti designed the lightweight Superleggera chair for Cassina.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31","imageCredit93":"https://www.moma.org/collection/works/509252","imageNote93":"Museum object photograph added for study; not an Alpha page reproduction."},{"qid":540,"stem":"Which building type does NOT belong to a typical classical Greek city?","choices":["Thermae","Theatre","Agora","Bouleuterion","Temple"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Large public thermae are particularly Roman; Greek cities had theatres, agoras, council houses and temples.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":541,"stem":"What is the sequence of Rome's main political periods?","choices":["Kingdom; Empire; Kingdom","Kingdom; Empire; Republic","Kingdom; Republic; Empire","Empire; Republic; Kingdom","Empire; Kingdom; Empire"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Ancient Rome was first a kingdom, then a republic, then an empire.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":542,"stem":"Which painter was NOT an Impressionist?","choices":["Renoir","Manet","Fattori","Degas","Monet"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Fattori was associated with the Italian Macchiaioli. Manet influenced the Impressionists, though he did not join their exhibitions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":543,"stem":"Which word denotes a type of portico?","choices":["Pronaos","Cella","Atrium","Opisthodomos","Dipteros"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The pronaos is a porch before a temple's main chamber.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":544,"stem":"Who made a series of artworks titled Composition?","choices":["Marc Chagall","Wassily Kandinsky","Henri Matisse","Yves Klein","Paul Klee"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Kandinsky numbered numerous abstract paintings Composition I, II and so on.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":545,"stem":"Order these paintings chronologically.\n1. Madonna of the Goldfinch, Raphael\n2. Supper at Emmaus, Caravaggio\n3. Poppies, Monet\n4. Portrait of Federico da Montefeltro, Piero della Francesca\n5. The Grand Canal towards Rialto, Canaletto","choices":["1; 4; 2; 3; 5","4; 1; 2; 5; 3","4; 5; 1; 2; 3","5; 4; 1; 2; 3","4; 1; 2; 3; 5"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Piero (fifteenth century), Raphael (early sixteenth), Caravaggio (early seventeenth), Canaletto (eighteenth), Monet (nineteenth).","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":546,"stem":"In a Roman circus, a meta marked the start or finish of a race. What was it?","choices":["A pointed pillar","A vault","A column","An arch","A milestone"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The meta was a turning post, often represented as a conical pillar.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":547,"stem":"In architecture, what is a contrafforte?","choices":["A layer","A reinforcing buttress","A space","A technique","A frame"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"A buttress strengthens a wall against outward forces.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":548,"stem":"What is one segment of a column shaft called?","choices":["Rocchio (drum)","Base","Ratta","Colletto","Cippo"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"A column shaft assembled from pieces is built of drums.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":549,"stem":"Which Italian verb best replaces dare in the expression “ci sono funghi che danno la morte”?","choices":["Ratificano","Producono","Risolvono","Portano","Procurano"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source selects procurano (“cause/bring about”). Portano (“bring”) can also be idiomatic in this sentence.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":550,"stem":"In structural engineering, a beam's “portata” refers to:","choices":["Its length","The resistance or load capacity in relation to its span","Its thickness","The height at which it is placed","The load actually applied to it"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The intended meaning is its structural carrying capacity, which depends in part on span.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":551,"stem":"What is a ciborium in a church?","choices":["A burial place","An empty tomb","A monastery enclosure","A canopy over an altar","An atrium"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A ciborium is an architectural canopy over an altar; the source paraphrases it as a tabernacle.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":552,"stem":"Who designed Rome's church of God the Merciful Father, known for its three white sails?","choices":["Renzo Piano","Santiago Calatrava","Le Corbusier","Richard Meier","Antoni Gaudí"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Richard Meier designed the Jubilee Church.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":553,"stem":"Which organisation maintains the World Heritage List?","choices":["WHO","UNICEF","OECD","UNESCO","WTO"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"UNESCO inscribes and monitors World Heritage Sites.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":554,"stem":"Which style does Urbino's Ducal Palace exemplify?","choices":["Romanesque","Gothic","Renaissance","Neoclassical","Neo-Gothic"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Ducal Palace is a major fifteenth-century Renaissance complex.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":555,"stem":"Which filmmaker is NOT usually considered part of Italian Neorealism?","choices":["Roberto Rossellini","Vittorio De Sica","Pietro Germi","Franco Zeffirelli","Luchino Visconti"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Zeffirelli is not normally classed with the Neorealist filmmakers.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":556,"stem":"Which Italian word is etymologically different from the others?","choices":["Panlogismo","Paneuropeo","Panpsichismo","Panflettista","Panteista"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Panflettista derives from “pamphlet”; the others begin with Greek pan-, meaning “all.”","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":557,"stem":"Who was Ignatius of Loyola?","choices":["An explorer","The inventor of the sextant","Founder of the Society of Jesus","Winner of the Battle of Cyprus","Pope Adrian VI before his election"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Ignatius founded the Jesuit order in the sixteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":558,"stem":"Which major award is given to architects?","choices":["Pritzker Prize","Pulitzer Prize","Emmy Award","César Award","Nobel Prize"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Pritzker Prize honours architectural achievement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":559,"stem":"What is cloud computing?","choices":["Offline technology for buying goods","Using remote servers to access hardware and software resources, free or by subscription","Using remote servers exclusively for hardware resources","An online catalogue of computers","Free access to very complex numerical calculations"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Cloud services provide computing or software resources via networked remote infrastructure.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":560,"stem":"Which work is NOT by Perugino?","choices":["Adoration of the Magi","Miracle of the Snow","Visitation with Saint Anne","Apparition of the Virgin to Saint Bernard","Saint George and the Dragon"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The source attributes Saint George and the Dragon to Raphael, not Perugino.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":561,"stem":"When did Art Nouveau spread widely?","choices":["From the last decade of the nineteenth century into the early twentieth","Between the world wars","In the 1950s","In the mid-nineteenth century","From 1970 onwards"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Art Nouveau flourished around 1890–1910.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":562,"stem":"Which institution of the Italian Republic is chosen by direct universal suffrage?","choices":["Government","President of the Republic","Senate","Prime Minister","High Council of the Judiciary"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Italian voters elect the Senate; the president and prime minister are chosen by other constitutional processes.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":563,"stem":"Which art form can an Italian “ipostilo” (hypostyle) describe?","choices":["Painting","Sculpture","Building","Poem","Musical composition"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Hypostyle means a space whose roof is supported by columns, as in a hypostyle hall.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":564,"stem":"Which of these is NOT traditionally one of the named branches of architectural work in this question?","choices":["Urban planning","Industrial design","Decoration","Territorial planning","Restoration"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The source treats decoration as the odd one out among major planning and design specialisms; the boundary depends on the educational classification used.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":565,"stem":"The foundational theses of modern urbanism formulated in 1933 became known as the Charter of:","choices":["Athens","Rome","Paris","Rio de Janeiro","Vienna"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The CIAM Athens Charter set out principles of functionalist planning.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":566,"stem":"How did Brunelleschi approach classical architecture?","choices":["Slavish imitation","Conscious reinterpretation","Deliberate rejection","Marginal reuse","Pragmatic adhesion"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Brunelleschi studied classical forms and creatively reinterpreted them for Renaissance architecture. The printed key C conflicts with this standard characterisation.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":567,"stem":"In which Italian city are works by Kenzo Tange found?","choices":["Milan","Turin","Rome","Verona","Bari"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Tange's projects in the Milan area include the BMW Italia headquarters; the source chooses Milan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":568,"stem":"Curved and dynamic lines are characteristic of which architecture here?","choices":["Baroque","Romanesque","Twentieth-century","Mannerist","Carolingian"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Baroque architecture frequently emphasises curved surfaces and spatial movement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":569,"stem":"Who wrote the fantasy novel The Lord of the Rings?","choices":["J. K. Rowling","C. S. Lewis","Vladimir Nabokov","J. R. R. Tolkien","Isaac Asimov"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Tolkien authored The Lord of the Rings.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":570,"stem":"In modern architecture, what does CIAM stand for?","choices":["Carico instabile agente mediano","International Convention of Friends of Modernism","Anti-movement inclined buttress","International Congresses of Modern Architecture","Anti-modernist iconographic criteria"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"CIAM expands to Congrès internationaux d'architecture moderne.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":571,"stem":"Which sequence gives these architectural styles in chronological order?","choices":["Romanesque; Renaissance; Gothic; Mannerist","Gothic; Romanesque; Renaissance; Mannerist","Gothic; Mannerist; Romanesque; Renaissance","Renaissance; Romanesque; Mannerist; Gothic","Romanesque; Gothic; Renaissance; Mannerist"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Romanesque precedes Gothic, Renaissance and Mannerism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":572,"stem":"What nationality was the architect Walter Gropius?","choices":["German","Turkish","French","Swiss","Austrian"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Gropius was born in Berlin and founded the German Bauhaus.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":573,"stem":"Which of the listed arts has an evocative purpose in the source's classification?","choices":["Painting","Music","Architecture","Restoration","Urban planning"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The printed answer identifies painting. Music also evokes emotions; this source's classification is too broad for a uniquely graded item.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":574,"stem":"What are the “minor arts” in the traditional historical classification?","choices":["Non-figurative arts","Products made by apprentices to famous masters","Early Medieval arts","An artistic rebellion","Arts producing usually functional objects"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The term historically includes applied and decorative arts producing usable objects.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":575,"stem":"What does a VoIP service allow?","choices":["Using computers to send emails","Calling over an ordinary domestic analogue phone line alone","Calling using only a 56K modem","Making a telephone call using an internet connection","Shopping without entering sensitive information"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Voice over Internet Protocol carries voice conversations over IP networks.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":576,"stem":"In which century was Giorgione born?","choices":["Fifteenth","Sixteenth","Seventeenth","Fourteenth","Thirteenth"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Giorgione was born around the 1470s.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":577,"stem":"Who painted The Three Ages of Man?","choices":["Gustav Klimt","Michelangelo","Giorgione","Albrecht Dürer","Giorgio Morandi"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Giorgione is credited with a painting known as The Three Ages of Man; Klimt made another work with a similar title, so the title alone may be ambiguous.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":578,"stem":"In whose workshop did Titian receive important early training in Venice?","choices":["Giorgione","Andrea Mantegna","Michelangelo","Giovanni Bellini","Piero della Francesca"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Titian trained in Giovanni Bellini's workshop and later worked closely with Giorgione. Specifying early workshop training resolves the original ambiguity.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v40","originalRecord":{"qid":578,"stem":"Titian trained with which painter?","choices":["Giorgione","Andrea Mantegna","Michelangelo","Giovanni Bellini","Piero della Francesca"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The printed key says Giorgione; historical accounts place Titian as an apprentice in Bellini's workshop, later collaborating closely with Giorgione. Both A and D are plausible depending on “trained with.”","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","correctionNote":"Source defect corrected to provide one defensible answer; original wording and choices retained in the audit record.","id":"ALPHA-B007-Q578","major":"Art History","label":"Titian's teacher","era":"Renaissance","examLanguages":["en","it"],"sourceImage":"IMG_3485.jpeg","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"88b50a4c094198a86c26357526e8e8cfcabbca84fefde5b240c7fc747c4930f4"},{"qid":579,"stem":"Which city holds an annual snow-sculpture festival?","choices":["Sapporo","Helsinki","Palermo","Linz","Moscow"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Sapporo's annual Snow Festival is famous for large snow sculptures.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":580,"stem":"Which twentieth-century French writer wrote Nausea?","choices":["Georges Bataille","Jean-Paul Sartre","Albert Camus","Françoise Sagan","André Gide"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Sartre's philosophical novel Nausea appeared in 1938.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":581,"stem":"What is the opposite of the Italian verb aizzare (“incite”)?","choices":["Sobillare","Interrare","Istigare","Frenare","Rinfrescare"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Frenare means to restrain or hold back; aizzare means to incite.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":582,"stem":"What does the Italian noun alone mean?","choices":["Halo","Laurel","Degree","Darkness","Shadow"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Alone denotes a halo or aura.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":583,"stem":"Which of these saints was born in Africa?","choices":["Ambrose of Milan","Thomas Aquinas","Paul of Tarsus","Anthony of Padua","Augustine of Hippo"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Augustine was born in Tagaste in Roman North Africa.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":584,"stem":"What is the ancient sculpture shown?","choices":["The Virgin of the Rocks","Venus de Milo","Winged Victory of Samothrace","Venus Victrix","Athena Parthenos"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The armless Hellenistic statue is the Venus de Milo.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":585,"stem":"Put these late-twentieth-century events in chronological order.\na. May 1968 protests in France; b. John F. Kennedy's assassination; c. First Moon landing; d. Fall of the Berlin Wall; e. Election of Karol Wojtyła as pope","choices":["b; a; c; e; d","a; c; d; b; e","c; b; a; e; d","a; b; c; d; e","b; c; a; e; d"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The dates are 1963, 1968, 1969, 1978 and 1989.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":586,"stem":"What style is Florence's Palazzo Rucellai, shown in the image?","choices":["Romanesque","Gothic","Baroque","Renaissance","Rationalist"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Alberti's Renaissance façade uses ordered pilasters and classical proportions.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B007-Q586","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3485.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[778,1070,969,1328]],"method":"source_figure_crop","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3485.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"D","contentFingerprint":"bb9a4d15122ab16b48979d087486622cf31d3de46c734f81488bc1268a291f95"},{"qid":587,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q587","major":"Art History","era":"Ancient","label":"Myron and the Discobolus","stem":"The Discobolus shown in the image is a sculpture by which artist?","choices":["Myron","Phidias","Polykleitos","Praxiteles","Lysippos"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","scoringKey":"A","explanation":"The Discobolus (Discus Thrower) is attributed to the Greek sculptor Myron.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[221,151,424,428]],"method":"source_crop","essential":true,"width":203,"height":277},"visualSubject":"Discobolus","visualConcept":"Sculptor identification from the statue","replacementAssetPlan":"Use a licensed/public-domain reproduction for generated variants.","contentFingerprint":"6b68ef8858edb820686cde0f053fac1b4cc958368b9826015e09daf8d09db968"},{"qid":588,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q588","major":"Art History","era":"Renaissance","label":"Leonardo and the Last Judgment","stem":"Which of these works is NOT by Leonardo da Vinci?","choices":["The Virgin of the Rocks","Madonna of the Carnation","The Last Judgment","The Annunciation","Lady with an Ermine"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","scoringKey":"C","explanation":"The Last Judgment on the Sistine Chapel altar wall is by Michelangelo, not Leonardo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","contentFingerprint":"53c13c9dc85f03c5008fe78672d1dec2107ceae789b780bbf907f62e56cc6951"},{"qid":589,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q589","major":"History & Politics","era":"20th century","label":"Robert F. Kennedy as Attorney General","stem":"Which office did Robert F. Kennedy hold in the United States during the 1960s?","choices":["Attorney General","Vice President","Governor of California","Secretary of State","President"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","scoringKey":"A","explanation":"Robert F. Kennedy served as US Attorney General from 1961 to 1964.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","contentFingerprint":"1e6d141cc713e7eebfe921d13e20374bfd254ad0b26b45bf1140f315d84a4b8f"},{"qid":590,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q590","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Impressionism and geometric perspective","stem":"Which statement is NOT characteristic of Impressionist painting?","choices":["Brushstrokes are free: dabs, commas and streaks","Painting is done outdoors","Forms are not enclosed by rigid outlines","Light is crucial because it transforms objects and colours","Space is constructed according to geometric rules of perspective"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","scoringKey":"E","explanation":"Impressionists emphasised fleeting light, loose strokes and open-air painting rather than rigid geometric construction.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","contentFingerprint":"c251c1759c4c94af0120cfe755bdb7383e39b280821421236ea952036e48f55c"},{"qid":591,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q591","major":"Art History","era":"18th century","label":"Venice in Canaletto’s vedute","stem":"Which city is often depicted in Canaletto’s views (vedute)?","choices":["Florence","Venice","Padua","Verona","Rome"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","scoringKey":"B","explanation":"Canaletto is particularly known for detailed views of Venice.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","contentFingerprint":"d49e74dcb27460a3f6e2c5e7d664335bf23243922a1bc23da0ddd668eab9cedc"},{"qid":592,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q592","major":"Art History","era":"18th century","label":"Canaletto and the camera obscura","stem":"Which aid helped Canaletto achieve rigorous perspective in his city views?","choices":["Sfumato","Detailed preparatory sketches alone","A camera obscura","Strong chiaroscuro","None: as an Expressionist, he rejected optical representation"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","scoringKey":"C","explanation":"A camera obscura projects a scene and can help an artist study its perspective.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_and_printed_key_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","contentFingerprint":"52e62f157da2c39a4b3772e53f864e679c2ecdf95e3f9b90264056c4e1ff0cdd"},{"qid":593,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q593","major":"Architecture","era":"Classical","label":"Doric column and its base","stem":"Which statement about the usual forms of classical column orders is false?","choices":["An Ionic capital has two symmetrical volutes","A classical Greek Doric column stands on a pedestal formed by a concave ring and a rectangular block","A Tuscan column is unfluted","A Doric shaft tapers towards the top","A Corinthian capital has carved plant motifs"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","scoringKey":"B","explanation":"A classical Greek Doric column normally stands directly on the stylobate, without the described base.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","correctionNote":"Specified classical Greek Doric order to avoid the ambiguity of Roman Doric variants.","contentFingerprint":"eec5567a31f3d8f39837ad6f8dc8aab7f6195b037615202e05d4ce5960f2624e"},{"qid":594,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q594","major":"Art History","era":"19th century","label":"Van Gogh’s expressive painting","stem":"Compared with these alternative descriptions, which best captures the expressive aim associated with Vincent van Gogh’s painting?","choices":["To give a truthful account of objects and the social function of art","To depict atmospheric effects and the aesthetic play of light","To show everyday scenes considered improper","To express human and artistic anguish and solitude","To eliminate perspective and three-dimensionality"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","scoringKey":"D","explanation":"Van Gogh used expressive colour and brushwork to convey intense emotion; the option does not describe every work.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","correctionNote":"Qualified the broad source description as a comparative association, rather than a universal claim about every work.","contentFingerprint":"52f03494759f84341d3a266bce002c24140d5d6b5a87d31bbb7f4c909f8fa0c1"},{"qid":595,"id":"ALPHA-B007-Q595","major":"History & Politics","era":"Early modern to modern","label":"French political figures and attributed sayings","stem":"Match these French political figures with the sayings traditionally attributed to them: a. Henry IV; b. Louis XIV; c. François Guizot; d. Louis XV; e. Charles de Gaulle. 1. “Enrich yourselves”; 2. “After me, the deluge”; 3. “Only fools never make mistakes”; 4. “Paris is well worth a Mass”; 5. “I am the state.”","choices":["a–4; b–5; c–1; d–2; e–3","a–5; b–2; c–1; d–3; e–4","a–4; b–3; c–2; d–5; e–1","a–3; b–2; c–5; d–4; e–1","a–1; b–5; c–3; d–4; e–2"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","scoringKey":"A","explanation":"The conventional matches are Henry IV–4, Louis XIV–5, Guizot–1, Louis XV–2 and de Gaulle–3. These are traditional attributions, not verified verbatim quotations.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"corrected_source_reviewed","language":"en","version":"source-en-v41","sourceImage":"IMG_3608.jpeg","sourcePage":63,"examLanguages":["en","it"],"reviewStatus":"ready","holdReason":"","answerBasis":"Original page 63 and photographed printed answer key 351–790; five options retain source order.","correctionNote":"Identified historical sayings as traditional attributions; authenticity of verbatim wording is not implied.","contentFingerprint":"e3b76bcd685f494ee94618cd1a94fd6ec676101af1102948d55810dc692f1011"},{"qid":596,"stem":"Why did Andy Warhol use mass-market products and entertainment celebrities as subjects?","choices":["To advertise products as a specialist packaging designer","To reflect the mass repetition of recognisable everyday imagery in modern life","To create purely objective geometric painting without natural references","To promote Campbell's industry after the war","Because his choices were random and he cared only about new composition techniques"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Pop Art examines how mass media and commercial imagery shape modern perception.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":597,"stem":"Which artist–work pairing is wrong?","choices":["Marcel Duchamp – Bicycle Wheel (1913)","Man Ray – Rayograph (1925)","Paul Gauguin – Sunflowers (1888)","Jacques-Louis David – Death of Marat (1793)","Antonio Canova – The Three Graces (c.1815)"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The Sunflowers series is by Vincent van Gogh, not Paul Gauguin.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":598,"stem":"According to a historical anecdote, whom did Caravaggio use as the model for the Virgin in Death of the Virgin?","choices":["His mother","A well-known courtesan called Lena","His lover","The corpse of a woman drowned in the Tiber","The corpse of a woman he had killed"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The drowned-woman account is a traditional story, not established documentary fact; the printed key presents it as an answer.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":599,"stem":"Who made the work shown?","choices":["Paul Klee","Marc Chagall","The Blue Rider group","Wassily Kandinsky","Henri Matisse"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The abstract geometric painting is by Kandinsky; a group is not an individual artist.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":600,"stem":"Who designed the new Fiera Milano complex at Rho-Pero?","choices":["Daniel Libeskind","Ettore Sottsass","Massimiliano Fuksas","Giorgetto Giugiaro","Sergio Pininfarina"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Fuksas designed the trade-fair complex and its sweeping glass canopy.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":601,"stem":"In which period did the architect become theorised as the sole designer who plans a building before its subsequent construction?","choices":["Renaissance","Middle Ages","Rationalism","Hellenism","Mannerism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Renaissance treatises emphasised design as a distinct intellectual activity before construction.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":602,"stem":"Which persecution began with Kristallnacht?","choices":["Persecution of Christians in ancient Rome","Nazi persecution of Jews","Persecution of Christians in the People's Republic of China","Persecution of indigenous Brazilians in the Amazon","Persecution of Huguenots in the sixteenth century"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Kristallnacht was an anti-Jewish Nazi pogrom in November 1938; Nazi persecution had already begun earlier, so “began” in the source is historically imprecise.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":603,"stem":"What style is the Arco della Pace in Milan?","choices":["Neoclassical","Baroque","Corinthian","Hellenistic","Rationalist"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The nineteenth-century triumphal arch uses Neoclassical language.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":604,"stem":"Which saint is known as the “Angelic Doctor”?","choices":["Thomas Aquinas","Augustine","Januarius","Ignatius of Loyola","Francis of Assisi"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Thomas Aquinas is traditionally called Doctor Angelicus.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":605,"stem":"According to the source, what goal did Gothic architecture add to constructive techniques inherited from the Romanesque?","choices":["To amaze and display technical achievement","To satisfy the great families' defensive needs","To quote Romanesque architecture","To plan an ideal new city","To combine classical materials with cast iron and plaster"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The source emphasises the expressive and symbolic effect of Gothic structural ambition, including lofty vaults.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":606,"stem":"What style is Milan's Basilica of Sant'Ambrogio primarily known for?","choices":["Baroque","Gothic","Renaissance","Romanesque","Neoclassical"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The basilica is a major monument of Lombard Romanesque architecture.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":607,"stem":"Arrange these events in chronological order.\na. European sailors first round Cape Horn\nb. Vasco da Gama reaches India via the Cape of Good Hope\nc. First European circumnavigation of the globe\nd. James Cook reaches Australia\ne. Bering sails the strait later named after him","choices":["c; b; a; d; e","b; e; c; a; d","c; e; a; b; d","b; c; a; e; d","a; c; b; d; e"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The sequence is da Gama (1498), circumnavigation (1519–22), Cape Horn (1616), Bering (1728) and Cook (1770).","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":608,"stem":"Where was Giuseppe Mazzini born?","choices":["Genoa","Venice","Milan","Nice","Foggia"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Mazzini was born in Genoa in 1805.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":609,"stem":"Which is a traditional motto of the United States?","choices":["Plus ultra","Dieu et mon droit","Unity in diversity","E pluribus unum","Liberty or death"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"E pluribus unum, “out of many, one,” appears on the Great Seal of the United States.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":610,"stem":"According to the source, what is architecture's primary function?","choices":["Protection from weather","Distribution of forces","Expression","Symbolism","Display"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Providing shelter is a fundamental function of architecture, though architecture serves other social and cultural functions too.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":611,"stem":"How long is the term of the President of the Italian Republic?","choices":["Three years","Four years","Five years","Seven years","Six years"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The Italian presidential term lasts seven years.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":612,"stem":"Which listed city contains examples of Lombard architecture in the historical sense intended by the source?","choices":["Parma","Venice","Milan","Como","Benevento"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Benevento was a major centre of the Lombard Duchy and preserves Lombard-period architecture. “Lombard architecture” also sometimes describes buildings in northern Italy, so the phrasing is ambiguous.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":613,"stem":"In a Roman house, what was a lararium?","choices":["A labyrinth","A water basin","A household shrine","The atrium","A portico"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"A lararium held images of the household gods.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":614,"stem":"Which group is the odd one out?","choices":["Balearic Islands","Canary Islands","Cyclades","Aegean Islands","Aeolian Islands"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Canaries are in the Atlantic, whereas the others are Mediterranean island groups.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":615,"stem":"Which is the opposite of the Italian adjective deferente?","choices":["Insufficiente","Insolente","Rilassante","Ignorante","Sospettoso"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Deferente means respectful; insolente means insolent.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":616,"stem":"Which Italian author wrote Marcovaldo?","choices":["Alberto Moravia","Dino Buzzati","Carlo Collodi","Elsa Morante","Italo Calvino"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Italo Calvino wrote the linked stories about Marcovaldo.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":617,"stem":"Near which city is CERN?","choices":["Strasbourg","Zurich","Basel","Geneva","Bern"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"CERN is near Geneva, at the French–Swiss border.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":618,"stem":"In which century was Timur, also called Tamerlane, born?","choices":["Tenth","Eleventh","Twelfth","Fourteenth","Seventeenth"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Timur was born in 1336.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":619,"stem":"Who was the last shah of Iran?","choices":["Mohammad Mosaddegh","Ruhollah Khomeini","Farouk I","Reza Pahlavi","Ben Ali"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Mohammad Reza Pahlavi was deposed in the 1979 Iranian Revolution; the source calls him Reza Pahlavi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":620,"stem":"How is Dante's Divine Comedy divided?","choices":["99 chapters","12 books","34 songs","Three cantiche","24 cantos"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Its three cantiche are Inferno, Purgatorio and Paradiso.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":621,"stem":"Which historical period is traditionally called the Hellenistic period?","choices":["From the Persian Wars to the third century BCE","Greek culture from the late Neolithic to the Mycenaean age","The period of the first Olympic Games","The period of Greek political control over the whole Mediterranean","From Alexander the Great's death to Roman rule over Egypt"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The conventional bounds are 323 BCE to 30 BCE.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":622,"stem":"Which term is NOT connected to a classical Greek city in the source's terminology?","choices":["Agora","Forum","Stoa","Asty","Acropolis"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Forum denotes a Roman civic square; agora is its rough Greek counterpart.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":623,"stem":"In architecture, what is a tiburio?","choices":["The circular support on which a dome rests","A window admitting light to a church nave","A conical roof in Alpine bell towers","A roofed architectural enclosure surrounding a dome","The bishop's chair next to an ambo"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"A tiburio encloses a dome externally, often with a roof above it.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":624,"stem":"Who were the Pre-Raphaelites?","choices":["Painters active before Raphael","A nineteenth-century artistic movement","Painters trained in Pinturicchio's Urbino workshop","An early eighteenth-century movement","Court painters in late fifteenth-century Milan and Urbino"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood formed in Britain in 1848.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":625,"stem":"Which Roman figure changed the javelin, was consul seven times and defeated two Germanic peoples?","choices":["Julius Caesar","Scipio Africanus","Metellus Pius","Gaius Marius","Romulus"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Marius reformed the army and defeated the Cimbri and Teutones.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":626,"stem":"Which movement is NOT usually classified as a twentieth-century avant-garde?","choices":["Gothic Revival","Futurism","Expressionism","De Stijl","Constructivism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Gothic Revival is a historicist nineteenth-century movement.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":627,"stem":"Who designed Sant'Ivo alla Sapienza in Rome, shown here?","choices":["Guarino Guarini","Filippo Juvarra","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Francesco Borromini","Pietro da Cortona"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Borromini designed the church and its distinctive spiral lantern.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":628,"stem":"In which century did Filippo Brunelleschi die?","choices":["Fifteenth","Fourteenth","Thirteenth","Sixteenth","Twelfth"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"He died in 1446, in the fifteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":629,"stem":"Which institution most strongly drove the revival of Italian Renaissance architecture according to the source?","choices":["Nobility","Monarchies","Cities","Clergy","Military"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Wealthy Italian city-states and their civic patrons commissioned major projects.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":630,"stem":"Which Verdi opera contains the chorus “Va, pensiero”?","choices":["Aida","La traviata","Nabucco","Il trovatore","Don Giovanni"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The chorus is from Verdi's Nabucco.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":631,"stem":"In which century did Pieter Bruegel the Elder live?","choices":["Sixteenth","Seventeenth","Eighteenth","Fifteenth","Fourteenth"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Bruegel lived around 1525–1569.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":632,"stem":"Which architectural style is associated here with a tendency toward anti-classicism?","choices":["Romanesque","Gothic","Mannerism","Neoclassicism","Lombard"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Mannerist architects deliberately disturbed classical balance and convention. The printed key E is questionable.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":633,"stem":"Put these Italian events in chronological order.\n1. Garibaldi's victory over the Hunters of the Alps and Austrian departure from Milan\n2. Capture of Rome through Porta Pia\n3. Capital moved to Florence\n4. Annexation of Trentino–Alto Adige\n5. Expedition of the Thousand","choices":["1; 5; 3; 2; 4","5; 3; 1; 2; 4","1; 5; 2; 3; 4","5; 1; 3; 4; 2","5; 1; 2; 3; 4"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Events 1 and 5 occurred in 1859 and 1860 respectively, followed by Florence as capital (1865), Rome (1870) and Trentino (after World War I). The printed key A follows this order.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":634,"stem":"What was a Roman architectural menianum?","choices":["A portico","An atrium","A corridor","A balcony or raised terrace","A basement room"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Maenianum originally denoted a balcony or projecting gallery.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":635,"stem":"Who first completed an orbit of Earth in 1961?","choices":["Neil Armstrong","Buzz Aldrin","Valentina Tereshkova","John Glenn","Yuri Gagarin"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Gagarin orbited Earth aboard Vostok 1 in April 1961.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":636,"stem":"From which city did the first Orient Express service depart in 1883?","choices":["London","Geneva","Vienna","Paris","Venice"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The original service departed from Paris toward Constantinople.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":637,"stem":"Who is represented in the painting shown?","choices":["Jean-Jacques Rousseau","Cesare Beccaria","Voltaire","Jean-Paul Marat","Maximilien Robespierre"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"David's Death of Marat depicts Jean-Paul Marat murdered in his bath.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":638,"stem":"Which was NOT an Art Nouveau artist?","choices":["Victor Horta","Gustav Klimt","Henry van de Velde","Paul Cézanne","Otto Wagner"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Cézanne was a Post-Impressionist painter; the others are linked to Art Nouveau or the Vienna Secession.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":639,"stem":"Who sculpted The Veiled Christ, shown here?","choices":["Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Giovanni da Nola","Giuseppe Sanmartino","Michelangelo","Antonio Canova"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Sanmartino sculpted the extraordinary marble veil in Naples.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":640,"stem":"Which sequence orders these philosophers from earliest to latest?","choices":["Epicurus; Pythagoras; Aristotle; Plato; Socrates","Epicurus; Plato; Aristotle; Socrates; Pythagoras","Pythagoras; Socrates; Plato; Aristotle; Epicurus","Pythagoras; Epicurus; Socrates; Plato; Aristotle","Aristotle; Socrates; Pythagoras; Epicurus; Plato"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Pythagoras precedes Socrates, Plato, Aristotle and Epicurus.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":641,"stem":"With which movement is the pictured house associated?","choices":["De Stijl","Cubism","Dadaism","Pop Art","Surrealism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Rietveld Schröder House embodies De Stijl's orthogonal planes.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":642,"stem":"Which statement about the French Revolution is incorrect?","choices":["Louis XVI was beheaded in 1790","The Bastille was stormed on 14 July 1789","Liberty, equality, fraternity became a revolutionary motto","Robespierre and the Jacobins dominated the Terror","Napoleon overthrew the Directory in 1799"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Louis XVI was executed in January 1793, not 1790.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":643,"stem":"Which event does the painting depict?","choices":["Civilians killed by Napoleon's troops in Russia in 1812","The 1799 Neapolitan revolution","The 1770 Boston Massacre","Civilians shot after the May 1808 uprising in Madrid","A French scene from the 1793 Terror"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Goya's Third of May 1808 shows French soldiers executing Spanish civilians.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":644,"stem":"Match the numbered design lamps with their names and designers.\na. Tolomeo – Michele De Lucchi; b. Eclisse – Vico Magistretti; c. Tizio – Richard Sapper; d. Cobra – Elio Martinelli; e. Arco – Achille and Pier Giacomo Castiglioni","choices":["1–a; 2–d; 3–b; 4–c; 5–e","1–e; 2–a; 3–c; 4–b; 5–d","1–e; 2–a; 3–b; 4–c; 5–d","1–d; 2–e; 3–c; 4–a; 5–b","1–d; 2–c; 3–a; 4–b; 5–e"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The tall curved Arco is 1; adjustable Tolomeo is 2; Tizio is 3; hemispherical Eclisse is 4; Cobra is 5.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B006-Q644","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3492.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[[159,169,291,319],[155,326,284,479],[155,485,289,612],[155,625,309,729],[155,739,306,848]],"method":"clean_labelled_visual_tiles","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3492.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"B","contentFingerprint":"f685e23f6a0025f99c5cc5874eb33106b646f45b1683ea00c07f3c166fba73bf"},{"qid":645,"stem":"What do the Lateran Treaties regulate?","choices":["Relations between the Italian state and Catholic Church, with bilateral changes not requiring constitutional amendment","Religious practice in Italy without constitutional significance","Relations between Italy and all denominations, with constitutional relevance","Relations between Italy and all religions without constitutional review","The Catholic Church's creed"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The treaties of 1929 established the legal relationship between Italy and the Holy See.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":646,"stem":"Which school, founded in 1919, sought to integrate art, crafts and technology?","choices":["Chicago School","De Stijl","Nancy School","Wiener Werkstätte","Bauhaus"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Gropius founded the Bauhaus in Weimar in 1919.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":647,"stem":"Who said “Veni, vidi, vici”?","choices":["Jesus","Cicero","Julius Caesar","Seneca","Asterix"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Caesar reported his rapid victory at Zela in this phrase.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":648,"stem":"Which was NOT a twentieth-century artistic avant-garde movement according to the source?","choices":["Rationalism","Neoplasticism","Expressionism","Futurism","Constructivism"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The source treats Rationalism as an architectural tendency, while the other four are listed artistic avant-gardes; the categories overlap.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":649,"stem":"Match each avant-garde movement to its country of origin.\n1. Cubism; 2. Futurism; 3. Constructivism; 4. Neoplasticism; 5. Expressionism\na. Russia; b. France; c. Germany; d. Italy; e. Netherlands","choices":["1–b; 2–e; 3–d; 4–a; 5–c","1–a; 2–d; 3–e; 4–b; 5–c","1–d; 2–e; 3–a; 4–c; 5–b","1–b; 2–d; 3–a; 4–e; 5–c","1–a; 2–c; 3–d; 4–e; 5–b"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Cubism emerged in France, Futurism in Italy, Constructivism in Russia, De Stijl in the Netherlands and Expressionism in Germany.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":650,"stem":"Which is NOT usually a principle of the sharing economy according to this source?","choices":["Access can be preferred to ownership","Transparency and open data encourage innovation","Strangers can overcome mutual distrust","Competition is dangerous","Unused resources are wasted value"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The source presents sharing as cooperation, while “competition is dangerous” is too categorical to be a defining principle.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":651,"stem":"When was the Futurist Manifesto published, and who wrote it?","choices":["1953, Soffici","1940, Boccioni","1909, Marinetti","1890, Carrà","1924, Mayakovsky"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Marinetti published the manifesto in 1909.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":652,"stem":"The Pergamon Altar reconstructed in Berlin belongs to which period?","choices":["Roman","Hellenistic","Mesopotamian","Egyptian","Etruscan"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Pergamon Altar is a major monument of Hellenistic Greek art.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":653,"stem":"What has the Hellenistic Winged Victory of Samothrace lost?","choices":["Wings and arms","Only its wings","Hands","Legs","Arms and head"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The statue retains its wings but lacks its head and arms.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":654,"stem":"Which group correctly matches all buildings with their cities?\nAra Pacis Museum; Arab World Institute; MART; Guggenheim Museum; Opera House","choices":["Rovereto; Rome; Sydney; Bilbao; Paris","Rome; Bilbao; Sydney; Paris; Rovereto","Rome; Paris; Bilbao; Sydney; Rovereto","Paris; Rome; Rovereto; Bilbao; Sydney","Rome; Paris; Rovereto; Bilbao; Sydney"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The five sites are respectively Rome, Paris, Rovereto, Bilbao and Sydney.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":655,"stem":"Match each numbered work with its designer.\n1. Terme Aramami; 2. Plaza of the Three Powers, Brasília; 3. Ara Pacis Museum, Rome; 4. Auditorium Parco della Musica, Rome; 5. Guggenheim Museum, Bilbao","choices":["Tadao Ando; Oscar Niemeyer; Richard Meier; Renzo Piano; Frank Gehry","Oscar Niemeyer; Richard Meier; Renzo Piano; Tadao Ando; Frank Gehry","Richard Meier; Oscar Niemeyer; Renzo Piano; Frank Gehry; Tadao Ando","Oscar Niemeyer; Frank Gehry; Richard Meier; Tadao Ando; Renzo Piano","Tadao Ando; Richard Meier; Oscar Niemeyer; Frank Gehry; Renzo Piano"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The sequence matches Ando's baths, Niemeyer's Brasília buildings, Meier's Ara Pacis, Piano's auditorium and Gehry's Bilbao museum.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":656,"stem":"To what period does the illustrated site plan belong?","choices":["Roman","Baroque","Romanesque","Renaissance","Postmodern"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"The geometric complex of Palladian buildings and courtyards has a Renaissance planning language.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":657,"stem":"Which is NOT a characteristic of Frank Lloyd Wright's Prairie House?","choices":["Recessed façade and discreet entrance","Central hearth symbolising family life","Flat roof used as a garden","Continuity between indoors and nature","Extended low horizontal lines"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Prairie Houses commonly use low-pitched roofs and strong horizontals rather than roof gardens.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":658,"stem":"Which technique produced the famous work shown?","choices":["Oil on canvas","Acrylic on canvas","Lithography","Screen printing","Stencil"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Warhol's repeated Marilyn images use silkscreen printing.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":659,"stem":"Why did Cavour seek Austrian aggression before the Second Italian War of Independence?","choices":["To avoid blame before the international community","To avoid moral blame before God","Because Piedmont's army was not ready","Because Piedmont rejected defensive war","Because French military support depended on Austria being the aggressor"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"France's commitment to support Piedmont depended on Austria appearing to initiate the war.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":660,"stem":"Which statement about Pop Art is incorrect according to the source?","choices":["Its name abbreviates Popular Art","It developed from the 1950s","It uses mass-media images and consumer products","Warhol and Lichtenstein are leading artists","Its essential goal is an explicit ideological denunciation"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Pop Art appropriates mass culture but cannot be defined simply by ideological denunciation.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":661,"stem":"When did cast iron and iron become major building materials with structural engineering?","choices":["Second half of the nineteenth century","Late eighteenth century","First half of the eighteenth century","Never, because they are too heavy","Between the two world wars"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Iron construction expanded strongly in the industrial nineteenth century; experimentation began earlier.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":662,"stem":"Which movement does the pictured painting exemplify?","choices":["Fauvism","Surrealism","Art Nouveau","Dadaism","Symbolism"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Klimt's ornamental, curvilinear Tree of Life is associated with the Vienna Secession and Art Nouveau.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":663,"stem":"Which painting movement sought to depict appearances as seen at a particular instant under particular lighting?","choices":["Neoclassicism","Impressionism","Romanticism","Cubism","Expressionism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Impressionists studied changing light and immediate visual impressions.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":664,"stem":"What is Leonardo da Vinci's sfumato?","choices":["Overlaid transparent watercolour washes","Wet-on-wet colour diffusion","Subtle tonal gradations and soft chiaroscuro transitions","Small adjacent points of contrasting colours","None of these"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Sfumato softens outlines through gradual transitions of light and shadow.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":665,"stem":"When was Botticelli's Birth of Venus painted, approximately?","choices":["1482","1264","1330","1540","1412"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The painting dates to the late 1480s; the source offers 1482 as the nearest choice.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":666,"stem":"Which artist–work pairing is incorrect?","choices":["Raphael – Portrait of the Fornarina","Mantegna – Camera degli Sposi frescoes","Palladio – Villa Capra “La Rotonda”","Michelangelo – Piazza del Campidoglio design","Brunelleschi – Arch of the Argentarii in Florence"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The Arch of the Argentarii is an ancient Roman monument in Rome, unrelated to Brunelleschi.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":667,"stem":"Which order of historical figures, earliest to latest, is correct?","choices":["Muhammad; Constantine; Charlemagne; Dante; Piero della Francesca; Julius II; Canova; Vivaldi; Mazzini; Modigliani","Constantine; Charlemagne; Muhammad; Dante; Julius II; Piero; Canova; Vivaldi; Modigliani; Mazzini","Charlemagne; Constantine; Muhammad; Dante; Julius II; Piero; Canova; Vivaldi; Modigliani; Mazzini","Muhammad; Constantine; Charlemagne; Julius II; Dante; Piero; Canova; Vivaldi; Mazzini; Modigliani","Constantine; Muhammad; Charlemagne; Dante; Piero della Francesca; Julius II; Antonio Vivaldi; Antonio Canova; Giuseppe Mazzini; Amedeo Modigliani"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"The correct sequence begins in the fourth century with Constantine, then Muhammad, Charlemagne and Dante, and ends with Mazzini and Modigliani.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":668,"stem":"What was Jean de La Fontaine?","choices":["An architect","A poet and fabulist","A statistician","A philosopher","An economist"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"La Fontaine is famous for his fables.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":669,"stem":"What does the Italian adjective vernacolare mean?","choices":["Seasonal","Particular","Vernacular or dialectal","Spring-like","Twilight-related"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Vernacolare describes local speech or vernacular tradition.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":670,"stem":"What is Saint Petersburg's Nevsky Prospekt?","choices":["A column","A palace","A triumphal arch","A street","A church"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Nevsky Prospekt is a principal street in Saint Petersburg.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":671,"stem":"Entasis in a Greek temple corrects the visual impression of which element?","choices":["Column shafts","Metopes and triglyphs","Pediment cornice","Vault","Pavement"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Entasis is a slight swelling of the column shaft.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":672,"stem":"What is the plan shape of San Vitale in Ravenna?","choices":["Greek cross","Circular","Octagonal","Square","Hexagonal"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The church is organised around an octagonal central plan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":673,"stem":"How is pictorial space organised in Piero della Francesca's Flagellation of Christ?","choices":["One ancient-style interior scene","One exterior scene with modern buildings","Two distinct architectural scenes, interior and exterior, separated in space and time","Two overlapping architectural scenes, simultaneous but spatially apart","None of these"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The scene at left places Christ inside; the figures at right stand in a distinct outdoor space.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":674,"stem":"Which famous painter holds the parasol in this Robert Capa photograph?","choices":["Marc Chagall","Henri Matisse","Marcel Duchamp","Salvador Dalí","Pablo Picasso"],"correctIndex":4,"officialKey":"E","explanation":"Capa photographed Picasso with Françoise Gilot at the beach.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":675,"stem":"Which architect wrote the quoted recollection of Michelangelo's reception during his changes to St Peter's Basilica?","choices":["Bernini","Borromini","Michelangelo","Pietro da Cortona","Palladio"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The source attributes the passage to Borromini; it reflects on Michelangelo's experimental design and criticism.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":676,"stem":"The Vienna Secession was an Austrian art movement related to:","choices":["Baroque","Art Nouveau","Rationalist architecture","Romanticism","Surrealism"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The Secession developed a Viennese form of Art Nouveau.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":677,"stem":"Where is Torre Velasca?","choices":["Turin","Verona","Florence","Milan","Rovereto"],"correctIndex":3,"officialKey":"D","explanation":"Torre Velasca is a Milan landmark.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":678,"stem":"Who redesigned Piazza del Popolo in Rome?","choices":["Giovanni Antolini","Giuseppe Piermarini","Giuseppe Valadier","Gian Lorenzo Bernini","Pietro da Cortona"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Valadier reshaped the piazza in the early nineteenth century.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":679,"stem":"How did Carlo Maderno enlarge St Peter's Basilica?","choices":["Convert the central nave","Attach a portico","Add a longitudinal body, changing the Greek-cross plan into a Latin-cross plan","Restore interior frescoes","Add an apse"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Maderno extended the nave toward the entrance, producing a Latin-cross plan.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":680,"stem":"Match the designers to their works.\na. Vignola; b. Gio Ponti; c. Bernini; d. Biagio Rossetti; e. Piermarini\n1. St Peter's colonnade; 2. Royal Palace, Milan; 3. Palazzo dei Diamanti, Ferrara; 4. Pirelli Tower, Milan; 5. Church of the Gesù, Rome","choices":["b–1; c–4; a–3; e–5; d–2","a–5; b–1; c–4; d–3; e–2","b–4; e–2; a–5; c–1; d–3","e–2; d–1; c–3; a–2; b–5","a–5; b–2; d–4; c–3; e–1"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Ponti is associated with the Pirelli Tower, Piermarini with the royal palace, Vignola with the Gesù, Bernini with the colonnade and Rossetti with Palazzo dei Diamanti.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":681,"stem":"Match objects and their designers or manufacturers.\nA. Millennium Dome; B. Fiat Panda; C. Superleggera chair; D. Tolomeo lamp\n1. Giorgetto Giugiaro; 2. Cassina; 3. Michele De Lucchi; 4. Richard Rogers","choices":["A–4; B–1; C–2; D–3","A–1; B–2; C–4; D–3","A–4; B–2; C–3; D–1","A–2; B–3; C–4; D–1","A–2; B–1; C–4; D–3"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Rogers designed the dome, Giugiaro the Panda, Cassina produced Ponti's Superleggera, and De Lucchi co-designed the Tolomeo lamp.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":682,"stem":"Who is the artist working in the photograph?","choices":["Jackson Pollock","Rembrandt","Lucio Fontana","Roy Lichtenstein","Renzo Piano"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The photograph shows Fontana cutting the surface of a canvas.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":683,"stem":"What was the “Sudeten question”?","choices":["The issue of German minorities in a mountainous region of Czechoslovakia between the world wars","A mountain territory between Romania and Hungary","German territory assigned to Poland after World War I","Alto Adige annexed to Italy after World War I","The militarisation of the Ruhr by France"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The Sudetenland was a region of Czechoslovakia with many ethnic German residents.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":684,"stem":"What was Chartism?","choices":["An Irish literary movement","A British reform movement led largely by workers in the early nineteenth century","An Irish armed anti-Masonic group","A Spanish literary movement","A German rural cooperative movement"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"The People's Charter demanded political reforms including a broader franchise.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":685,"stem":"What happened to Maximilian of Habsburg in 1867?","choices":["He was shot in England","He married Catherine of Russia","He was executed in Mexico","He abdicated and repaired to the United States","He became chancellor of the Austrian Reich"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"Maximilian, installed as emperor of Mexico, was executed at Querétaro.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":686,"stem":"How do the window openings of Venice's Doge's Palace facing St Mark's Square vary by storey?","choices":["They differ on every floor","They match on the first and second floors","They match on ground and first floors","They are identical on every floor","They match from the second floor upward"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"Arcades, loggias and upper windows have distinct arrangements.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":687,"stem":"From which cultural tradition does Moorish style derive?","choices":["Spanish","Arab","Turkish","English","Italian"],"correctIndex":1,"officialKey":"B","explanation":"Moorish architecture is associated with Islamic traditions in North Africa and Iberia.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":688,"stem":"Which four were the historic Italian maritime republics?","choices":["Amalfi, Genoa, Pisa, Venice","Ancona, Amalfi, Genoa, Venice","Livorno, Pisa, Genoa, Venice","Naples, Ostia, Pisa, Genoa","Bari, Genoa, Livorno, Venice"],"correctIndex":0,"officialKey":"A","explanation":"The conventional four are Amalfi, Genoa, Pisa and Venice.","figure":false,"sourceReview":"page_transcribed_key_pending_audit","language":"en","version":"source-en-v31"},{"qid":689,"stem":"Which labelled part of the architectural diagram is the metope?","choices":["A","C","B","D","E"],"correctIndex":2,"officialKey":"C","explanation":"The metope is the decorated panel between triglyphs in the Doric frieze, labelled B in the diagram; the third option (“B”) is correct.","figure":true,"sourceReview":"figure_repaired_and_source_checked","language":"en","version":"source-en-v42","id":"ALPHA-B008-Q689","examLanguages":["en","it"],"visualMetadata":{"sourceImage":"IMG_3498.jpeg","sourceBoxes":[],"method":"clean_redraw_from_source_diagram","essential":true},"sourceImage":"IMG_3498.jpeg","reviewStatus":"ready","answerBasis":"Photographed source page and printed key; diagram labels and option order checked.","scoringKey":"C","contentFingerprint":"abd0a542e139c172b5f375217a7dd035b8f1e4a3b27186808d25ea417fd1459b"},{"qid":690,"stem":"According to the source, what effect did Mussolini's delay in joining Germany's war have?","choices":["Germany abandoned its attack on Poland","Poland mounted an unexpected defence","Germany lost Soviet support","Hitler postponed the start of the invasion of Poland by six days","Greece deployed troops at the border"],"correc